      ],
      "lineAnnotations": {
        "0": {"synopsis":"Cold start: execution begins here at power-on and reset.\nSwitches off the NMI generator so no display NMIs arrive during set-up.\nLoads BC with $7FFF, the top address for the RAM fill and check at $03CB,\nand jumps there.\nDoes not return.\nChanges: BC.","comment":"Any write to port $FD stops the NMI generator; the value in A is irrelevant"},
        "2": {"comment":"BC = $7FFF, the highest address the RAM fill/check starts from"},
        "5": {"comment":"Go on at $03CB: fills $7FFF down to $4000 with $02, then checks the RAM"},
        "8": {"synopsis":"RST $08: stops with an error. The byte after the RST holds the error code (the report\nminus one); X_PTR = CH_ADD marks where it happened. Goes on at ERROR_L."},
        "16": {"synopsis":"RST $10: prints the character in A (PRINT_CHAR). Anything but a space also clears\nFLAGS bit 0 on the way ($07F1)."},
        "24": {"synopsis":"RST $18: A = the character at CH_ADD, moving past spaces (code 0) and the cursor\n(NEXT_CHAR)."},
        "28": {"synopsis":"GET_CHAR's test, also reached from RST $20: returns unless A is a space (code 0); a space\nfalls on into NEXT_SKIP, which moves past it."},
        "32": {"synopsis":"RST $20: CH_ADD + 1 (NEXT_CHAR), then past spaces as GET_CHAR does; A = the character."},
        "40": {"synopsis":"RST $28: runs the calculator (CALCULATE) on the operation codes that follow the RST,\nup to end-calc."},
        "43": {"synopsis":"end-calc ($34): ends the calculator's run. The code pointer becomes the return address,\nso the Z80 goes on after the end-calc byte."},
        "48": {"synopsis":"RST $30: makes BC bytes of room at the end of the workspace, just below the calculator\nstack, with E_LINE kept as it was (WORK_ROOM_END).\nExit: DE = the room's first byte (where STKBOT was), HL = its last byte, BC = the count."},
        "56": {"synopsis":"INT (interrupt mode 1), raised by the ULA while the display file runs, at the end of each\nline: R's bit 6 low during a refresh. The interrupted code's address is on the stack.\nC counts the 8 scan lines of a character row: while lines are left, the same row is run\nagain (HL still at its start); after the 8th, the return address (just past the row's\nNEWLINE HALT) is the next row, B counts the rows, and the last one returns from\nDISPLAY_LINES.","comment":"One scan line fewer in this row"},
        "60": {"comment":"Row done: HL = the next row, past the NEWLINE"},
        "61": {"comment":"The last row: back from DISPLAY_LINES"},
        "63": {"comment":"C = 8 scan lines for the next row"},
        "65": {"synopsis":"Sets R for the next line, enables interrupts and runs the row of the display file at HL\n(again, for each of its 8 lines).","comment":"R for the line, interrupts on ..."},
        "68": {"comment":"... and run the row (again)"},
        "69": {"synopsis":"Lines left in the row (reached by JP NZ, so the RET Z does not return here): the\ninterrupted address is dropped and the same row runs again (INT_NEXT_LINE).","comment":"Lines left: drop the return address, run the same row"},
        "73": {"synopsis":"CH_ADD + 1, and once more past the cursor ($7F); A = the character there."},
        "76": {"synopsis":"HL + 1, then SET_CH_ADD: where SET_CH_ADD loops to step past the cursor."},
        "77": {"synopsis":"CH_ADD = HL, moved past the cursor when HL is on it; A = the character."},
        "86": {"synopsis":"RST $08's tail: HL = its return address, the byte after the RST; L = that byte, the error\ncode; on into ERROR_L."},
        "88": {"synopsis":"Stops with error code L: ERR_NR = L, the stack back to ERR_SP, the display mode put right\n(SLOW_FAST); then $14BC empties the calculator stack and returns to the address in the\nslot ERR_SP points at. During the syntax check that is KEY_NEWLINE's return (the editor);\nwhile running, the return of the CALL SCAN_LINE at $0673 (the run loop, which reports).\nGOSUB and RETURN move ERR_SP with the GOSUB stack.\nEntered from ERROR_RST and from TEST_ROOM."},
        "102": {"synopsis":"The NMI handler: runs at every NMI the ULA raises while the NMI generator is on.\nCounts A' up by one. While the count has not reached 0 it returns at once (NMI_RETURN),\nso A' counts NMIs for whoever set it.\nWhen the count reaches 0, goes on at NMI_COUNT_END.","comment":"Switch to A', the NMI counter"},
        "103": {"comment":"Count this NMI"},
        "104": {"comment":"Still negative: not done, return"},
        "107": {"comment":"Reached 0: the counted NMIs are over"},
        "109": {"synopsis":"Back to the interrupted code, with A' holding the new count."},
        "111": {"synopsis":"The NMI count reached 0: a border is over. Saves AF, BC, DE, HL (the program's: the frame\nrestores them at the next border), takes D_FILE + $8000 into HL, HALTs for the next HSYNC so\nthe picture starts on time, switches the NMI generator off, and goes on with the frame at\nIX (BORDER_LINES set it)."},
        "116": {"comment":"HL = D_FILE"},
        "119": {"comment":"HL = D_FILE + $8000"},
        "121": {"comment":"Wait for the next interrupt"},
        "122": {"comment":"NMI generator off"},
        "124": {"comment":"On to the display stage IX holds"},
        "126": {"synopsis":"Key table, unshifted: the code each key gives, in the order KEY_DECODE numbers the keys:\nZ X C V, A S D F G, Q W E R T, 1 2 3 4 5, 0 9 8 7 6, P O I U Y, NEWLINE L K J H, SPACE . M N B."},
        "165": {"synopsis":"Key table, shifted: the code each key gives with SHIFT, in the same order. Keyword\ntokens ($C0 and up) and editing keys ($70-$79) are among them."},
        "204": {"synopsis":"Function mode (F): the token each key gives, in the same order; a shifted key uses its\nunshifted key's entry (FUNCTION_KEY). $78 (K/L) where a key has no function."},
        "243": {"synopsis":"Graphics mode (G): the graphic each shifted key gives, from the 44th key position (shifted A)\nto the 73rd (shifted H) (GRAPHICS_KEY). $77 and $78 for keys without a graphic."},
        "273": {"synopsis":"Keyword table: the spelling of each keyword, its last character with bit 7 set. The first\nentry is a dummy, then token $C0 (\"\"), AT, TAB, ?, CODE, VAL, ... and last RND, INKEY$ and PI,\nwhich are codes $40-$42 (FIND_TOKEN)."},
        "508": {"synopsis":"HL + 1; returns while it is below E_LINE. At E_LINE it drops its return and falls into\nSLOW_FAST: how SAVE's and LOAD's byte loops end, returning from the command with SLOW mode\nstarted again if CDFLAG asks for it. LOAD's E_LINE is the one the load itself has stored.\nExit: HL = the next address.\nChanges: AF, DE, HL.","comment":"The next address"},
        "510": {"comment":"Below E_LINE?"},
        "517": {"comment":"Yes: go on"},
        "518": {"comment":"At E_LINE: drop the return, end in SLOW_FAST"},
        "519": {"synopsis":"Starts SLOW mode when CDFLAG asks for it and the hardware can do it.\nReturns at once when bits 6 and 7 of CDFLAG agree.\nOtherwise it tests whether display NMIs arrive: A' = $7F, the NMI generator on for a\nshort wait, then off. NMI counts A' up, so $80 afterwards means at least one NMI came:\nbit 7 is set and the display starts (DISPLAY_FRAME); in that case it returns with the\npicture being produced by interrupts. With no NMI, bit 6 is cleared (STAY_FAST).\nChanges: AF, AF', B, HL.","comment":"HL = CDFLAG"},
        "525": {"comment":"Carry = bit 6 XOR bit 7 of CDFLAG"},
        "526": {"comment":"They agree: nothing to do"},
        "527": {"comment":"$7F for A': NMI adds one for each NMI"},
        "530": {"comment":"B = 17: the length of the wait"},
        "532": {"comment":"NMI generator on (any write to port $FE)"},
        "534": {"synopsis":"SLOW_FAST's wait with the NMI generator on; then off again, and A's count shows whether an\nNMI came.","comment":"Wait while NMIs may arrive"},
        "536": {"comment":"NMI generator off again"},
        "538": {"comment":"A = the count, flags as they were before the test"},
        "539": {"comment":"Carry = bit 7 of the count: set if $7F became $80"},
        "540": {"comment":"No NMI arrived: stay in FAST mode"},
        "542": {"comment":"CDFLAG bit 7: SLOW mode is running"},
        "544": {"comment":"Saved for the display code, which pops them on its way back"},
        "548": {"comment":"Start producing the picture"},
        "550": {"synopsis":"No display NMIs arrived: clears bit 6 of CDFLAG, so SLOW mode is no longer\nasked for and the machine stays in FAST mode.","comment":"SLOW mode no longer asked for"},
        "553": {"synopsis":"One frame of the picture, the TV's vertical sync (VSYNC) included; jp $0229 at its end runs\nthe next. Klive's ULA model gives the hardware: an opcode fetched above 32K whose byte has\nbit 6 clear is latched as a character and executed as a NOP, and drawn at the next refresh\nfrom (I & $FE) + code x 8 + the line counter; INT comes when A6 (R's bit 6) is low during a\nrefresh; NMI at each HSYNC while the NMI generator is on; an IN with A0 low starts VSYNC and\nan OUT ends it and resets the line counter.\n1. FRAMES counts down (FRAMES_COUNT). Its bit 15 is set in normal running; PAUSE clears it\n   and enters at $022D with its count, and when the low 15 bits run out the frame returns\n   at once (RET NC): the PAUSE is over. A run read FRAMES as $FF7C idle, $00B5 during\n   PAUSE 200, $FEA2 after it.\n2. VSYNC: the keyboard is read (KEYBOARD's IN starts VSYNC) into LAST_K, a steady reading\n   sets CDFLAG bit 0, DB_ST counts the debounce, a delay loop gives VSYNC its length, and\n   OUT ($FF) ends it.\n3. The top border (BORDER_LINES), the 24 text lines (DISPLAY_LINES, running the display file\n   at D_FILE + $8000), the bottom border, then the next frame.\nIn SLOW mode the borders give the CPU back to the program: NMIs count the blank lines and\nNMI_COUNT_END resumes here. In FAST mode the frame runs only while WAIT_KEY or PAUSE call it,\nand returns when a key is read.","comment":"HL = FRAMES - 1"},
        "557": {"synopsis":"FRAMES's count, also PAUSE's entry with its count in HL: when the low 15 bits have run out,\nbit 15 goes into carry and is cleared.","comment":"PAUSE enters here with its count: its low 15 bits run out?"},
        "564": {"comment":"Yes: bit 15 into carry, and cleared"},
        "567": {"synopsis":"The count has not run out: a memory read that keeps the timing equal, and carry set.","comment":"No: a read for the timing; carry set"},
        "569": {"synopsis":"FRAMES = HL. With carry clear (a PAUSE ran out) it returns at once; otherwise the keyboard\nis read (VSYNC starts) and LAST_K and the debounce are updated."},
        "570": {"comment":"FRAMES = HL"},
        "573": {"comment":"Bit 15 was clear (a PAUSE ran out): return"},
        "574": {"comment":"Read the keyboard: its IN starts VSYNC"},
        "577": {"comment":"BC = the last reading, LAST_K = this one"},
        "587": {"comment":"The same as before?"},
        "589": {"comment":"And DB_ST 0?"},
        "600": {"comment":"CDFLAG bit 0 clear: no new key yet"},
        "602": {"comment":"Changed, or still bouncing"},
        "604": {"comment":"Steady: FAST mode?"},
        "606": {"comment":"CDFLAG bit 0: a new, steady key reading"},
        "608": {"comment":"FAST mode: back to WAIT_KEY or PAUSE with it"},
        "609": {"comment":"So both paths take the same time"},
        "612": {"synopsis":"Both paths join here, B the delay count for VSYNC_WAIT; HL = DB_ST for the update after it."},
        "618": {"synopsis":"VSYNC's length (a DJNZ delay); then DB_ST is updated, OUT ($FF) ends VSYNC and the top\nborder starts.","comment":"A delay: VSYNC's length"},
        "621": {"comment":"A key down in the reading?"},
        "627": {"comment":"DB_ST: restarted while a key is down, halved each frame"},
        "631": {"comment":"Any OUT ends VSYNC and resets the line counter"},
        "633": {"comment":"HL = D_FILE + $8000: the display file above 32K"},
        "638": {"comment":"The top border"},
        "641": {"comment":"For the timing"},
        "643": {"comment":"B = 25 rows (the display file's first NEWLINE, then 24 lines), C = 1"},
        "648": {"comment":"Run the display file: the text lines"},
        "652": {"comment":"The bottom border"},
        "655": {"comment":"The next frame"},
        "658": {"synopsis":"A border of MARGIN blank lines. Called by DISPLAY_FRAME: the return address (where the frame\ngoes on) goes into IX.\nSLOW mode: A' = 1 - MARGIN, the NMI generator on, and the registers saved on entry to the\nframe restored: this RET returns to the program, which runs while the NMIs count the lines;\nNMI_COUNT_END comes back (JP (IX)) when the count reaches 0.\nFAST mode (FAST_BORDER): the lines are made with HALTs and INTs at the display file's start\n(DISPLAY_LINES with B = 1), then JP (IX).","comment":"IX = where the frame goes on"},
        "660": {"comment":"C = MARGIN"},
        "663": {"comment":"SLOW mode?"},
        "669": {"comment":"A' = 1 - MARGIN: NMI's count up to 0"},
        "674": {"comment":"NMI generator on"},
        "676": {"comment":"The program's registers back ..."},
        "680": {"comment":"... and back to it while the border goes by"},
        "681": {"synopsis":"FAST mode's border: C (MARGIN) blank lines made by running the display file's first NEWLINE\nwith interrupts, then on with the frame (JP (IX)).","comment":"FAST: R for a blank line, one row"},
        "685": {"comment":"C (MARGIN) blank lines at the display file's start"},
        "689": {"comment":"For the timing"},
        "691": {"comment":"On with the frame"},
        "693": {"synopsis":"Runs the display file from HL as Z80 code, with interrupts on: R = A, then A = $DD for the\nlines after. Each character executes as a NOP and is drawn; each line's NEWLINE ($76, bit 6\nset) is a real HALT, which waits for the INT that R's count brings at the line's end\n(INT_DISPLAY). B = the rows, C = the lines left of the first.","comment":"R: when the first INT comes"},
        "695": {"comment":"A = $DD: R for each line after"},
        "698": {"comment":"Execute the display file"},
        "699": {"synopsis":"Reads the keyboard: HL = the reading, in the form LAST_K holds and KEY_DECODE takes, 0 bits\nfor keys down. Each of the 8 rows (port $FE with B = $FE, $FD, ... $7F) gives 5 key bits:\nH = all rows' bits ANDed (a 0 for each column with a key down) shifted up one, with SHIFT\nin bit 0; L = a 0 for each row with a key down. SHIFT's own bit (row $FE, bit 0) is left\nout of the scan, so SHIFT alone leaves L = $FF.\nAlso sets MARGIN from bit 6 of the last read: 55 blank lines when set, 31 when clear (the US\nmachine's port reads 0 there, Klive's core README).\nObserved: none $FFFF, A $FDFD, SHIFT $FEFF, SHIFT+A $FCFD, Z $FBFE, Q+P $FDDB, A+Q $FDF9,\n1 $FDF7, SPACE $FD7F, NEWLINE $FDBF; MARGIN 55 on the 50 Hz model, 31 on the US one.\nThe first IN starts VSYNC (Klive's ULA model): DISPLAY_FRAME calls this at the start of its\nVSYNC; INKEY$ (OP_INKEY) calls it too.\nChanges: AF, BC, D, HL; MARGIN.","comment":"No keys yet"},
        "702": {"comment":"B = $FE: the first row; C = the port"},
        "707": {"comment":"First row only: SHIFT's bit left out"},
        "709": {"synopsis":"KEYBOARD's loop: one keyboard row into H (its columns) and L (its place), then the next.","comment":"Bits 5-7 are not keys"},
        "711": {"comment":"D = the row's key bits"},
        "713": {"comment":"A = $FF when no key in this row ..."},
        "716": {"comment":"... else B, a 0 at this row's place"},
        "717": {"comment":"L: the rows with a key down"},
        "720": {"comment":"H: the columns with a key down"},
        "722": {"comment":"The next row; no carry after the eighth"},
        "724": {"comment":"Read it (after the last row: row $FE again)"},
        "728": {"comment":"Carry = SHIFT"},
        "729": {"comment":"H = the columns, shifted up, SHIFT in bit 0"},
        "731": {"comment":"Carry = bit 6 of the read"},
        "734": {"comment":"MARGIN = 55 when bit 6 is set, 31 when clear"},
        "743": {"synopsis":"Stops SLOW mode for now: when CDFLAG bit 7 is set, waits for the next interrupt, switches\nthe NMI generator off and clears bit 7. Bit 6 still asks for SLOW, so SLOW_FAST starts it again."},
        "748": {"comment":"Wait for the next interrupt"},
        "749": {"comment":"NMI generator off"},
        "751": {"comment":"SLOW mode is not running"},
        "756": {"synopsis":"Report F: SAVE with an empty name (observed: SAVE \"\" gives F/0)."},
        "758": {"synopsis":"SAVE name (class 5). The name is a string expression, not empty (report F); TAPE_NAME also\nmarks its last character with bit 7 in place, so a SAVE in a program line changes the line\n(observed: 10 SAVE \"AB\" lists as SAVE \"A[B]\" after a run).\nIn FAST mode: about 5 s of silence ($12CB rounds of BREAK_TEST and a 256-step DJNZ, about\n3,450 T each, computed at 3.25 MHz), the name's bytes up to the one with bit 7, then memory\nfrom $4009 (VERSN) to E_LINE - 1: the system variables from VERSN on, the program, the\ndisplay file and the variables, a byte at a time (SAVE_BYTE). BREAK at any time is report D\n(observed: D/10). It ends through TAPE_NEXT_ADDR, returning from SAVE in SLOW_FAST.\nObserved: SAVE \"AB\" sent $26 $A7, then $00 $0A $00 $87 from $4009 on.","comment":"DE = the name; carry when it is empty"},
        "761": {"comment":"Empty: report F"},
        "763": {"comment":"HL = the name"},
        "764": {"comment":"$12CB rounds of silence"},
        "767": {"synopsis":"SAVE's silence: BREAK_TEST, then 256 DJNZ rounds, DE ($12CB) times.","comment":"BREAK: report D"},
        "772": {"synopsis":"The 256-round DJNZ of a silence round.","comment":"256 rounds (B = 0)"},
        "779": {"synopsis":"Sends the name, a byte at a time, up to its byte with bit 7.","comment":"A name byte"},
        "782": {"comment":"Up to the one with bit 7"},
        "787": {"comment":"Then memory from VERSN"},
        "790": {"synopsis":"Sends memory from VERSN up to E_LINE (TAPE_NEXT_ADDR ends the loop and SAVE)."},
        "793": {"comment":"Until E_LINE: TAPE_NEXT_ADDR ends the loop and SAVE"},
        "798": {"synopsis":"Sends the byte at HL to the tape, most significant bit first: 4 pulses for a 0, 9 for a\n1. A pulse is the VSYNC output switched off by OUT ($FF) and on by BREAK_TEST's IN from\nport $FE (Klive's ULA model), with delays: about 975 T, 300 us at 3.25 MHz (computed).\nAfter each bit a gap of 256 DJNZ rounds, about 4,350 T (1.3 ms). BREAK is report D.\nObserved: $26 went as 4,4,9,4,4,9,9,4 pulses.\nChanges: AF, BC, E.","comment":"E = the byte"},
        "799": {"comment":"A 1 shifted in behind: E is 0 after the eighth bit"},
        "800": {"synopsis":"The next bit of E into carry: C = 9 pulses for a 1, 4 for a 0; returns after the eighth."},
        "802": {"comment":"All 8 bits sent"},
        "803": {"comment":"The bit: A = $FF for a 1, 0 for a 0"},
        "804": {"comment":"C = 9 pulses for a 1, 4 for a 0"},
        "809": {"synopsis":"One pulse: OUT ($FF), a delay, BREAK_TEST's IN from $FE, a delay; C of them.","comment":"VSYNC off"},
        "813": {"synopsis":"The first delay of a pulse ($23 DJNZ rounds)."},
        "815": {"comment":"BREAK test; its IN from $FE turns VSYNC on"},
        "818": {"synopsis":"Report D when BREAK_TEST found BREAK (no carry).","comment":"BREAK: report D"},
        "822": {"synopsis":"The second delay of a pulse ($1E DJNZ rounds), then the next pulse."},
        "824": {"comment":"The next pulse"},
        "827": {"synopsis":"The gap after a bit (256 DJNZ rounds, B = 0), with carry cleared for RL E; then the\nnext bit.","comment":"The gap after the bit (B = 0: 256 rounds); clears carry for RL E"},
        "830": {"comment":"The next bit"},
        "832": {"synopsis":"LOAD name (class 5). The name is a string expression (TAPE_NAME); empty means any program,\nD bit 7 set. In FAST mode:\n1. Skips bytes (LOAD_SKIP, read and dropped) until a silence: LOAD_BYTE's wait for a pulse\n   runs out, drops its return and goes on at LOAD_NAME, as in the second of silence at a\n   recording's start.\n2. Reads the recording's name, up to its byte with bit 7, comparing each byte with the\n   given one; a difference goes back to 1 (observed: LOAD \"X\" against a recording named\n   $80 stopped at $036F with C = $80 and never matched).\n3. Adds 1 to E_LINE's high byte, so the end test (TAPE_NEXT_ADDR) cannot stop before the\n   recording's own E_LINE has arrived, then stores bytes from $4009 (VERSN) up to that\n   E_LINE: the system variables, program, display file and variables. D = 0 from here on:\n   BREAK or a silence restarts the machine (SYS_RESET, the old program lost: observed).\n   Before that BREAK is report D (observed, D = $C2).\nIt ends through TAPE_NEXT_ADDR, returning from LOAD in SLOW_FAST.\nObserved (a 943-byte recording, real pulses): E_LINE $4397 + $100 when the first byte\ncame; the bytes stored from $4009 those of the file; the load ended at the file's E_LINE\n$43B4, its last 4 bytes not read; 1119 frames.","comment":"DE = the name; carry when it is empty"},
        "835": {"comment":"D bit 7 = carry: no name given (an address has bit 7 clear)"},
        "839": {"synopsis":"LOAD's first step: reads and drops bytes until a silence takes LOAD_BYTE to LOAD_NAME.","comment":"Read and drop bytes until a silence"},
        "844": {"synopsis":"Reads a byte from the tape into C, most significant bit first (C starts as 1, a marker\nthat comes out as carry after the eighth bit).\nEach bit: waits for a pulse (LOAD_WAIT, up to 256 reads of port $FE, each followed by OUT\n($FF)): bit 0 of the read is SPACE (BREAK, LOAD_BREAK), bit 7 the tape signal. Then counts\nsamples in E from $94 down until 26 in a row are quiet: 63 to 148 samples is a 0, 149 or\nmore (E past 0, bit 7 set) a 1, fewer than 63 noise, and the bit starts again (observed:\nE $38 for a 0, $D7 for a 1).\nA silence (no pulse in 256 reads, about 17,400 T, 5 ms, computed) does not return: the\nreturn address is dropped and its high byte ($03) compared with D. D = 0 (storing the\nprogram) restarts the machine (SYS_RESET); otherwise it goes on at LOAD_NAME.\nExit: C = the byte, B = 0.\nChanges: AF, BC.","comment":"C = 1: the marker"},
        "846": {"synopsis":"Starts a bit: B = 0, up to 256 reads for a pulse.","comment":"Up to 256 reads for a pulse"},
        "848": {"synopsis":"One read of port $FE (keyboard row $7F and the tape): BREAK goes to LOAD_BREAK, a pulse\nto LOAD_PULSES; 256 quiet reads are a silence.","comment":"Keyboard row $7F (SPACE) and the tape signal"},
        "852": {"comment":"VSYNC off again"},
        "854": {"comment":"Bit 0 clear: SPACE, BREAK"},
        "858": {"comment":"Bit 7 set: a pulse"},
        "863": {"comment":"A silence: A = the return address's high byte, $03"},
        "864": {"comment":"D = 0 (storing the program): restart"},
        "865": {"synopsis":"Restarts the machine (SYS_RESET) when carry is clear: a silence or BREAK while storing\nthe program (D = 0)."},
        "868": {"synopsis":"After a silence: HL = the given name, and the recording's name is read and compared.","comment":"A silence before a name: HL = the given name"},
        "870": {"synopsis":"One byte of the recording's name, compared with the given name's unless none was given\n(D bit 7); a difference goes back to LOAD_SKIP.","comment":"C = the recording's next name byte"},
        "873": {"comment":"No name given: any will do"},
        "878": {"comment":"The same as the given name's?"},
        "879": {"comment":"No: back to skipping"},
        "881": {"synopsis":"On to the next name byte, up to the one with bit 7; then E_LINE + $100 and storing from\nVERSN."},
        "882": {"comment":"Until the byte with bit 7"},
        "885": {"comment":"E_LINE + $100: no early end before the recording's E_LINE"},
        "888": {"comment":"Store from VERSN"},
        "891": {"synopsis":"Stores bytes from VERSN up to E_LINE (TAPE_NEXT_ADDR ends the loop and LOAD); D = 0 from\nhere.","comment":"D = 0 (B after LOAD_BYTE): a silence or BREAK now restarts"},
        "895": {"comment":"Store the byte"},
        "896": {"comment":"Until E_LINE: TAPE_NEXT_ADDR ends the loop and LOAD"},
        "901": {"synopsis":"A pulse came: counts samples in E, down from $94, until 26 in a row are quiet.","comment":"A pulse: count the samples"},
        "902": {"comment":"E counts down from $94"},
        "904": {"synopsis":"Signal present: the quiet count starts again at 26.","comment":"B = 26 quiet samples end the bit"},
        "906": {"synopsis":"One sample of the tape signal (bit 7 of port $FE)."},
        "907": {"comment":"Sample the tape (bit 7)"},
        "910": {"comment":"E bit 7: past 0, 149 or more samples"},
        "913": {"comment":"Signal: the quiet count starts again"},
        "915": {"comment":"Quiet: count it"},
        "918": {"comment":"149 or more samples: a 1 (carry is clear, CCF sets it)"},
        "920": {"comment":"E $56 or more: under 63 samples, noise"},
        "924": {"synopsis":"Carry = the bit (CCF of the test); it goes into C, and the marker coming out ends the byte.","comment":"Carry = the bit"},
        "925": {"comment":"Into C; carry out: the marker, 8 bits read"},
        "930": {"synopsis":"BREAK: restarts the machine while storing the program (D = 0), else report D.","comment":"BREAK: with D = 0 (storing the program), restart"},
        "932": {"comment":"SYS_RESET through LOAD_RESET_IF (AND A cleared carry)"},
        "934": {"synopsis":"Report D: BREAK during SAVE, or during LOAD before the name has matched (observed)."},
        "936": {"synopsis":"The name of SAVE and LOAD: evaluates the expression (SCAN_EXPR); a number is report C.\nWhen checking, it drops its return, so the command returns at once.\nRunning: FAST mode (SET_FAST), the string taken off the calculator stack (STK_FETCH) and its\nlast character marked with bit 7, in the string itself.\nExit: DE = HL = the name's start, and carry, when it is empty (the test is the sign of C - 1,\nso a length of 129 to 256 counts as empty too); otherwise no carry, HL = its last character.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL.","comment":"Evaluate the name"},
        "942": {"comment":"Carry = FLAGS bit 7 (running), sign = bit 6 (a number)"},
        "943": {"comment":"A number: report C"},
        "946": {"comment":"Checking: return from the command"},
        "949": {"comment":"FAST mode"},
        "952": {"comment":"DE = the text, BC = its length"},
        "958": {"comment":"Empty: return with carry (still set from ADD A,A)"},
        "959": {"comment":"HL = the last character"},
        "960": {"comment":"Bit 7 marks the end of the name"},
        "963": {"synopsis":"NEW: starts the machine again from RAM_FILL with BC = RAMTOP - 1, so RAMTOP and what is\nabove it are kept. FAST mode first (SET_FAST).","comment":"FAST mode"},
        "966": {"comment":"BC = RAMTOP - 1: the fill stops below RAMTOP"},
        "971": {"synopsis":"Start-up after START: sizes the RAM, sets up the system variables and the memory\nareas, then falls into the main loop (MAIN_LOOP).\nEntry: BC = the highest address to test ($7FFF from START).\n1. Fills BC down to $4000 with $02, then checks it from the bottom up (RAM_CHECK).\n2. RAMTOP = the first address that failed, or BC+1 (RAM_TOP_FOUND).\n3. The stack goes below RAMTOP; I = $1E, IM 1, IY = $4000 (SYS_RESET).\n4. An empty display file, variables area and edit line, then SLOW mode if the display\n   NMIs work (EDIT_RESET).\nDoes not return.","comment":"HL = BC, the address the fill starts from (top of RAM to try)"},
        "973": {"comment":"A = $3F: the fill stops when H drops to $3F, after $4000 is written"},
        "975": {"synopsis":"Writes $02 from the top down to $4000.","comment":"Write $02: RAM_CHECK expects to count it down to 0"},
        "977": {"comment":"Next address down"},
        "978": {"comment":"Has HL gone below $4000?"},
        "979": {"comment":"No: keep filling"},
        "981": {"synopsis":"Walks up from $4000 checking the fill. A byte is RAM when the $02 written there can be\ncounted down to 0 with two DEC (HL). The walk stops at the first byte that cannot (no\nmemory, or a mirror of a byte already counted down) or after BC.\nExit: HL = the first address that is not RAM, or BC+1.","comment":"Clear carry for the SBC"},
        "982": {"comment":"HL - BC, undone by the ADD: carry stays set while HL < BC"},
        "985": {"comment":"The next address to check"},
        "986": {"comment":"Past BC: everything up to BC is RAM"},
        "988": {"comment":"RAM holds $02: this makes it $01"},
        "989": {"comment":"0 already: the byte read $01, not the fill: stop here"},
        "991": {"comment":"Count it down again: $01 to $00 only if the first write stuck"},
        "992": {"comment":"0: this byte is RAM, check the next one"},
        "994": {"synopsis":"HL is the first address that is not RAM: it becomes RAMTOP. Falls into SYS_RESET.","comment":"RAMTOP = the first address above the RAM found"},
        "997": {"synopsis":"Sets up the machine stack below RAMTOP, the CPU's I, interrupt mode and IY, and the\nsystem variables and memory areas from $407D up; then falls into EDIT_RESET and the\nmain loop.\nAlso entered from $0361 (in the tape code): a restart that keeps RAMTOP as it is.","comment":"HL = RAMTOP"},
        "1001": {"comment":"$3E below RAMTOP: the GOSUB stack's bottom word ($3E00) that RETURN stops at"},
        "1004": {"comment":"SP = RAMTOP-2: the machine stack grows down from here"},
        "1006": {"comment":"HL = RAMTOP-4, the slot the next word pushed occupies"},
        "1007": {"comment":"ERR_SP = RAMTOP-4: the first word below SP"},
        "1012": {"comment":"I = $1E: the ULA fetches character patterns from $1E00"},
        "1014": {"comment":"Interrupt mode 1: INT goes to $0038"},
        "1016": {"comment":"IY = $4000: (IY+n) addresses the system variables from now on"},
        "1020": {"comment":"CDFLAG = $40: SLOW mode asked for (bit 6), not yet running (bit 7)"},
        "1024": {"comment":"HL = $407D, the first byte after the system variables"},
        "1027": {"comment":"D_FILE = $407D: the display file starts there"},
        "1030": {"comment":"25: the display file's first NEWLINE and one for each of 24 lines"},
        "1032": {"synopsis":"Writes B NEWLINE codes from HL up: an empty display file, every line collapsed to\njust its NEWLINE.","comment":"$76 is NEWLINE"},
        "1035": {"comment":"Until all 25 are written"},
        "1037": {"comment":"VARS = the byte after the display file"},
        "1040": {"comment":"Empty variables area, with E_LINE and the calculator stack after it"},
        "1043": {"synopsis":"Puts an empty edit line at E_LINE and starts SLOW mode if it can, then falls into\nthe main loop.\nAlso entered from $0664, which starts a new edit line and keeps the program.","comment":"Empty edit line (cursor and NEWLINE) at E_LINE; DF_SZ = 2"},
        "1046": {"comment":"SLOW mode if the display NMIs work, FAST otherwise"},
        "1049": {"synopsis":"The main loop, one pass per change to the listing:\n1. Clears the screen and lists the program from S_TOP, the current line (E_PPC) marked\n   with an inverse '>'. When the current line did not fit, moves S_TOP and lists again.\n2. Shows the edit line in the lower part of the screen (EDIT_LINE_SHOW), growing the\n   lower part until it fits.\n3. Waits for a key (WAIT_KEY), decodes it by the cursor mode, and inserts it before the\n   cursor or runs its editing action (EDIT_KEY_TABLE).\nThe editing actions return to EDIT_LINE_SHOW, or here when the listing must change.","comment":"Clear the whole screen"},
        "1052": {"comment":"HL = E_PPC, the current line"},
        "1055": {"comment":"DE = S_TOP, the line the listing starts at"},
        "1060": {"comment":"Is the current line above the top of the listing?"},
        "1063": {"comment":"No: list from S_TOP"},
        "1066": {"comment":"Yes: S_TOP = E_PPC, the current line goes to the top"},
        "1069": {"synopsis":"Lists the screen from S_TOP, then checks the current line was among the lines shown.","comment":"Find the first line numbered S_TOP or more"},
        "1072": {"comment":"Found exactly: list from it"},
        "1074": {"comment":"Otherwise from the line before it"},
        "1075": {"synopsis":"Lists the screen from the line found; then, if the current line was not shown (BERG),\nmoves S_TOP and lists again.","comment":"List; BERG = 1 if the last line listed comes before the current one"},
        "1078": {"comment":"BERG 1 becomes 0: the listing ended before the current line"},
        "1081": {"comment":"The current line was shown: on to the edit line"},
        "1083": {"comment":"Find the current line; DE = the line before it"},
        "1089": {"comment":"Compare CH_ADD with the line before the current one"},
        "1098": {"comment":"Past it: move S_TOP down one line (S_TOP_NEXT)"},
        "1103": {"comment":"S_TOP = the number of the line before the current one"},
        "1106": {"comment":"List again"},
        "1108": {"synopsis":"Cursor down: the next program line becomes the current one (E_PPC), and the listing is\nredrawn (S_TOP_NEXT with HL at E_PPC).","comment":"HL = E_PPC"},
        "1111": {"synopsis":"Moves the line number held at (HL) (E_PPC or S_TOP) on to the number of the next program\nline, and redraws the listing; leaves it alone while FLAGX bit 5 is set.","comment":"DE = the line number held there"},
        "1116": {"comment":"One more"},
        "1117": {"comment":"Find the first line numbered that or more"},
        "1120": {"comment":"DE = its number (the last line's, or 0, past the end)"},
        "1124": {"synopsis":"Stores DE as the line number in the word whose high byte is at HL, unless FLAGX bit 5 is\nset; then redraws the listing. Also entered from KEY_UP.","comment":"FLAGX bit 5: leave the listing as it is"},
        "1130": {"comment":"Store the new line number"},
        "1133": {"comment":"Redraw from the top"},
        "1135": {"synopsis":"Starts an empty edit line, then shows it. Where KEY_EDIT returns when it has nothing\nto copy.","comment":"An empty edit line"},
        "1138": {"synopsis":"Shows the edit line and waits for the next key.\nRemoves the hidden numbers from the edit line (STRIP_NUMBERS), sets the K/L cursor mode\n(SET_KL_MODE), clears the lower part of the screen and prints the line there\n(PRINT_LOWER). When it does not fit, the lower part grows by one line, up to 24, and the\nline is printed again.","comment":"HL = E_LINE"},
        "1141": {"synopsis":"Walks the edit line up to its NEWLINE, removing each number marker and the hidden 5-byte\nnumber after it.","comment":"A = the next byte of the edit line"},
        "1142": {"comment":"$7E marks a number stored in its hidden 5-byte form"},
        "1149": {"comment":"Remove the marker and its 5 bytes"},
        "1152": {"comment":"Check the byte that moved into its place"},
        "1154": {"synopsis":"Up to the edit line's NEWLINE; then the cursor mode (SET_KL_MODE) and PRINT_LOWER.","comment":"NEWLINE ends the line"},
        "1157": {"comment":"Not yet: the next byte"},
        "1159": {"comment":"Set the K or L cursor mode for the cursor's position"},
        "1162": {"synopsis":"Clears the lower part of the screen and prints the edit line in it; when it overflows,\nadds a line to the lower part and tries again.","comment":"Clear the lower part (DF_SZ lines)"},
        "1168": {"comment":"ERR_NR = $FF: no error yet"},
        "1172": {"comment":"Print the edit line; running out of room sets ERR_NR"},
        "1175": {"comment":"No error: the line fitted"},
        "1181": {"comment":"A = DF_SZ, the lines the lower part has"},
        "1186": {"comment":"Already 24: it cannot grow"},
        "1189": {"comment":"One line more for the lower part"},
        "1195": {"comment":"Print position = line DF_SZ, column 1"},
        "1201": {"synopsis":"Walks HL back to the previous byte equal to A (the NEWLINE before the line just added to\nthe lower part); the line's contents then go on a collapsed display."},
        "1203": {"comment":"Until the byte before equals it"},
        "1207": {"comment":"A = the high byte of RAMTOP"},
        "1212": {"comment":"Under $4D00 (a collapsed display): reclaim that line's contents"},
        "1215": {"comment":"Print again"},
        "1217": {"synopsis":"Waits for a key and handles it. Clears X_PTR (no syntax error marker). In FAST mode the\npicture is shown only now, while waiting (DISPLAY_FRAME); in SLOW mode the display\ninterrupts read the keyboard. Either way, bit 0 of CDFLAG set means a new, steady key\nreading in LAST_K.\nAlso entered from $06DD, after a command has run.","comment":"X_PTR = 0: no syntax error marker"},
        "1226": {"comment":"FAST mode (CDFLAG bit 7 clear)?"},
        "1228": {"comment":"Then show the picture until a key is read"},
        "1231": {"synopsis":"Waits for CDFLAG bit 0, a new steady key reading; decodes it and handles it by MODE.","comment":"Wait for CDFLAG bit 0: a new key reading"},
        "1235": {"comment":"BC = LAST_K, the keyboard reading"},
        "1239": {"comment":"Mark it taken"},
        "1242": {"comment":"HL = the key's entry in the key tables; carry if one key"},
        "1245": {"comment":"No single key: back to the edit line"},
        "1247": {"comment":"A = MODE"},
        "1251": {"comment":"MODE 0 (K or L)"},
        "1254": {"comment":"MODE above 1: graphics"},
        "1256": {"comment":"MODE 1 (F) lasts one key: MODE = 0"},
        "1259": {"comment":"E = the key's position, from 0"},
        "1261": {"comment":"A shifted key (39 and up)?"},
        "1265": {"comment":"Shifted: the same entry as its unshifted key"},
        "1266": {"synopsis":"A key in function mode: its token from the function table ($00CC).","comment":"The function table"},
        "1271": {"synopsis":"A key in graphics mode: NEWLINE leaves the mode (as the K/L key does), a character key\ngives its inverse character, and a shifted key a graphic from the table at $00F3.","comment":"A = the key's code"},
        "1274": {"comment":"NEWLINE: leave graphics mode"},
        "1276": {"comment":"A character ($00-$3F)?"},
        "1278": {"comment":"Bit 7: the inverse character"},
        "1280": {"comment":"Insert it"},
        "1282": {"comment":"Shifted keys: $00C7 + position, $00F3 for the first"},
        "1285": {"synopsis":"HL = the key's entry in the table chosen (+ DE), then its code (KEY_CODE).","comment":"HL = the entry in the chosen table"},
        "1288": {"synopsis":"A key in K or L mode. In K mode (FLAGS bit 2 clear) a letter key gives its keyword\ntoken, $E6 and up; anything else gives its own code.","comment":"A = the key's code"},
        "1289": {"comment":"L mode: the code as it is"},
        "1295": {"comment":"Letters ($26-$3F) become keyword tokens ($E6-$FF)"},
        "1297": {"comment":"Was it a letter?"},
        "1301": {"synopsis":"A = the code from the key table.","comment":"No: the code from the table"},
        "1302": {"synopsis":"A code $70-$7F (an editing key: CP $F0 overflows for them) runs its action\n(EDIT_KEY_JUMP); any other code is inserted.","comment":"Codes $70-$7F (editing keys) overflow: run their action"},
        "1307": {"synopsis":"Inserts the key's code before the cursor and shows the edit line again.","comment":"E = the code"},
        "1308": {"comment":"HL = the cursor's address"},
        "1312": {"comment":"Insert the code before the cursor"},
        "1315": {"synopsis":"Shows the edit line again (EDIT_LINE_SHOW): where the editing actions end.","comment":"Show the edit line again"},
        "1318": {"synopsis":"Inserts A before the byte at HL: one byte of room (MAKE_ROOM_ONE), then A in it.\nStops with report 4 when there is no room."},
        "1323": {"synopsis":"NEWLINE in graphics mode: A = $78, and on into EDIT_KEY_JUMP with that code's action.","comment":"NEWLINE in graphics mode acts as the K/L key"},
        "1325": {"synopsis":"Runs the action of editing key code A ($70-$79): pushes its address from\nEDIT_KEY_TABLE ($0482 + 2A) and runs SET_KL_MODE, which returns into the action with\nHL at the cursor.","comment":"HL = $0482 + 2A: the code's entry ($0562 for $70)"},
        "1334": {"comment":"The action runs when SET_KL_MODE returns"},
        "1335": {"synopsis":"Sets the K/L cursor mode for the cursor's position: FLAGS bit 2, set for L.\nWalks the edit line from E_LINE to the cursor ($7F): K at the start and after THEN, L after\nany other letter or keyword; digits, symbols and hidden numbers leave it as it is. With\nFLAGX bit 5 set it is always L.\nExit: HL = the cursor's address.","comment":"HL = E_LINE"},
        "1338": {"comment":"FLAGX bit 5: always L"},
        "1344": {"synopsis":"K mode so far (FLAGS bit 2 clear), then the scan goes on.","comment":"K mode so far"},
        "1348": {"synopsis":"SET_KL_MODE's walk: the next byte up to the cursor; letters and keywords give L, THEN\ngives K, digits, symbols and hidden numbers leave it.","comment":"A = the next byte"},
        "1349": {"comment":"The cursor: done"},
        "1353": {"comment":"Skip a hidden number"},
        "1358": {"comment":"Digits and symbols leave the mode as it is"},
        "1362": {"comment":"THEN: K mode again"},
        "1366": {"synopsis":"L mode (FLAGS bit 2 set), then the scan goes on.","comment":"A letter or keyword: L mode"},
        "1372": {"synopsis":"Removes the byte at HL (RECLAIM with BC = 1)."},
        "1378": {"synopsis":"EDIT_KEY_TABLE: the action of each editing key code, one word each, from $70\n(EDIT_KEY_JUMP reads it as $0482 + 2 x code):\n$70 up KEY_UP, $71 down KEY_DOWN, $72 left KEY_LEFT, $73 right KEY_RIGHT,\n$74 GRAPHICS KEY_MODE, $75 EDIT KEY_EDIT, $76 NEWLINE KEY_NEWLINE,\n$77 RUBOUT KEY_RUBOUT, $78 K/L KEY_MODE, $79 FUNCTION KEY_MODE."},
        "1398": {"synopsis":"Cursor left: swaps the cursor with the byte before it; nothing at the start of the line.","comment":"At the start? (then no action)"},
        "1401": {"comment":"A = the byte before the cursor"},
        "1402": {"comment":"The cursor takes its place"},
        "1407": {"synopsis":"Cursor right: swaps the cursor with the byte after it; nothing when that is the NEWLINE."},
        "1408": {"comment":"A = the byte after the cursor"},
        "1411": {"comment":"NEWLINE: already at the end"},
        "1413": {"comment":"The cursor moves right"},
        "1416": {"synopsis":"Puts the byte where the cursor was (the cursor has moved), then the edit line is shown again.","comment":"The byte goes where the cursor was"},
        "1417": {"synopsis":"To EDIT_REDRAW: the editing action is done."},
        "1419": {"synopsis":"RUBOUT: removes the byte before the cursor; nothing at the start of the line.","comment":"At the start? (then no action)"},
        "1422": {"comment":"Remove the byte before the cursor"},
        "1427": {"synopsis":"HL - 1. Returns when the cursor is not at the start of the edit line; when it is, drops\nthe return address, so the key's action ends here (EDIT_KEY_EXIT).","comment":"HL = the byte before the cursor"},
        "1433": {"comment":"Is the edit line's first byte the cursor?"},
        "1436": {"comment":"Yes: drop the caller"},
        "1437": {"synopsis":"To EDIT_KEY_END."},
        "1439": {"synopsis":"Cursor up: the line before the current one becomes the current line (E_PPC), and the\nlisting is redrawn (SET_LINE_WORD).","comment":"HL = E_PPC"},
        "1442": {"comment":"Find it; DE = the line before it"},
        "1446": {"comment":"DE = that line's number (0 if none)"},
        "1449": {"comment":"HL = the high byte of E_PPC"},
        "1455": {"synopsis":"GRAPHICS, K/L and FUNCTION: MODE = the code's low 3 bits, so 4 (G), 0 (K or L) and 1 (F).","comment":"MODE = code AND 7"},
        "1463": {"synopsis":"Part of LINE_NO: HL is not a line, so it tries the line at DE, and then two zero bytes in the\nROM ($04C2, inside the LD HL,$0000 at $04C1), which read as line 0."},
        "1467": {"synopsis":"DE = the number of the line at HL (high byte first). When HL is not a line (its first byte\nhas bit 6 or 7 set, as the display file's NEWLINE has), the line at DE is used, and when that\nis not one either, 0.\nExit: HL = the address of the number's low byte.","comment":"Not a line number's high byte?"},
        "1476": {"synopsis":"EDIT: copies the current line (E_PPC) into the edit line: its number is printed there as\ndigits, then the cursor, then the line's text. With FLAGX bit 5 set, with no line, or with\nno room, it starts an empty edit line instead (EMPTY_EDIT_LINE).","comment":"Clear the lower part"},
        "1479": {"comment":"Return to EMPTY_EDIT_LINE unless the copy is made"},
        "1491": {"comment":"DF_CC = E_LINE: printing goes into the edit line"},
        "1497": {"comment":"S_POSN = column 33, line 24"},
        "1509": {"comment":"No line (number 0): an empty edit line"},
        "1513": {"comment":"Print the line's number as digits into the edit line"},
        "1517": {"comment":"BC = the length of the line's text"},
        "1521": {"comment":"DE = where the printing stopped"},
        "1527": {"comment":"The cursor after the number"},
        "1535": {"comment":"The text and 29 bytes more must fit below the stack"},
        "1538": {"comment":"No room: an empty edit line"},
        "1539": {"comment":"Copy the text and its NEWLINE"},
        "1542": {"comment":"Drop EMPTY_EDIT_LINE's return address"},
        "1543": {"comment":"STKBOT = STKEND = the end of the new edit line"},
        "1548": {"synopsis":"NEWLINE: enters the edit line.\n1. Clears the lower part. A typed line (FLAGX bit 5 clear) also clears the printer buffer\n   and the screen, unless it is COPY, which prints the screen; it returns to MAIN_LOOP.\n   INPUT's answer (FLAGX bit 5 set) returns to EDIT_LINE_SHOW instead.\n2. Checks the syntax (CHECK_LINE). An error comes back through ERR_SP to MAIN_LOOP with the\n   line still in the editor, an inverse S at the error.\n3. Removes the cursor and reads the line number (EDIT_LINE_NO).\n4. A numbered line goes into the program (STORE_LINE). A line without a number runs at once\n   as a command, PPC = $FFFE (RUN_EDIT_LINE); INPUT's answer is taken there too, and the\n   program goes on (INPUT_ANSWER). An empty line only starts a new edit line.","comment":"Clear the lower part"},
        "1551": {"comment":"Where INPUT's answer goes back to"},
        "1554": {"comment":"FLAGX bit 5: the line is INPUT's answer"},
        "1560": {"comment":"HL = E_LINE"},
        "1564": {"comment":"COPY ($FF) prints the screen: keep it"},
        "1568": {"comment":"Empty the printer buffer"},
        "1571": {"comment":"Clear the screen"},
        "1574": {"synopsis":"A typed line returns to MAIN_LOOP after the check (its address goes into the ERR_SP slot).","comment":"Go back to MAIN_LOOP afterwards"},
        "1577": {"synopsis":"Puts the return (MAIN_LOOP, or EDIT_LINE_SHOW for INPUT's answer) in the ERR_SP slot, checks\nthe line, removes the cursor and stores, runs or answers it.","comment":"Into the ERR_SP slot for the check: a syntax error returns to the editor"},
        "1578": {"comment":"Check the syntax; an error returns through ERR_SP"},
        "1581": {"comment":"Off again: while running, CALL SCAN_LINE's return takes the slot"},
        "1582": {"comment":"HL = the cursor's address"},
        "1585": {"comment":"Remove the cursor"},
        "1588": {"comment":"BC = the line's number; NZ for INPUT's answer"},
        "1591": {"comment":"INPUT's answer"},
        "1595": {"comment":"A line number: store the line"},
        "1598": {"comment":"BC = $FFFE"},
        "1600": {"comment":"PPC = $FFFE: a command, not a program line"},
        "1604": {"comment":"DF_SZ = 2"},
        "1608": {"comment":"DE = D_FILE: no program line follows a command"},
        "1614": {"synopsis":"INPUT's answer, its check passed. An answer starting with a NEWLINE only starts a new edit\nline (EDIT_RESET), but an empty answer to INPUT A never got here: CHECK_LINE stopped it with\nthe error marker first (observed). Otherwise the print position INPUT saved in T_ADDR comes\nback, and the answer runs (RUN_EDIT_LINE) with the program going on at NXTLIN.","comment":"Empty answer?"},
        "1618": {"comment":"BC = T_ADDR: the print position INPUT saved"},
        "1622": {"comment":"Back to it"},
        "1625": {"comment":"DE = NXTLIN: where the program goes on"},
        "1629": {"comment":"DF_SZ = 2"},
        "1633": {"synopsis":"Runs the edit line (a command, or INPUT's answer), then each program line from NXTLIN,\nuntil the program ends, an error occurs or BREAK is pressed; then reports (REPORT).\nEntry: DE = the line to go on with afterwards.","comment":"A = the first character"},
        "1636": {"synopsis":"An empty line only starts a new edit line (EDIT_RESET); otherwise FLAGS = $80 and the run\nbegins.","comment":"Empty: only a new edit line"},
        "1639": {"comment":"FLAGS = $80: run, not only check"},
        "1644": {"synopsis":"Runs one line: NXTLIN = the next, SCAN_LINE on this one; then reports an error, BREAK or the\nprogram's end, or goes on with NXTLIN.","comment":"NXTLIN = the line after this one"},
        "1648": {"comment":"CH_ADD = the line's text"},
        "1651": {"comment":"Run its statements; this CALL's return ($0676) is in the ERR_SP slot,\nso an error while running comes back here to be reported"},
        "1654": {"comment":"Printer output off"},
        "1660": {"comment":"X_PTR high byte = $C0: no error marker"},
        "1663": {"comment":"Empty calculator stack"},
        "1666": {"comment":"No longer waiting for INPUT"},
        "1670": {"comment":"ERR_NR bit 7 clear: an error, report it"},
        "1676": {"comment":"HL = NXTLIN"},
        "1679": {"comment":"Bit 6 or 7 set: the display file, past the last line"},
        "1685": {"comment":"PPC = the next line's number"},
        "1695": {"comment":"HL = the line after it"},
        "1696": {"comment":"Carry: BREAK is not pressed"},
        "1699": {"comment":"Run that line"},
        "1704": {"comment":"An error already?"},
        "1708": {"comment":"BREAK: report D (code 12)"},
        "1710": {"synopsis":"Ends a run: prints the printer buffer if it holds anything, then the report on the bottom\nline as code/line (0/10 after line 10 ran to the end; 0/0 after a command). Sets OLDPPC\nfor CONT; then a new edit line, and back to WAIT_KEY.","comment":"PR_CC bit 7 clear: the printer buffer holds something"},
        "1714": {"comment":"Print it"},
        "1717": {"comment":"Line 1, column 33: the left of the bottom line"},
        "1723": {"comment":"A = ERR_NR"},
        "1726": {"comment":"BC = PPC, the line that ran last"},
        "1730": {"comment":"ERR_NR + 1: the report code, 0 for OK"},
        "1731": {"comment":"OK: OLDPPC stays"},
        "1733": {"comment":"Report 9 (STOP)?"},
        "1737": {"comment":"STOP: CONT goes on at the next line"},
        "1738": {"synopsis":"OLDPPC = BC, the line CONT goes on at (the next line, unless the report is about this one).","comment":"OLDPPC = the line CONT goes on at"},
        "1744": {"comment":"The report names the line itself"},
        "1745": {"synopsis":"Prints the report: its code, '/', the line number; then a new edit line and WAIT_KEY.","comment":"The code, as a digit or letter"},
        "1750": {"comment":"'/'"},
        "1751": {"comment":"The line's number"},
        "1754": {"comment":"A new edit line"},
        "1757": {"comment":"Wait for the next key"},
        "1760": {"synopsis":"Puts a numbered edit line into the program, in line-number order. A line with the same\nnumber is removed first; when the edit line holds only the number, that is all (how a line\nis deleted). Memory moves in FAST mode (SET_FAST); returns to EDIT_RESET.\nEntry: BC = the line's number, CH_ADD = its text.","comment":"E_PPC = the new line: it becomes the current line"},
        "1767": {"comment":"DE = CH_ADD, the text after the number"},
        "1771": {"comment":"Return to EDIT_RESET"},
        "1777": {"comment":"The text's length, up to STKBOT, its NEWLINE included"},
        "1778": {"comment":"The line's number"},
        "1779": {"comment":"FAST mode while memory moves"},
        "1782": {"comment":"Clear the screen"},
        "1786": {"comment":"Find the line with that number"},
        "1789": {"comment":"None: nothing to remove"},
        "1791": {"comment":"HL = it, BC = its length"},
        "1794": {"comment":"Remove the old line"},
        "1797": {"synopsis":"Opens room for the new line (its text + 4 bytes) unless only a NEWLINE was typed (a\ndeletion, done).","comment":"BC = the text's length"},
        "1801": {"comment":"Only the NEWLINE: the line is deleted, done"},
        "1806": {"comment":"4 more bytes: the number and the length"},
        "1807": {"comment":"One byte early, so pointers at HL move up too"},
        "1808": {"comment":"Open the room"},
        "1811": {"comment":"SLOW mode again if it is asked for"},
        "1821": {"comment":"Copy the text in from the end of the edit line, downwards"},
        "1826": {"comment":"DE = the line's number"},
        "1830": {"comment":"The text's length, low byte below"},
        "1834": {"comment":"The number, high byte first"},
        "1836": {"synopsis":"LLIST (class 3): FLAGS bit 1 set, then LIST_CMD: the same listing, through PRINT_CHAR to\nthe printer buffer (PRINTER_CHAR), which is printed a line at a time; no full screen stops\nit. Bit 1 is cleared again by the run loop after the line ($0676) and by CLS_LINES.","comment":"FLAGS bit 1: printer output"},
        "1840": {"synopsis":"LIST [n] (class 3: n is 0 when left out, OPTIONAL_NUM). Runs only when running.\nE_PPC = n AND $3FFF, so line n becomes the current one (observed: LIST 20000 leaves E_PPC\n3616); then lists from the first line numbered n or more (FIND_LINE) to the end of the\nprogram (LIST_LINES), the current line marked with an inverse '>'. A full screen stops it\nwith report 5 (observed: LIST of 25 lines showed 10-220 and 5/0).\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL; E_PPC.","comment":"BC = n; report B past 65535"},
        "1844": {"comment":"Bits 15-14 dropped"},
        "1848": {"comment":"E_PPC = n AND $3FFF"},
        "1851": {"comment":"HL = the first line numbered n or more"},
        "1854": {"synopsis":"Lists program lines from HL to the end of the program, each with its number and a space,\nor an inverse '>' for the current line (E_PPC), through PRINT_CHAR (the screen, or the\nprinter for LLIST). Each line sets BERG first: 1 when it comes before the current line, 0\nafter it (and past the program), unchanged on the current line itself.\nA full screen stops it with report 5. The main loop calls it at $0433 with the stack at the\nERR_SP slot, so the CALL's return ($0436) is also the error return: report 5 comes back\nthere as if LIST_LINES had returned, ERR_NR left at 4, and BERG = 1 says the current line\nwas not reached; S_TOP then moves down a line and the screen is listed again (observed:\n25 lines, report 5 then $0436 with BERG 1, S_TOP 30 to 40, the second pass to the end).\nPRINT_CHAR works on the other register set, so DE and HL survive the printing.\nReturns (LIST_ONE_LINE drops the loop's return) when past the last line.","comment":"E = 0"},
        "1856": {"synopsis":"LIST_LINES's loop: one line at a time, until LIST_ONE_LINE returns from it."},
        "1861": {"synopsis":"Lists the line at HL; past the last line it returns from LIST_LINES instead.","comment":"BC = E_PPC"},
        "1865": {"comment":"Compare this line's number with it"},
        "1868": {"comment":"$92, an inverse '>', for the current line"},
        "1875": {"comment":"Not the current line: E = 1 if it comes before it"},
        "1877": {"synopsis":"BERG = E; past the program it returns from LIST_LINES, else prints the line's number, the\nmarker or a space, and the line.","comment":"BERG = E"},
        "1884": {"comment":"Past the last line: return from LIST_LINES"},
        "1889": {"comment":"To the length"},
        "1890": {"comment":"An inverse '>' for the current line, else a space"},
        "1892": {"comment":"Past the length: HL = the text"},
        "1894": {"synopsis":"Prints the line at HL up to and including its NEWLINE. Hidden numbers are skipped,\nkeywords spelt out (PRINT_TOKEN), the cursor shown as an inverse K, L, F or G for the mode\n(PEL_CURSOR), and an inverse S printed where X_PTR points (the syntax error).\nMoves CH_ADD along the line.","comment":"CH_ADD = the line"},
        "1901": {"synopsis":"PRINT_EDIT_LINE's loop: at X_PTR an inverse S first, then the next byte.","comment":"At X_PTR?"},
        "1913": {"comment":"$B8: an inverse S marks the error"},
        "1916": {"synopsis":"The next byte of the line at CH_ADD; a hidden number is skipped (SKIP_NUMBER)."},
        "1919": {"comment":"A = the next byte"},
        "1921": {"comment":"Skip a hidden number"},
        "1929": {"comment":"The cursor?"},
        "1933": {"comment":"NEWLINE: print it and return"},
        "1937": {"comment":"Bit 6: a keyword token"},
        "1941": {"comment":"Spell it out"},
        "1946": {"synopsis":"Prints an ordinary character (bit 6 clear) and goes on with the next byte.","comment":"Print the character"},
        "1949": {"synopsis":"Prints the cursor: an inverse F or G from MODE ($AB, $AC), or in MODE 0 an inverse K or L\nfrom FLAGS bit 2 ($B0, $B1)."},
        "1962": {"synopsis":"The cursor's character: B ($AB or $B0) + bit 2 of A, which is MODE or FLAGS."},
        "1972": {"synopsis":"When A is the number marker $7E, moves HL past the hidden 5-byte number after it.\nExit: Z set when it was a number."},
        "1981": {"synopsis":"Turns a keyboard reading (BC, as in LAST_K) into a key: HL = the key's entry in the key\ntables ($007E unshifted, $00A5 shifted), with carry set. No carry when no key, or more\nthan one, is pressed.\nExit: D = 0, E = the key's position, from 1."},
        "1991": {"synopsis":"KEY_DECODE's count: adds L to A for each set bit of C below its first clear one, the\nkey's place in the row."},
        "2012": {"synopsis":"A leading zero: not printed when E = $FF; otherwise E (0, a space, or $1C, '0') is printed."},
        "2017": {"synopsis":"Prints one decimal digit of HL: how many times BC (minus 1000, 100 or 10) can be added to\nit; HL keeps the remainder. A leading zero prints as a space when E = 0, and not at all when\nE = $FF. After a digit E = $1C, so later zeros print as 0."},
        "2018": {"synopsis":"Adds BC (a negative power of ten) until the carry stops: A counts the times."},
        "2027": {"synopsis":"Prints A as a digit: the character $1C + A ('0' to '9', then 'A' and up, as report codes\nneed). E = $1C after it."},
        "2030": {"synopsis":"Prints A as RST $10 does: a space straight through PRINT_CHAR, anything else through\nPRINT_NONSPACE."},
        "2033": {"synopsis":"Clears FLAGS bit 0 (the last thing printed is not a space), then PRINT_CHAR."},
        "2037": {"synopsis":"Prints the character in A: on the screen when bit 1 of FLAGS is clear ($0808, at the\nprint position S_POSN), to the other output ($0851) when it is set.\nKeeps the alternate HL."},
        "2039": {"comment":"FLAGS bit 1 chooses where the character goes"},
        "2050": {"synopsis":"FLAGS bit 1 set: the character goes to the printer buffer (PRINTER_CHAR).","comment":"FLAGS bit 1: to the printer buffer"},
        "2053": {"synopsis":"PRINT_CHAR's exit: the alternate HL back, EXX, return."},
        "2056": {"synopsis":"Prints character A on the screen at the print position (DF_CC, S_POSN).\nWhere the display file holds a character, the new one replaces it. Where it holds the\nline's NEWLINE, the line is shorter than 32 (a collapsed display): the character is\ninserted before the NEWLINE and the line grows (PS_INSERT). A full line goes on at the start\nof the next one. A NEWLINE moves to the next line. Running into the lower part of the\nscreen, or past the bottom, is report 5.","comment":"D = the character"},
        "2062": {"comment":"At the start of a line (column count 33)?"},
        "2066": {"synopsis":"PRINT_SCREEN's work: a NEWLINE goes to PS_NEWLINE; a character replaces the display file's\nbyte, is inserted before a short line's NEWLINE, or moves to the next line when this one\nis full.","comment":"A NEWLINE to print?"},
        "2074": {"comment":"Is the display file's byte here the line's NEWLINE?"},
        "2076": {"comment":"No: the new character replaces it"},
        "2078": {"comment":"Yes: room left on this line?"},
        "2079": {"comment":"Yes: insert the character before the NEWLINE"},
        "2082": {"comment":"No: past the NEWLINE, to the next line"},
        "2088": {"comment":"S_POSN = the next line's start"},
        "2092": {"synopsis":"At a line's start: report 5 when it is the lower part's first line or past the bottom."},
        "2093": {"comment":"Into the lower part?"},
        "2098": {"comment":"Or past the bottom?"},
        "2099": {"comment":"No: print it here"},
        "2101": {"synopsis":"Stops with error code 4 (report 5): no room on the screen.","comment":"Error code 4"},
        "2106": {"synopsis":"Room for one byte before the NEWLINE (MAKE_ROOM_ONE): the line grows.","comment":"Room for one byte before the NEWLINE"},
        "2110": {"synopsis":"The character into the display file; DF_CC and the column count move on.","comment":"The character into the display file"},
        "2112": {"comment":"DF_CC = the next position"},
        "2115": {"comment":"One column fewer left"},
        "2119": {"synopsis":"A NEWLINE: the print position goes to the next line's start (SET_PRINT_POS), FLAGS bit 0 set.","comment":"NEWLINE: the next line, from its start"},
        "2122": {"comment":"FLAGS bit 0: no space before a keyword next"},
        "2129": {"synopsis":"Puts character A into the printer buffer (PRINT_CHAR with FLAGS bit 1 set: LPRINT, LLIST).\nA NEWLINE prints the buffer (PRBUFF_OUT). Any other character goes at PR_CC's low byte\n($403C-$405B; bit 7, the empty mark, is dropped); when that has reached $5C, the buffer's\nNEWLINE (32 characters held), the buffer is printed and emptied first. PR_CC moves on.\nChanges: AF, C, HL.","comment":"NEWLINE: print the buffer"},
        "2133": {"comment":"C = the character"},
        "2137": {"comment":"The position, the empty mark dropped"},
        "2139": {"comment":"At the buffer's NEWLINE: 32 characters held"},
        "2144": {"comment":"Then print it first; HL = $403C after"},
        "2147": {"comment":"The character into the buffer"},
        "2149": {"comment":"PR_CC = the next position"},
        "2153": {"synopsis":"COPY (class 0): prints the screen's upper 22 lines, whatever DF_SZ is: PRBUFF_OUT's line\nprinter entered at $0876 with D = $16 lines from D_FILE + 1 (observed). A collapsed line\nprints only the characters it holds.\nWith nothing answering port $FB (Klive's port reads $FF) it returns at once (observed:\nCOPY in a program, then report 0).","comment":"22 lines"},
        "2155": {"comment":"From the display file's first line"},
        "2161": {"synopsis":"Prints the printer buffer (PRBUFF: one line, 32 characters and a NEWLINE) and empties it\n(CLEAR_PRBUFF). COPY enters at PRN_LINES with D lines from HL instead.\nCalled by PRINTER_CHAR on a NEWLINE or a full buffer, and by REPORT when the buffer still\nholds something at the end of a run (observed: LPRINT \"AB\"; then 33 Cs: a flush from\n$0860 at 32 characters, PR_CC $5C, then the last 3 on the NEWLINE, PR_CC $3F).\nIn FAST mode (SET_FAST), each character line as 8 pixel rows, through port $FB:\n- before a row, reads $FB until bit 7 is set, testing BREAK each time (BREAK writes $7F and\n  is report D); a read with bit 6 set ends the whole print at once (PRN_END);\n- a character's row is its byte in the character set at $1E00 (code x 8 + row), inverted\n  for an inverse character; its 8 pixels go out one per write in bit 7, the leftmost\n  first, each after a read with bit 0 set;\n- a row ends at the line's NEWLINE with one more write, after the same wait.\nOn the last line, rows 3, 6 and 7 put bits into every write's bits 0-1 (D = E AND 2E, then\nshifted right). At the end $04 is written; then SLOW_FAST, and the buffer is emptied.\nKlive emulates no printer: port $FB reads $FF, so bit 6 ends every print at the first\nread (observed).\nChanges: AF, DE, HL.","comment":"One line"},
        "2163": {"comment":"From PRBUFF"},
        "2166": {"synopsis":"COPY's entry into the line printer: FAST mode, BC kept, D lines from HL.","comment":"FAST mode"},
        "2170": {"synopsis":"One character line: E = 0, the first pixel row.","comment":"The line's start waits"},
        "2172": {"comment":"E = the pixel row, 0-7"},
        "2173": {"synopsis":"One pixel row: a write to $FB, HL = the line's start.","comment":"A write before each row"},
        "2175": {"comment":"HL = the line's start"},
        "2176": {"synopsis":"Waits for read bit 7 before a row, testing BREAK (report D) each time; bit 6 ends the print.","comment":"BREAK?"},
        "2181": {"comment":"BREAK: writes $7F, report D"},
        "2186": {"synopsis":"Read bit 7 set: the row starts. Bit 6 set ends the print (PRN_END); D gets the row's extra\nbits on the last line.","comment":"Bit 7 into carry, bit 6 into the sign"},
        "2189": {"comment":"Bit 6: end the print"},
        "2192": {"comment":"Bit 7 clear: wait"},
        "2196": {"comment":"D = E AND 2E on the last line (D < 2), else 0"},
        "2203": {"comment":"D: the extra bits of this row's writes"},
        "2204": {"synopsis":"One character of the row: NEWLINE ends the row; otherwise C = its pattern byte for this row\n($1E00 + code x 8 + row), inverted for an inverse character.","comment":"The next character"},
        "2207": {"comment":"NEWLINE: the row is done"},
        "2212": {"comment":"Code x 8, inverse bit to carry ..."},
        "2216": {"comment":"... H = $1E, or $1F for codes $20-$3F"},
        "2220": {"comment":"+ the row: HL = the pattern byte"},
        "2222": {"comment":"Carry = the inverse bit"},
        "2225": {"comment":"C = the 8 pixels, inverted for an inverse character"},
        "2229": {"synopsis":"One pixel: the next bit of C into bit 7 of the write, over D's bits shifted right.","comment":"D shifted right with the next pixel in bit 7"},
        "2234": {"synopsis":"Waits for read bit 0, then writes the pixel; 8 of them, then the next character.","comment":"Wait for bit 0 of a read"},
        "2239": {"comment":"One pixel out"},
        "2247": {"synopsis":"The row's last write after the bit 0 wait; then the next row (8), the next line (D), and $04\nafter the last.","comment":"Wait for bit 0"},
        "2252": {"comment":"D shifted right, no pixel: the row's last write"},
        "2257": {"comment":"The next row ..."},
        "2258": {"comment":"... until 8"},
        "2262": {"comment":"Drop the line start; HL = the next line"},
        "2263": {"comment":"The next line"},
        "2266": {"comment":"The last write: $04"},
        "2270": {"synopsis":"SLOW mode again if asked for (SLOW_FAST), BC back, and on into CLEAR_PRBUFF.","comment":"SLOW mode again if asked for"},
        "2273": {"comment":"BC as on entry; falls into CLEAR_PRBUFF"},
        "2274": {"synopsis":"Empties the printer buffer: 32 spaces and a NEWLINE in PRBUFF, and PR_CC = its start with\nbit 7 set, which marks it empty.","comment":"HL = the last byte of PRBUFF"},
        "2277": {"comment":"The NEWLINE that ends the buffer"},
        "2281": {"synopsis":"Fills PRBUFF with B spaces, downwards."},
        "2282": {"comment":"32 spaces"},
        "2287": {"comment":"Bit 7: the buffer is empty"},
        "2293": {"synopsis":"AT: moves the print position to line B, column C (counted from the top left). Line over\n23 or column over 31 is report B; a line in the lower part of the screen is report 5. Then\nthe print position (or, for the printer, PR_CC) is set, counting down: 24 - line, 33 - column.","comment":"23 - the line"},
        "2296": {"comment":"Over 23: report B"},
        "2298": {"synopsis":"Line count A (24 - the line): report 5 in the lower part; B = it + 1, then the column's\ncheck. TAB enters here too.","comment":"In the lower part (DF_SZ lines)?"},
        "2301": {"comment":"Then report 5"},
        "2305": {"comment":"B = 24 - the line"},
        "2308": {"comment":"31 - the column"},
        "2309": {"synopsis":"Report B when the column was over 31; else C = 33 - the column.","comment":"Over 31: report B"},
        "2314": {"comment":"C = 33 - the column"},
        "2315": {"synopsis":"Moves the print position to BC: SET_PRINT_POS on the screen, PR_CC = $5D - C for the\nprinter (FLAGS bit 1).","comment":"To the screen: SET_PRINT_POS"},
        "2324": {"comment":"To the printer: PR_CC = $5D - C"},
        "2328": {"synopsis":"Sets the print position: S_POSN = BC (C = column, B = line, both counting down, so\n33 and 24 are the top left) and DF_CC to that place in the display file.\nFinds the line by walking back from VARS over B + 1 NEWLINEs, then searches it for a\nNEWLINE within column + 1 bytes. A full line: DF_CC = the cell. A shorter (collapsed)\nline: spaces are inserted before its NEWLINE up to the column (MAKE_ROOM) and DF_CC =\nthat NEWLINE, now at the column; with column count 1 (past the last column) nothing is\ninserted and DF_CC = the line's NEWLINE.\nExit: HL = DF_CC; B = 0.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL.","comment":"S_POSN = BC"},
        "2332": {"comment":"HL = VARS, just past the display file's last NEWLINE"},
        "2335": {"comment":"D = the column count"},
        "2339": {"comment":"C = 34 - column count = column + 1: the bytes to search"},
        "2342": {"comment":"B + 1 NEWLINEs back: the one before the wanted line"},
        "2343": {"synopsis":"Walks back over B NEWLINEs from VARS; then DF_CC = the cell, padding a short line with spaces\nup to it.","comment":"Back to the previous NEWLINE"},
        "2349": {"comment":"The line's first byte"},
        "2350": {"comment":"A NEWLINE within column + 1 bytes: a short line"},
        "2352": {"comment":"HL = the cell, or that NEWLINE"},
        "2357": {"comment":"BC ran out (a full line): done"},
        "2359": {"comment":"Column count 1: no padding"},
        "2361": {"comment":"Room for the missing spaces before the NEWLINE; DE = its last byte"},
        "2368": {"synopsis":"Fills the new room with spaces (code 0), downwards; DF_CC = the NEWLINE now at the column.","comment":"Fill them with spaces (code 0), downwards"},
        "2375": {"comment":"DF_CC = the NEWLINE, now at the column"},
        "2379": {"synopsis":"Prints keyword token A spelt out from the keyword table (FIND_TOKEN), with spaces:\n- before it, when FIND_TOKEN gives carry (a token $D8 or over whose spelling starts with a\n  letter or digit: AND, THEN, TO, the commands) and FLAGS bit 0 is clear (the last thing\n  printed was not a space, nor the start of a line);\n- after it, for a token $C0 or over whose spelling ends in a letter, a digit or '$' (not RND,\n  INKEY$, PI, nor ** <= and the like). That space goes through PRINT_CHAR and sets FLAGS\n  bit 0, so a keyword right after it adds no second one.\nObserved: a line holding A <= B AND C THEN PRINT SIN X ** 2 ; INKEY$ ; STR$ 1 ; PI ; RND ;\nA TO B (as tokens) lists as A<=B AND C THEN PRINT SIN X**2;INKEY$;STR$ 1;PI;RND;A TO B.\nChanges: AF, BC.","comment":"The token waits"},
        "2383": {"comment":"No carry: no space before it"},
        "2385": {"comment":"Nor after a space"},
        "2391": {"comment":"A space first"},
        "2393": {"synopsis":"Prints the spelling a character at a time, up to its byte with bit 7; then decides the space\nafter.","comment":"The next character of the spelling"},
        "2397": {"comment":"Bit 7 marks the last one"},
        "2402": {"comment":"B = the token; A = its last character x 2"},
        "2403": {"comment":"RND, INKEY$, PI ($40-$42): no space after"},
        "2406": {"comment":"Ends in '$' ($0D x 2): a space after"},
        "2410": {"comment":"Ends in a symbol (under $1C): no space"},
        "2413": {"synopsis":"Prints a space after the keyword through PRINT_CHAR, with FLAGS bit 0 set so the next keyword\nadds none before it.","comment":"A space, and FLAGS bit 0: none before the next keyword"},
        "2421": {"synopsis":"BC = the spelling of token A in the keyword table ($0111): codes $C0-$FF (and $80-$BF,\nbit 6 is not looked at) are entry A AND $3F, plus 1; $40-$42 (RND, INKEY$, PI) entries $41-$43.\nThe first entry is a lone '?', returned for codes $43-$7F.\nExit: carry when the token wants a space before it: $D8 or over (operators and commands) and\nits spelling starting with a letter or digit (A = that character + $E4).\nChanges: AF, BC."},
        "2425": {"comment":"$C0 and up: the entry number from the low 6 bits"},
        "2431": {"synopsis":"Codes $43 and over: the '?' entry. Otherwise skips A + 1 spellings to the token's own.","comment":"$43 and over: the '?' entry, no carry"},
        "2435": {"comment":"Entry A + 1 (entry 0 is '?')"},
        "2437": {"synopsis":"Steps over one spelling (up to its byte with bit 7); B of them.","comment":"Past one spelling: up to its byte with bit 7"},
        "2444": {"comment":"RND, INKEY$, PI: carry set from CP $43, cleared by CCF"},
        "2448": {"comment":"No carry from $18 ($D8, '**') up"},
        "2450": {"synopsis":"Carry inverted: set for tokens $D8 and up.","comment":"Carry: $D8 and up"},
        "2451": {"synopsis":"BC = the spelling; with carry, it stays only when the spelling starts with a letter or digit."},
        "2454": {"comment":"No carry: done"},
        "2455": {"comment":"Carry stays only when the first character is a letter or digit"},
        "2459": {"synopsis":"Makes room for one byte before HL (MAKE_ROOM with BC = 1).\nExit: DE = the new byte."},
        "2462": {"synopsis":"Opens BC bytes of room before HL: checks they fit (TEST_ROOM, report 4 when not), moves\nthe pointer variables above HL up by BC (MOVE_POINTERS) and the memory from HL to STKEND\nup with them (LDDR).\nExit: DE = the last byte of the new room."},
        "2477": {"synopsis":"Adds BC to each of the nine pointer variables from D_FILE to STKEND that point past HL:\nwhat keeps them right when memory is opened or closed at HL. Then sets BC, DE and HL\nup for the block move that follows.","comment":"HL = D_FILE, the first of the nine"},
        "2484": {"synopsis":"MOVE_POINTERS's loop: the next pointer variable, compared with the position on the stack."},
        "2504": {"synopsis":"On to the next pointer variable (A of the nine left)."},
        "2520": {"synopsis":"Finds the first program line numbered HL or more, walking from the start of the program\n($407D).\nExit: HL = that line (or the display file, past the last line); DE = the line before it;\nZ set when the number matched exactly.","comment":"HL = the program's start"},
        "2526": {"synopsis":"Compares each line's number with BC (CMP_LINE_NO): returns at the first not smaller,\nelse steps to the next line (NEXT_ITEM)."},
        "2538": {"synopsis":"Compares the line number at HL (high byte first) with BC: carry when it is smaller, Z when\nthey are equal. The display file's NEWLINE ($76) is greater than any line number, so a\nsearch stops there."},
        "2546": {"synopsis":"Steps over the program line or variable at HL, and falls into LEN_DE_TO_HL.\nExit: HL = this one, DE = the next one, BC = its length in bytes."},
        "2561": {"synopsis":"A number variable: BC = 5 (a FOR variable: 17, value, limit, step and line), then its name."},
        "2564": {"comment":"A FOR variable (carry): 17 bytes"},
        "2568": {"synopsis":"Walks the name, up to the byte whose bit 7 (into carry) is set."},
        "2575": {"synopsis":"A program line: past its number; the length follows."},
        "2576": {"synopsis":"A string or an array (and a line, from NI_LINE): BC = the length word that follows."},
        "2581": {"synopsis":"HL = past the item: + BC."},
        "2583": {"synopsis":"BC = HL - DE; then HL = DE (the start) and DE = the old HL (the end)."},
        "2591": {"synopsis":"Clears the lower part of the screen, the bottom DF_SZ lines (CLS_LINES).","comment":"B = DF_SZ"},
        "2602": {"synopsis":"Clears the whole screen: CLS_LINES with all 24 lines.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL.","comment":"B = 24: every line"},
        "2604": {"synopsis":"Clears the bottom B lines of the screen. Sets the print position to line B, column 33\n(the left edge), then fills from there to the end of the display with spaces when RAMTOP\nis $4D00 or more (CLS_SPACES), or removes the display's contents from there instead\nwhen there is less RAM (CLS_COLLAPSED). Ends with the print position at line B, column 33.","comment":"FLAGS bit 1 clear: PRINT_CHAR prints on the screen"},
        "2608": {"comment":"C = 33: the left edge"},
        "2611": {"comment":"Print position = line B, column 33; DF_CC points at it"},
        "2615": {"comment":"A = the high byte of RAMTOP"},
        "2620": {"comment":"Below $4D00 (under 3.25K of RAM): no spaces, a collapsed display"},
        "2622": {"comment":"Bit 7 on S_POSN's line count: the lower-part test (B = DF_SZ) cannot stop the fill; it ends at $8101 (observed)"},
        "2626": {"synopsis":"Prints spaces until the print position has counted down to the end of the display."},
        "2627": {"comment":"Print a space (A = 0)"},
        "2635": {"comment":"Column and line both counted down to the end?"},
        "2639": {"comment":"Print position back to line B, column 33"},
        "2642": {"synopsis":"RAMTOP below $4D00: clears the bottom B lines by collapsing them. Entry: HL = DF_CC, the\nfirst of them; B = their number. An LDIR of B bytes from HL - 1 to HL, overlapping, copies\nthe NEWLINE before them (the line above's, or the display file's first) along, so the B\nlines become bare NEWLINEs; then everything after them up to VARS is removed\n(RECLAIM_DE_HL). The print position stays at their first line, column 33.\nObserved on the 1K model: CLS after PRINT \"AB\" and PRINT \"CD\": B = 24 from D_FILE + 1, the\nLDIR ended at D_FILE + 25, then the 4 bytes up to VARS were reclaimed: the display file is\n25 NEWLINEs again. Clearing the lower part gave B = 2, then B = 1 (CLS_LOWER).\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL.","comment":"DE = the first line to clear"},
        "2644": {"comment":"HL = the NEWLINE before it"},
        "2645": {"comment":"BC = the number of lines"},
        "2648": {"comment":"Copy that NEWLINE along: B bare NEWLINEs, DE past them"},
        "2650": {"comment":"Remove the rest, up to VARS"},
        "2653": {"synopsis":"Removes the bytes from DE up to, not including, HL (RECLAIM)."},
        "2656": {"synopsis":"Removes BC bytes at HL: moves the pointer variables past them down by BC (MOVE_POINTERS)\nand the memory above down with them (LDIR).\nExit: HL = where the bytes were."},
        "2675": {"synopsis":"Reads the edit line's line number. CH_ADD = E_LINE, A = its first character. With FLAGX\nbit 5 set (INPUT's answer) it returns at once with NZ. Otherwise BC = the number at the\nstart of the line, 0 when there is none, with Z set; 10000 or more stops with report C.\nThe conversion uses the calculator stack at MEMBOT ($1548, $158A).","comment":"HL = E_LINE"},
        "2686": {"comment":"INPUT's answer: NZ, no line number"},
        "2690": {"comment":"The calculator stack at MEMBOT for now"},
        "2693": {"comment":"The digits at CH_ADD onto the calculator stack"},
        "2696": {"comment":"BC = that number; carry if too big"},
        "2704": {"comment":"Carry if BC is 10000 or more"},
        "2705": {"synopsis":"Report C: a line number of 10000 or more, or one too big for BC.","comment":"Report C"},
        "2708": {"comment":"Z: a line number, or 0"},
        "2709": {"comment":"Empty calculator stack, MEM = MEMBOT"},
        "2712": {"synopsis":"Prints BC as a decimal number without leading zeros; BC with bit 7 set (a command's PPC,\n$FFFE) prints as 0."},
        "2725": {"synopsis":"Prints the line number at HL (high byte first) as four digits, leading zeros as spaces;\nHL moves on to its low byte."},
        "2733": {"synopsis":"PRINT_LINE_NO's digits for the number in HL; E = 0 prints leading zeros as spaces, $FF\nnot at all (PRINT_BC_NUM's entry)."},
        "2751": {"synopsis":"The units digit, always printed; PRINT_BC_NUM jumps here with A = 0 for BC with bit 15\nset, which prints as 0."},
        "2757": {"synopsis":"Returns to its caller only when running. When checking it drops the return address, so\nthe caller itself returns: how PRINT's output steps are skipped during the syntax check."},
        "2763": {"synopsis":"LPRINT: FLAGS bit 1 set, so the output goes to the printer buffer, then PRINT.","comment":"FLAGS bit 1: printer output"},
        "2767": {"synopsis":"PRINT: prints its items, separated by ',' (on to the next half of the line, PRINT_COMMA)\nor ';' (nothing between). An item is AT line,column; TAB column; or an expression, printed\nas a number (PRINT_FP) or a string. Ends with a NEWLINE unless the last thing is a\nseparator. When checking only the syntax is followed: everything that prints goes through\nRUN_ONLY.\nRuns of PRINT 1,2 and PRINT \"A\";\"B\" printed '1' and '2' 16 columns apart, and 'AB'.","comment":"A = the first character"},
        "2768": {"comment":"Nothing: just a NEWLINE"},
        "2773": {"synopsis":"One PRINT item: a separator (PRINT_SEP), AT line,column (TWO_PARAMS, AT_POS), TAB\n(PRINT_TAB) or an expression (PRINT_EXPR).","comment":"',' gives 0 with carry clear, ';' 0 with carry set"},
        "2777": {"comment":"A separator"},
        "2779": {"comment":"AT ($C1)?"},
        "2784": {"comment":"The line: a number"},
        "2787": {"comment":"Then ','"},
        "2793": {"comment":"The column: a number"},
        "2796": {"comment":"Checking: on to the next item"},
        "2799": {"comment":"Line on top, column below"},
        "2802": {"comment":"B = the line, C = the column"},
        "2805": {"comment":"Move the print position there"},
        "2810": {"synopsis":"TAB column: column MOD 32, relative to the printer buffer when printing there; the print\nposition moves there (on the next line when it is already past).","comment":"TAB ($C2)?"},
        "2815": {"comment":"The column: a number"},
        "2821": {"comment":"A = it; report B if negative"},
        "2827": {"comment":"Column MOD 32"},
        "2830": {"comment":"To the printer?"},
        "2836": {"comment":"Printer: relative to PR_CC; a full buffer is printed first"},
        "2846": {"synopsis":"The screen's column test (also the printer's, from above): past the column already, then\nthe next line.","comment":"Screen: past the column already? Then the next line"},
        "2856": {"comment":"Move the print position there"},
        "2859": {"comment":"FLAGS bit 0: no space before a keyword next"},
        "2865": {"synopsis":"An expression: evaluated (SCAN_EXPR) and printed (PRINT_VALUE).","comment":"An expression"},
        "2868": {"comment":"Print its value"},
        "2871": {"synopsis":"After an item: a separator goes on to the next item; otherwise the statement ends with a\nNEWLINE (PRINT_NEWLINE).","comment":"A = the character after the item"},
        "2876": {"comment":"A separator: on to the next item"},
        "2878": {"comment":"Otherwise the statement ends here ..."},
        "2881": {"comment":"... with a NEWLINE"},
        "2884": {"synopsis":"',' moves to the next half of the line (PRINT_COMMA); ';' does not. A separator at the end\nof the statement means no NEWLINE; otherwise the next item.","comment":"',': on to the next half of the line"},
        "2888": {"comment":"A separator at the end: no NEWLINE"},
        "2891": {"comment":"The next item"},
        "2894": {"synopsis":"When checking, drops the caller and goes on to the next item (PRINT_AFTER); when running,\nreturns."},
        "2901": {"synopsis":"Prints the value the expression left: a number (PRINT_FP), or a string character by\ncharacter. Keywords inside the string are spelt out, and the \"\" token prints as one quote.\nX_PTR serves as the pointer along the string.","comment":"Checking: nothing to print"},
        "2904": {"comment":"A number?"},
        "2908": {"comment":"A string: DE = its text, BC = its length"},
        "2913": {"comment":"A number: print it"},
        "2916": {"synopsis":"The \"\" token inside a string prints as one quote.","comment":"The \"\" token: print one quote"},
        "2918": {"synopsis":"Prints one character of the string, then the next (PRINT_STR_NEXT)."},
        "2919": {"synopsis":"DE = the next character (from X_PTR, which the string's printing moves along)."},
        "2923": {"synopsis":"Prints the string's BC characters: ordinary characters, the \"\" token as a quote, keywords\nspelt out (PRINT_TOKEN).","comment":"No more characters?"},
        "2929": {"comment":"X_PTR = the next character"},
        "2933": {"comment":"A character: print it"},
        "2937": {"comment":"$C0, the \"\" token"},
        "2942": {"comment":"A keyword: spell it out"},
        "2948": {"synopsis":"Prints a NEWLINE (when running)."},
        "2955": {"synopsis":"',': a space, then on to column 16 when the print position is in the left half of the\nline, or the end of the line (so the next item goes on the next line) when it is in the\nright half. The printer's position is taken from PR_CC.","comment":"Checking: nothing"},
        "2962": {"comment":"A space first"},
        "2964": {"comment":"C = the column, counting down from 33"},
        "2975": {"comment":"The printer's column instead"},
        "2980": {"synopsis":"The comma's test: column count 17 or more (the left half) or not.","comment":"In the left half (17 or more)?"},
        "2985": {"comment":"Right half: to the end of the line"},
        "2987": {"synopsis":"Moves the print position to column count C (SET_POS_OUT).","comment":"Move the print position (column count C)"},
        "2991": {"synopsis":"PLOT and UNPLOT x,y (both syntax entries name this routine).\nTakes y, then x, off the calculator stack (TWO_PARAMS: over 255 is report B; a negative\nnumber counts as its size, so PLOT -3,1 plots 3,1) and stores them in COORDS.\nA character cell holds 2 x 2 points; x runs 0-63 from the left, y 0-43 from the bottom.\nThe cell is line (43 - y) / 2, column x / 2: y over 43 is report B here, x over 63 report B\nin AT_POS, which also moves the print position to the cell (report 5 in the lower part).\nThe point is one bit of a quadrant mask: 1 top left, 2 top right, 4 bottom left,\n8 bottom right. The cell's character becomes a mask (codes $00-$07 are their own mask,\n$80-$87 the code XOR $8F, any other character counts as empty), the point is set (PLOT)\nor cleared (UNPLOT), and the mask is turned back into a character the same way and\nprinted with RST $10, so the print position ends one cell past the point.\nPLOT and UNPLOT are told apart by T_ADDR, still inside the command's syntax entry\n($0C9C for PLOT, $0CA2 for UNPLOT, observed): below $0C9E, where UNPLOT's starts, is PLOT.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL; COORDS, S_POSN, DF_CC.","comment":"B = y, C = x"},
        "2994": {"comment":"COORDS: x in the low byte, y in the high"},
        "3000": {"comment":"43 - y"},
        "3001": {"comment":"y over 43: report B"},
        "3005": {"comment":"A = 1: the top left point"},
        "3007": {"comment":"B = the line, (43 - y) / 2; carry when y is even: the bottom row"},
        "3011": {"comment":"Bottom row: 4"},
        "3013": {"synopsis":"C = the column (x / 2); an odd x moves the point's bit to the right column (2 or 8).","comment":"C = the column, x / 2; carry when x is odd: the right column"},
        "3017": {"comment":"Right column: 2 or 8"},
        "3018": {"synopsis":"The print position to the cell (AT_POS), and B = the mask of the block graphic there (0 for\nany other character).","comment":"Keep the point's bit"},
        "3019": {"comment":"Print position to the cell; HL = it in the display file, B = 0"},
        "3022": {"comment":"The character in the cell"},
        "3024": {"comment":"Codes $00-$07 and $80-$87 rotate to under $10: a block graphic"},
        "3026": {"comment":"Any other character: B stays 0, an empty cell"},
        "3028": {"comment":"The code again; carry when it is inverse"},
        "3031": {"comment":"Inverse: the code XOR $8F is its mask"},
        "3033": {"synopsis":"B = the cell's mask.","comment":"B = the cell's mask"},
        "3034": {"synopsis":"PLOT or UNPLOT from T_ADDR's low byte against $9E: UNPLOT clears the point's bit.","comment":"Where UNPLOT's syntax entry starts"},
        "3040": {"comment":"T_ADDR's low byte - $9E: negative for PLOT"},
        "3045": {"comment":"UNPLOT: clear the point's bit"},
        "3049": {"synopsis":"PLOT: sets the point's bit."},
        "3050": {"comment":"PLOT: set the point's bit"},
        "3051": {"synopsis":"The mask back to a character: 0-7 as it is, 8 and up XOR $8F (an inverse code).","comment":"Mask 8 or more (bottom right set): the code is the mask XOR $8F, inverse"},
        "3057": {"synopsis":"Prints the character in the cell (RST $10, between two EXX); the print position moves past it.","comment":"RST $10 between two EXX (PRINT_CHAR itself starts with an EXX)"},
        "3058": {"comment":"Print the new character in the cell"},
        "3061": {"synopsis":"Takes two numbers off the calculator stack (PARAM_TO_A each): B = the top one, C = the one\nbelow, D and E their signs (1 or $FF). Report B when one is over 255."},
        "3074": {"synopsis":"A = the size of the number on the calculator stack (FP_TO_A); report B when it is over\n255. C = 1 when it is positive, $FF when negative."},
        "3086": {"synopsis":"SCROLL (class 0, from the syntax table): moves the upper part of the screen up one line.\nSets the print position to column 0 of the lower part's first line (SET_PRINT_POS with\nline count DF_SZ, column count 33), opens one byte there (MAKE_ROOM_ONE) and copies into\nit the NEWLINE before it: an empty line at the bottom of the upper part. DF_CC stays on\nit (MAKE_ROOM moves only pointers past the room) and S_POSN's line count goes up one to\nmatch. Then the top line, D_FILE + 1 to its NEWLINE inclusive, is reclaimed: printing\ncontinues at the start of the new empty line (observed: PRINT \"A\": PRINT \"B\": SCROLL:\nPRINT \"C\" leaves B on line 0 and C on line 21).\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL; S_POSN, DF_CC.","comment":"B = DF_SZ: the line count of the lower part's first line"},
        "3089": {"comment":"C = 33: column 0"},
        "3091": {"comment":"DF_CC = the start of that line"},
        "3094": {"comment":"One byte of room there: DE = it, HL = the byte before"},
        "3097": {"comment":"The NEWLINE ending the line above, copied into the new byte"},
        "3099": {"comment":"S_POSN's line count + 1: the print position is the new empty line"},
        "3102": {"comment":"HL = DE = D_FILE + 1, the top line's first byte"},
        "3108": {"comment":"Past the top line's NEWLINE (A = $76; BC = 0 after the LDDR: no limit)"},
        "3110": {"comment":"Reclaim the top line, its NEWLINE included"},
        "3113": {"synopsis":"Syntax offsets: one byte for each command keyword, $E1 (LPRINT) to $FF (COPY). The\ncommand's syntax entry is at this byte's address plus its value (SCAN_STATEMENT)."},
        "3144": {"synopsis":"Syntax entries: each command's operands, read by the syntax loop (SYNTAX_ITEM). Bytes 0-6\nare operand classes (CLASS_TABLE); $0B and up are characters the line must have ('=',\n',', TO, THEN). Classes 0, 3 and 5 end an entry, followed by the address of the command's\nroutine; class 2 ends LET's. The entries, decoded:\nLET 1 '=' 2 | GOTO 6 0 $0E81 | IF 6 THEN 5 $0DAB | GOSUB 6 0 $0EB5 | STOP 0 $0CDC\nRETURN 0 $0ED8 | FOR 4 '=' 6 TO 6 5 $0DB9 | NEXT 4 0 $0E2E | PRINT 5 $0ACF\nINPUT 1 0 $0EE9 | DIM 5 $1409 | REM 5 $0D6A | NEW 0 $03C3 | RUN 3 $0EAF | LIST 3 $0730\nPOKE 6 ',' 6 0 $0E92 | RAND 3 $0E6C | LOAD 5 $0340 | SAVE 5 $02F6 | CONT 0 $0E7C\nCLEAR 0 $149A | CLS 0 $0A2A | PLOT 6 ',' 6 0 $0BAF | UNPLOT 6 ',' 6 0 $0BAF\nSCROLL 0 $0C0E | PAUSE 6 0 $0F32 | SLOW 0 $0F2B | FAST 0 $0F23 | COPY 0 $0869\nLPRINT 5 $0ACB | LLIST 3 $072C"},
        "3258": {"synopsis":"Checks the edit line's syntax: FLAGS = $01 (bit 7 clear: check, do not run), then the line\nnumber (EDIT_LINE_NO), then SCAN_LINE. A syntax error stops with RST $08, X_PTR at it.","comment":"FLAGS = $01: check only"},
        "3265": {"synopsis":"Checks or runs (FLAGS bit 7 set: run) the statement at CH_ADD. Empties the calculator\nstack and ERR_NR first. INPUT's answer is taken as an expression of the type INPUT asked\nfor (CLASS_2_TYPE); STOP as the answer is report D when run. Anything else is a statement\n(SCAN_STATEMENT).","comment":"Empty calculator stack, MEM = MEMBOT"},
        "3271": {"comment":"ERR_NR = $FF: no error"},
        "3276": {"comment":"FLAGX bit 5: INPUT's answer"},
        "3280": {"comment":"STOP as INPUT's answer?"},
        "3282": {"comment":"A = FLAGX: bit 6 is the type INPUT asked for"},
        "3283": {"comment":"Not STOP: take the answer as an expression of that type"},
        "3286": {"comment":"STOP: report D, but only when running"},
        "3292": {"synopsis":"STOP: report 9. The syntax table's routine for STOP."},
        "3294": {"synopsis":"Checks or runs one statement at CH_ADD, through the syntax tables.\nIts first character must be a command keyword ($E1-$FF), else report C. The keyword's\nsyntax entry (from the offsets at $0C29) is read item by item (SYNTAX_ITEM): a character\nmust come next in the line; a class code runs its class routine (CLASS_TABLE), which checks\nan operand, and when running evaluates it. The classes that end an entry jump to the\ncommand's routine when running. An empty statement (NEWLINE) returns at once.","comment":"A = the first character"},
        "3297": {"comment":"NEWLINE: nothing to do"},
        "3301": {"comment":"On past the keyword"},
        "3303": {"comment":"Keyword $E1 (LPRINT) and up: a command"},
        "3305": {"comment":"Not a command: report C"},
        "3308": {"comment":"The command's syntax offset"},
        "3313": {"comment":"HL = its syntax entry"},
        "3316": {"synopsis":"The syntax loop: every class routine that returns comes back here for the entry's next\nitem (SYNTAX_ITEM at T_ADDR).","comment":"HL = T_ADDR, the next item of the entry"},
        "3319": {"synopsis":"Reads the syntax entry's next item at HL and acts on it; T_ADDR follows the entry.","comment":"A = the item"},
        "3321": {"comment":"T_ADDR = the item after it"},
        "3327": {"comment":"Come back to SYNTAX_NEXT afterwards"},
        "3329": {"comment":"$0B and up: a character the line must have"},
        "3333": {"comment":"A class: its offset in CLASS_TABLE"},
        "3340": {"comment":"HL = the class routine"},
        "3341": {"comment":"Run it when this returns"},
        "3342": {"comment":"A = the current character, for the class routine"},
        "3344": {"synopsis":"A character the syntax entry requires ('=', ',', TO, THEN): it must be the next one in\nthe line, else report C.","comment":"A = the current character"},
        "3345": {"comment":"The one the entry wants?"},
        "3348": {"comment":"Yes: past it"},
        "3350": {"synopsis":"CLASS_TABLE: one byte for each operand class 0-6; the class routine is at this byte's\naddress plus its value: 0 CLASS_0, 1 CLASS_1, 2 CLASS_2, 3 CLASS_3, 4 CLASS_4,\n5 CLASS_5, 6 CLASS_6."},
        "3357": {"synopsis":"The end of a statement. When running it returns, and the command runs (RUN_ROUTINE).\nWhen only checking, the line must end here (END_OF_LINE): the check of the statement is\nthen over, and it returns past the command's routine.","comment":"Checking only?"},
        "3360": {"comment":"Running: back to run the command"},
        "3361": {"comment":"Checking: drop the return to the command's routine"},
        "3362": {"synopsis":"Checking: the statement must end with the line (NEWLINE), else report C.","comment":"The statement must end with the line"},
        "3366": {"synopsis":"To REPORT_C.","comment":"Report C"},
        "3368": {"synopsis":"Class 3, an optional number (RUN, LIST, RAND, LLIST): an expression, or nothing, which is\n0 when running (OPTIONAL_NUM); then the statement ends (CLASS_0).","comment":"Nothing more in the line?"},
        "3373": {"synopsis":"Class 0: no more operands. The statement must end here (STATEMENT_END); when running, the\ncommand's routine runs (RUN_ROUTINE).","comment":"Z: check the statement's end"},
        "3374": {"synopsis":"Class 5: the command checks its own operands (PRINT, IF's statement, FOR, DIM, REM, LOAD,\nSAVE, LPRINT). Its routine runs at once, when checking and when running.","comment":"Drop the return to the syntax loop"},
        "3378": {"synopsis":"Jumps to the command's routine, whose address follows the class byte at T_ADDR."},
        "3384": {"comment":"BC = the address after the class byte"},
        "3386": {"synopsis":"Jumps to BC (PUSH BC, RET).","comment":"Jump to BC"},
        "3388": {"synopsis":"Class 1, a variable to assign to (LET, INPUT; CLASS_4 joins at CLASS_VAR for FOR and NEXT):\nlooks it up (LOOK_VARS) and leaves DEST, STRLEN and FLAGX for LET_ASSIGN.\n- Not there (carry, only when running): FLAGX = $02 (bit 1, a new variable) and DEST = the\n  name in the line, which LET_ASSIGN copies; a name followed by '(' is report 2 instead.\n- A number: DEST = the byte before its 5 bytes, the first byte of a single-letter name or\n  the last character of a longer one (SUBSCRIPTS gives the same for an array element).\n- A string, slice or string array element (through SUBSCRIPTS): when running its details\n  come off the calculator stack (STK_FETCH): DEST = its text, STRLEN = its length, and\n  STK_STRING's first byte goes into FLAGX bit 0 (1 for a whole simple string).\nSTRLEN is left as BC was for a number. Returns through REM_CMD's RET.\nObserved (DEST, FLAGX at $0D6A): LET A=1, new: the name in the line, $02; LET A=2: VARS + 0\n($66), $00; LET AB=4: the stored $A7 (B with bit 7), $00; LET A$=\"HI\", new: in the line, $02;\nLET A$=\"X\": the text, STRLEN 2, $01; LET A$(1)=\"Z\": the text, STRLEN 1, $00; LET Q(1)=1\nwith no Q: report 2.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL; DEST, STRLEN, FLAGX.","comment":"Find the variable; carry when it is not there"},
        "3391": {"synopsis":"CLASS_1 after LOOK_VARS; also class 4's way in. FLAGX = 0; a missing variable is new (bit 1)\nor report 2.","comment":"FLAGX = 0"},
        "3397": {"comment":"Not there: FLAGX bit 1, a new variable"},
        "3401": {"comment":"No '(' after it: a new simple variable"},
        "3403": {"synopsis":"Report 2: a variable that is not there (CLASS_VAR; OP_ALNUM).","comment":"Report 2"},
        "3405": {"synopsis":"Subscripts or a slice (SUBSCRIPTS) for Z; a string's details into DEST, STRLEN and FLAGX bit 0.","comment":"A string, an array, or '(': subscripts or a slice"},
        "3408": {"comment":"FLAGS bit 6: a number"},
        "3414": {"comment":"A string: A = 0 when checking"},
        "3418": {"comment":"Running: A = the whole-string byte, DE = the text, BC = its length"},
        "3424": {"comment":"FLAGX bit 0: a whole simple string"},
        "3426": {"comment":"HL = the text"},
        "3427": {"synopsis":"STRLEN = BC, DEST = HL; then class 1 returns through REM_CMD's RET.","comment":"STRLEN = BC"},
        "3431": {"comment":"DEST = HL, the variable"},
        "3434": {"synopsis":"REM: a bare RET. Class 5 has dropped the syntax loop, so this returns from the statement\nand the rest of the line is ignored."},
        "3435": {"synopsis":"Class 2, LET's '= expression': evaluates the expression (SCAN_EXPR). Its type (FLAGS bit 6)\nmust be the variable's, as class 1 left it in FLAGS, else report C. When running, the value\nis assigned (LET_ASSIGN, $1321); when checking, the statement must end here.","comment":"Drop the return to the syntax loop"},
        "3436": {"comment":"A = FLAGS: bit 6 is the variable's type"},
        "3439": {"synopsis":"Evaluates the expression; its type must match A's bit 6 (FLAGS, or FLAGX for INPUT's\nanswer), else report C. Running: LET_ASSIGN; checking: the statement must end.","comment":"Also entered with FLAGX, for INPUT's answer"},
        "3440": {"comment":"Evaluate the expression"},
        "3444": {"comment":"BC = LET_ASSIGN"},
        "3451": {"comment":"Bit 6: the same type?"},
        "3453": {"comment":"No: report C"},
        "3455": {"comment":"Running: assign it"},
        "3459": {"comment":"Checking: the statement must end here"},
        "3461": {"synopsis":"Class 4, the variable of FOR or NEXT: looks it up (LOOK_VARS). Its kind (C) must be one FOR\nand NEXT can use (C OR $9F = $FF), else report C; then as class 1 (CLASS_VAR).","comment":"Find the variable"},
        "3466": {"comment":"Bits 5 and 6 of C both set?"},
        "3469": {"comment":"No: report C"},
        "3474": {"synopsis":"Class 6, a number: evaluates an expression (SCAN_EXPR); report C unless it is numeric\n(FLAGS bit 6 set).","comment":"Evaluate the expression"},
        "3477": {"comment":"FLAGS bit 6: a number"},
        "3482": {"synopsis":"Stops with report C (RST $08): the error a line that breaks the syntax gets."},
        "3484": {"synopsis":"An optional number: an expression (CLASS_6) unless the line ends here; then nothing when\nchecking, 0 on the calculator stack when running.","comment":"Something there: a number"},
        "3486": {"comment":"Checking: nothing to do"},
        "3490": {"comment":"Running: 0 on the calculator stack"},
        "3494": {"synopsis":"Z set when only checking syntax (FLAGS bit 7 clear), NZ when running."},
        "3499": {"synopsis":"IF: the statement after THEN is checked or run as a statement of its own (SCAN_STATEMENT).\nWhen running, only if the condition (the number class 6 left on the calculator stack) is not\n0; otherwise the rest of the line is skipped.","comment":"Checking?"},
        "3504": {"comment":"Running: take the condition off the stack"},
        "3507": {"comment":"Its first byte: 0 for the value 0"},
        "3509": {"comment":"False: skip the rest of the line"},
        "3510": {"synopsis":"The statement after THEN (SCAN_STATEMENT): when checking, or when the condition was true.","comment":"True, or checking: the statement after THEN"},
        "3513": {"synopsis":"FOR, after 'variable = number TO number' (classes 4, 6, 6, then 5 for this routine):\nan optional STEP is read here (1 when there is none).\nWhen running, the variable gets the start value (LET_ASSIGN), and becomes a FOR variable:\nbit 7 of its first byte set, and after its value the limit, the step, and the line to go\nback to, PPC + 1 (12 bytes made for them the first time). MEM is pointed at it, so the\ncalculator's memories 0, 1 and 2 are its value, limit and step.\nWhen the start is already past the limit, the loop is not run: the program goes on after the\nNEXT for this variable (FOR_SKIP_LOOP).\nA run of 10 FOR I=1 TO 3 / 20 PRINT I / 30 NEXT I / 40 PRINT \"E\" printed 1 2 3 E and left I\nas $EE, then 4, 3, 1 (5 bytes each) and 11; with FOR I=5 TO 3 it printed only E.","comment":"STEP?"},
        "3517": {"comment":"Past STEP"},
        "3518": {"comment":"The step: a number"},
        "3521": {"comment":"The statement ends here; checking returns"},
        "3526": {"synopsis":"No STEP: the statement must end; running, a step of 1 goes on the calculator stack.","comment":"No STEP: the statement ends here"},
        "3529": {"comment":"Running: a step of 1"},
        "3532": {"synopsis":"Running: the value is assigned (LET_ASSIGN) and MEM points at the variable; it becomes a FOR\nvariable (bit 7), with 12 more bytes when it was not one.","comment":"Reorder the stack to limit, step, value: the value on top for LET"},
        "3539": {"comment":"The variable = the value; HL = its 5 bytes"},
        "3542": {"comment":"MEM = the variable: memories 0, 1, 2 are its value, limit, step"},
        "3546": {"comment":"A = its first byte"},
        "3547": {"comment":"Bit 7: a FOR variable"},
        "3552": {"comment":"HL = past the value"},
        "3553": {"comment":"Already a FOR variable?"},
        "3556": {"comment":"No: 12 bytes more, for the limit, the step and the line"},
        "3562": {"synopsis":"Copies the limit and step into the variable, then PPC + 1, the line NEXT goes back to; then\nthe test whether the loop runs at all."},
        "3563": {"comment":"The step and limit off the stack; their bytes stay where they were"},
        "3571": {"comment":"Copy the limit and step into the variable"},
        "3573": {"comment":"HL = PPC"},
        "3577": {"comment":"PPC + 1: NEXT goes back to the line after this one"},
        "3580": {"comment":"Stored after the step"},
        "3581": {"comment":"Past the limit already?"},
        "3584": {"comment":"No: the loop runs"},
        "3585": {"comment":"A command: nothing to skip"},
        "3590": {"comment":"B = the variable's letter (its kind byte, bit 6 cleared)"},
        "3595": {"comment":"Look for its NEXT from NXTLIN on"},
        "3598": {"synopsis":"The loop does not run: looks from NXTLIN for the NEXT of this variable."},
        "3599": {"comment":"The end of the program?"},
        "3604": {"comment":"HL = this line, DE = the next one"},
        "3611": {"comment":"CH_ADD = the line's text"},
        "3615": {"comment":"NEXT?"},
        "3621": {"comment":"The letter after NEXT"},
        "3623": {"comment":"This loop's variable?"},
        "3626": {"synopsis":"NXTLIN = the line after that NEXT (or the program's end): the run goes on there.","comment":"NXTLIN = the line after that NEXT, or the end"},
        "3630": {"synopsis":"NEXT: adds the step to the FOR variable (class 4 found it). Within the limit, the program\njumps back to the line stored in the variable (GOTO_LINE); past it, it goes on after NEXT.\nReport 2 when there is no such variable, report 1 when it is not a FOR variable.","comment":"FLAGX bit 1: no such variable"},
        "3634": {"comment":"Report 2"},
        "3637": {"comment":"HL = DEST, the variable"},
        "3640": {"comment":"Not a FOR variable: report 1"},
        "3645": {"comment":"MEM = its value"},
        "3648": {"comment":"value = value + step"},
        "3655": {"comment":"Past the limit?"},
        "3658": {"comment":"Yes: the loop is over"},
        "3662": {"comment":"HL = the line number after the value, limit and step (15 bytes)"},
        "3670": {"comment":"Jump there"},
        "3672": {"synopsis":"Stops with report 1: NEXT for a variable that is not a FOR variable."},
        "3674": {"synopsis":"Is the FOR variable past its limit? With MEM at the variable: value > limit for a step of\n0 or more, value < limit for a negative step (the operands are swapped then).\nExit: carry set when it is past (LOOP_PAST, after the end-calc at $0E69).","comment":"Limit, value, step; is the step negative?"},
        "3687": {"comment":"Within the limit: carry clear"},
        "3689": {"synopsis":"$34, an end-calc: where LOOP_TEST's jump-true goes when the variable is past its limit,\nso the calculator stops here and LOOP_PAST runs. The end-calc at $0E66 is reached only when\nit is not past."},
        "3690": {"synopsis":"Past the limit: carry set (after the end-calc at $0E69).","comment":"Past the limit: carry set"},
        "3692": {"synopsis":"RAND: SEED = the number; RAND 0, or RAND alone, takes FRAMES instead.","comment":"BC = the number"},
        "3699": {"comment":"0: BC = FRAMES"},
        "3703": {"synopsis":"SEED = BC.","comment":"SEED = BC"},
        "3708": {"synopsis":"CONT: GOTO_LINE with OLDPPC, the line REPORT kept for it.","comment":"HL = OLDPPC"},
        "3713": {"synopsis":"GOTO: BC = the line number (FIND_INT), then GOTO_LINE."},
        "3718": {"synopsis":"Jumps to line HL: NXTLIN = the first line numbered HL or more (FIND_LINE). $F000 or more\nis report B. GOTO, CONT and NEXT end here.","comment":"Too big for a line number?"},
        "3723": {"comment":"Find the line"},
        "3726": {"comment":"NXTLIN = it: the program goes on there"},
        "3730": {"synopsis":"POKE address, value: A = the value (FP_TO_A), -255 to 255, a negative one taken as 256\nminus its size; report B outside that. Then BC = the address (FIND_INT), and the byte is\nwritten unless an error has occurred.","comment":"A = the value; carry if too big"},
        "3733": {"comment":"Report B"},
        "3735": {"comment":"Positive: as it is"},
        "3737": {"comment":"Negative: 256 - its size"},
        "3739": {"synopsis":"BC = the address (FIND_INT); A is written there unless an error was found (ERR_NR)."},
        "3740": {"comment":"BC = the address"},
        "3744": {"comment":"ERR_NR bit 7 clear: an error, no write"},
        "3749": {"comment":"Write the byte"},
        "3751": {"synopsis":"BC = the number on the calculator stack (FP_TO_BC); report B when it is out of range."},
        "3757": {"synopsis":"Stops with report B: a number out of range."},
        "3759": {"synopsis":"RUN: GOTO the line (0 when none: class 3), then CLEAR (VARS_RESET).","comment":"GOTO"},
        "3762": {"comment":"CLEAR: empty the variables"},
        "3765": {"synopsis":"GOSUB: keeps the line to return to on the machine stack, then jumps as GOTO does.\nThe return line, PPC + 1, goes where the run loop's return address ($0676) was; that address\ngoes above it, and ERR_SP moves down to it. An error still returns to the run loop, and the\nreturn lines pile up below it, with the $3E00 word under RAMTOP at the bottom.\nThen GOTO_CMD, and a check that 6 more bytes fit (report 4 when they do not).\nA run of 10 GOSUB 100 / 20 PRINT \"B\" / 30 GOTO 200 / 100 PRINT \"A\" / 110 RETURN printed A B;\nat RETURN, SP = ERR_SP = $7FFA held $0676, $000B (line 11) and $3E00.","comment":"HL = PPC"},
        "3768": {"comment":"PPC + 1: the line to return to"},
        "3769": {"comment":"It takes the place of the run loop's return address ..."},
        "3770": {"comment":"... which goes above it"},
        "3771": {"comment":"ERR_SP = that: errors still return to the run loop"},
        "3775": {"comment":"GOTO the line"},
        "3778": {"synopsis":"BC = 6, and on into TEST_ROOM: the GOSUB stack's new entry must fit.","comment":"6 more bytes must fit (TEST_ROOM)"},
        "3781": {"synopsis":"Checks that BC more bytes fit: STKEND + BC must stay at least 36 bytes below the stack\npointer. Returns when they fit; otherwise stops with error code 3 (report 4).","comment":"HL = STKEND + BC"},
        "3795": {"synopsis":"Stops with error code 3 (report 4): no room.","comment":"Error code 3: no room"},
        "3800": {"synopsis":"RETURN: takes the last GOSUB's line off the machine stack, moves ERR_SP up to the run\nloop's return address, and jumps to the line (GOTO_LINE). The $3E00 word at the bottom (no\nGOSUB) is report 7, with the stack left as it was. A run of RETURN as a command gave 7/0.","comment":"HL = the run loop's return address"},
        "3801": {"comment":"HL = the word below it, which the address replaces"},
        "3803": {"comment":"The $3E00 at the bottom: no GOSUB"},
        "3807": {"comment":"ERR_SP = the run loop's return address again"},
        "3811": {"comment":"Jump to the line"},
        "3813": {"synopsis":"No GOSUB entry: both words go back and it is report 7.","comment":"Put both words back ..."},
        "3815": {"comment":"... and report 7"},
        "3817": {"synopsis":"INPUT variable, after class 1 has found it (DEST; FLAGS bit 6 its type; FLAGX bit 1 when\nit is new). Only in a program: as a command (PPC bit 15 set) it is report 8 (observed: 8/0).\nEmpties the calculator stack at E_LINE (STK_FROM_ELINE), so the edit line is built afresh\nthere; sets FLAGX bit 5 (waiting for INPUT) and bit 6 to the variable's type. The new edit\nline (RST $30) is the cursor and a NEWLINE for a number, '\"' cursor '\"' NEWLINE for a\nstring. T_ADDR = S_POSN, so the answer's run prints where the program left off. Then it\ndrops its own return (the statement goes no further) and hands over to the line editor\n(EDIT_LINE_SHOW), which stays in L mode while FLAGX bit 5 is set.\nThe answer is an expression of the variable's type, checked as a line (SCAN_LINE: STOP is\nreport D, anything else CLASS_2_TYPE); a wrong type, or an empty answer, gets the error\nmarker and INPUT goes on waiting. KEY_NEWLINE then runs it (INPUT_ANSWER) at the print\nposition from T_ADDR, assigns it, and the program goes on at NXTLIN; the run loop clears\nFLAGX bit 5 ($0682).\nObserved: waiting for INPUT A, FLAGX $62 and the edit line $7F $76; for INPUT A$, $22 and\n$0B $7F $0B $76. PRINT \"Q\"; INPUT A, answer 2+3, then PRINT A*2 printed Q10 on one line;\nanswer B*2 with B = 7 gave 14; answer Z (no such variable) report 2/10; STOP D/10; an empty\nanswer and \"X\" for a number left the inverse S and INPUT waiting.","comment":"PPC bit 15: run as a command?"},
        "3821": {"comment":"Yes: report 8"},
        "3823": {"comment":"Empty calculator stack at E_LINE: the edit line is built afresh"},
        "3829": {"comment":"FLAGX bit 5: waiting for INPUT"},
        "3831": {"comment":"FLAGX bit 6 clear for now"},
        "3833": {"comment":"A = FLAGS bit 6: the variable's type"},
        "3838": {"comment":"Room for the cursor and NEWLINE ..."},
        "3841": {"comment":"A number: no quotes"},
        "3843": {"comment":"... and two quotes for a string"},
        "3845": {"synopsis":"FLAGX bit 6 = the type; the new edit line made at E_LINE (RST $30), with quotes for a string.","comment":"FLAGX bit 6 = the type"},
        "3847": {"comment":"The room at E_LINE: DE = its first byte, HL = its last"},
        "3848": {"comment":"NEWLINE at the end"},
        "3853": {"comment":"C = 2 (a number): carry after two RRCAs"},
        "3857": {"comment":"A string: quotes ($0B) around the cursor"},
        "3860": {"synopsis":"The cursor in the new edit line; T_ADDR = S_POSN; the return is dropped and the editor\ntakes over (EDIT_LINE_SHOW).","comment":"The byte before the NEWLINE (or the closing quote)"},
        "3861": {"comment":"The cursor"},
        "3866": {"comment":"T_ADDR = S_POSN, the print position"},
        "3869": {"comment":"Drop the return: the statement goes no further"},
        "3870": {"comment":"The line editor takes over"},
        "3873": {"synopsis":"Report 8: INPUT as a command (observed: 8/0)."},
        "3875": {"synopsis":"FAST: stops SLOW mode (SET_FAST) and clears CDFLAG bit 6, so it is no longer asked for:\nthe picture is shown only while the machine waits for a key.\nRun: CDFLAG $00 after FAST.","comment":"Stop the display"},
        "3878": {"comment":"CDFLAG bit 6 clear: FAST mode from now on"},
        "3883": {"synopsis":"SLOW: CDFLAG bit 6 set, SLOW mode asked for, then SLOW_FAST, which starts it when the\nhardware gives display NMIs.\nRun: CDFLAG $C0 after SLOW (bits 7 and 6), as after start-up.","comment":"CDFLAG bit 6: SLOW mode asked for"},
        "3887": {"comment":"Start it, if the NMIs work"},
        "3890": {"synopsis":"PAUSE n: shows the picture for n frames, or until a key is pressed. SLOW mode is stopped,\nand the frame code entered at FRAMES_COUNT with n in HL: it counts FRAMES' low 15 bits down\nand returns when they run out (bit 15 clear) or a key is read. A PAUSE of 32768 or more has\nbit 15 set and ends only with a key. Then FRAMES' high byte is set back to $FF, SLOW mode\nrestarts if it is asked for, and the reading is marked taken (KEY_TAKEN), so the key that\nended the pause does not reach the editor.\nRun: PAUSE 40000 was still counting after 200 frames (FRAMES $9B81); a key ended it, FRAMES\nwent back to $FF20, and the key was not typed.","comment":"BC = n"},
        "3893": {"comment":"Stop SLOW mode"},
        "3896": {"comment":"HL = n: FRAMES' count, bit 15 clear below 32768"},
        "3898": {"comment":"Frames until the count runs out or a key comes"},
        "3901": {"comment":"FRAMES' high byte $FF: bit 15 set again"},
        "3905": {"comment":"SLOW mode again, if asked for"},
        "3908": {"comment":"The key that ended it is taken"},
        "3910": {"synopsis":"Reads keyboard row $7FFE, whose bit 0 is the SPACE key (BREAK): carry clear when it is\npressed. Falls into KEY_TAKEN."},
        "3915": {"synopsis":"Marks the keyboard reading taken: clears bit 0 of CDFLAG and sets DB_ST to $FF."},
        "3925": {"synopsis":"Evaluates the expression at CH_ADD, by operator priority. Pending operations wait on the\nmachine stack as pairs, B = the priority and C = the operation, above a 0 that marks the\nbottom (pushed here).\nFor each operand (SCAN_OPERAND): RND, PI, INKEY$, a number, a string in quotes, a\nvariable, an expression in brackets, or a function or unary minus that waits for its own\noperand. Then the operator after it (SCAN_OPERATOR): each waiting operation of the same or\nhigher priority is done first (OP_COMPARE), then the new one waits (OP_STACK).\nWhen checking, only the types are followed: FLAGS bit 6 set for a number. When running, the\nvalues go through the calculator stack, and the result is left there.\nExit: A = the character after the expression.","comment":"A = the first character"},
        "3926": {"comment":"B = 0: the bottom of the waiting operations"},
        "3929": {"synopsis":"Reads one operand, or a prefix (a function, NOT or a unary minus) that pushes its\noperation and comes back here for its operand.","comment":"RND?"},
        "3933": {"comment":"Checking: nothing to compute"},
        "3938": {"comment":"Running: BC = SEED"},
        "3945": {"comment":"SEED = ((SEED + 1) x 75 MOD 65537) - 1"},
        "3967": {"comment":"SEED = the new value; a copy stays on the stack"},
        "3971": {"comment":"The copy's exponent"},
        "3975": {"comment":"Minus 16: the copy divided by 65536, a value from 0 to 1"},
        "3978": {"synopsis":"To FIXED_NUMBER: RND's value is on the calculator stack."},
        "3980": {"synopsis":"PI: when running, PI/2 goes on the calculator stack and its exponent + 1 makes it PI;\nthen FIXED_NUMBER.","comment":"PI?"},
        "3989": {"comment":"Running: PI/2 on the stack ..."},
        "3992": {"comment":"... and its exponent + 1: PI"},
        "3993": {"synopsis":"Past the function's name; the operand is a number (SET_NUMERIC). RND and PI end here.","comment":"Past the function's name; the result is a number"},
        "3997": {"synopsis":"INKEY$: the keyboard is read (KEYBOARD, KEY_DECODE); the result is a string of one character,\nthe key's entry in the key table itself, or empty when not exactly one key is down.","comment":"INKEY$?"},
        "4001": {"comment":"Read the keyboard"},
        "4008": {"comment":"A key pressed: DE = its entry in the key tables"},
        "4012": {"comment":"BC = 1 for one key, 0 otherwise"},
        "4015": {"comment":"The string: one character, in the key table itself"},
        "4018": {"synopsis":"Any other operand: a variable or number (OP_ALNUM), '.', unary minus, an expression in\nbrackets, a string in quotes, or a function.","comment":"A digit or a letter?"},
        "4023": {"comment":"'.': a number like .5"},
        "4028": {"comment":"Unary minus: priority 9, operation $D8"},
        "4031": {"comment":"'-'?"},
        "4035": {"comment":"'('?"},
        "4042": {"comment":"The expression in the brackets"},
        "4045": {"comment":"It must end with ')'"},
        "4052": {"comment":"Its type stays as it is"},
        "4054": {"synopsis":"A string in quotes: its text up to the closing quote, with no copy made.","comment":"A string in quotes?"},
        "4061": {"comment":"Where the string's text starts"},
        "4064": {"synopsis":"The next character of the quoted string."},
        "4067": {"synopsis":"The closing quote ends the string: DE = its start, BC = its length (STR_RESULT); a NEWLINE\nbefore it is report C.","comment":"The closing quote?"},
        "4073": {"comment":"BC = the string's length, DE = its start"},
        "4077": {"synopsis":"A string operand: DE = its text, BC = its length. FLAGS bit 6 cleared; when running, its\ndetails go on the calculator stack (STK_STRING).","comment":"FLAGS"},
        "4080": {"comment":"Bit 6 clear: a string"},
        "4082": {"comment":"Running: onto the calculator stack"},
        "4088": {"synopsis":"To SCAN_OPERATOR: the operator after the operand."},
        "4091": {"synopsis":"Report C when the line ends before the closing quote; else the next character.","comment":"NEWLINE before the closing quote: report C"},
        "4095": {"synopsis":"To REPORT_C: a syntax error in the expression."},
        "4098": {"synopsis":"A function keyword, CODE ($C4) to NOT ($D7). NOT waits with priority 4. The others wait\nwith priority 16, operation $D9 + their position, so C AND $3F is the calculator code;\nbit 6 of C is cleared when the operand is a string (CODE, VAL, LEN), and bit 7 when the\nresult is one (STR$, CHR$).","comment":"Below CODE: report C"},
        "4102": {"comment":"NOT: priority 4, operation $EC"},
        "4109": {"comment":"Above NOT: not a function, report C"},
        "4111": {"comment":"Priority 16: functions bind first"},
        "4115": {"comment":"C = the operation"},
        "4120": {"comment":"CODE, VAL, LEN: a string operand"},
        "4122": {"synopsis":"The function's operation waits (PUSH_OPERATION); for A $EA and over (STR$, CHR$) C bit 7\nis cleared: a string result."},
        "4126": {"comment":"STR$, CHR$: a string result"},
        "4128": {"synopsis":"The operation waits on the machine stack (B its priority, C its code) for its operand.","comment":"The operation waits ..."},
        "4130": {"comment":"... for its operand"},
        "4133": {"synopsis":"A digit starts a number (OP_NUMBER); a letter a variable (LOOK_VARS, SUBSCRIPTS: report 2\nwhen it is not there), whose number, when running, is copied onto the calculator stack.","comment":"A digit: a number"},
        "4137": {"comment":"A letter: find the variable"},
        "4140": {"comment":"Not there: report 2"},
        "4149": {"comment":"Running, and a number?"},
        "4158": {"comment":"Copy its value onto the calculator stack"},
        "4167": {"synopsis":"A number in the line. When checking, its digits are read (READ_NUMBER) and its hidden form\nis put after them: a $7E marker and the 5-byte value. When running, the digits are passed\nover and the 5 bytes copied straight onto the calculator stack (NUMBER_RUN).","comment":"Checking?"},
        "4172": {"comment":"Read the digits onto the calculator stack"},
        "4179": {"comment":"6 bytes of room after the digits"},
        "4183": {"comment":"The number marker"},
        "4195": {"comment":"Take the 5-byte value off the calculator stack ..."},
        "4198": {"comment":"... and into the line after the marker"},
        "4202": {"comment":"CH_ADD = after the hidden number"},
        "4207": {"synopsis":"Running: past the digits to the $7E marker; the hidden 5 bytes are copied onto the\ncalculator stack and CH_ADD moves past them.","comment":"Running: past the digits to the marker"},
        "4217": {"comment":"Copy the hidden value onto the calculator stack"},
        "4224": {"comment":"CH_ADD = after it"},
        "4227": {"synopsis":"FLAGS bit 6 set: the operand is a number.","comment":"FLAGS bit 6: the operand is a number"},
        "4231": {"synopsis":"A = the character after the operand, and on into SCAN_OPERATOR."},
        "4232": {"synopsis":"The operator after an operand. A string followed by '(' is sliced first (STR_SLICE).\nAnything that is not an operator gets priority 0, which ends the expression.","comment":"'('?"},
        "4236": {"comment":"After a number it ends the expression"},
        "4242": {"comment":"After a string: its slice"},
        "4248": {"synopsis":"The operator's index: > < = + become 9-12, - * / 0-2, the keyword operators 3-8; anything\nelse has priority 0 and ends the expression.","comment":"Priority 0, base operation $C3"},
        "4251": {"comment":"Not an operator: end"},
        "4259": {"comment":"> < = + become 9 to 12"},
        "4263": {"synopsis":"- * / are 0-2; keyword operators (** OR AND <= >= <>) 3-8; other codes end the expression.","comment":"- * / are 0 to 2"},
        "4267": {"comment":"Keyword operators: ** OR AND <= >= <> ..."},
        "4275": {"comment":"... are 3 to 8"},
        "4277": {"synopsis":"C = $C3 + the index, B = its priority from the table at $110F."},
        "4278": {"comment":"C = $C3 + the operator's index"},
        "4283": {"comment":"B = its priority (from $110F)"},
        "4284": {"synopsis":"Compares the new operator (B, C) with the operation waiting on the stack (D, E). A waiting one\nof the same or higher priority is done now; one of lower priority keeps waiting, and the new\none waits above it (OP_STACK). Both 0: the expression is complete.","comment":"The waiting operation"},
        "4286": {"comment":"Lower than the new one: the new one waits too"},
        "4290": {"comment":"The bottom: the expression is done"},
        "4295": {"comment":"Checking?"},
        "4301": {"comment":"Running: B = the calculator code"},
        "4304": {"comment":"Do the operation"},
        "4309": {"synopsis":"Checking: the operand's type (FLAGS bit 6) must be the one the operation takes (E bit 6),\nelse report C.","comment":"Checking: the operand's type must be the one the operation takes"},
        "4315": {"synopsis":"Report C: an operand of the wrong type for its operation.","comment":"Otherwise report C"},
        "4318": {"synopsis":"FLAGS bit 6 = the result's type: a number, or a string when E bit 7 is clear."},
        "4322": {"comment":"The result: a number ..."},
        "4328": {"comment":"... or, with bit 7 of E clear, a string"},
        "4330": {"synopsis":"The next waiting operation is compared (OP_COMPARE)."},
        "4331": {"comment":"Compare with the next waiting operation"},
        "4333": {"synopsis":"The new operator waits. After a string operand, the string form of the operation is taken\n(the code + 8): + joins strings, the comparisons compare them and give a number; the\nother arithmetic operators are report C.","comment":"Keep the waiting operation"},
        "4335": {"comment":"After a number: as it is"},
        "4343": {"comment":"The string form of the operation"},
        "4346": {"comment":"AND after a string"},
        "4354": {"synopsis":"After a string: - * / ** OR AND are report C; + joins strings (a string result); a comparison\ngives a number.","comment":"- * / ** OR with a string: report C"},
        "4356": {"comment":"+ joins strings: a string result"},
        "4360": {"comment":"A comparison: a numeric result"},
        "4362": {"synopsis":"The operation waits on the machine stack; past the operator, the next operand.","comment":"The operation waits ..."},
        "4363": {"comment":"... past the operator, for the next operand"},
        "4367": {"synopsis":"Operator priorities, one byte each, read by OP_PRIORITY as $104C + C, C = $C3 + the\noperator's index:\n-  6 | *  8 | /  8 | ** 10 | OR 2 | AND 3 | <= 5 | >= 5 | <> 5 | >  5 | <  5 | =  5 | +  6\nThe operation itself is C AND $3F, a calculator code: $03 subtract up to $0F addition."},
        "4380": {"synopsis":"Finds the variable named at CH_ADD, and moves CH_ADD past its name.\nThe name gives the kind, in C bits 6 and 5: a letter followed by '(' is an array (0, 0),\nby '$' a string (1, 0; FLAGS bit 6 cleared), a single letter a number (1, 1), and more\nletters and digits a number with a longer name (0, 1). Bits 4-0 are the first letter's.\nWhen checking, nothing is searched: C gets bit 7. When running, the variables area is\nsearched from VARS (SEARCH_LOOP): a variable's first byte, bit 7 ignored, must equal C, and\na longer name must match character by character, spaces in the line skipped.\nExit: carry when not found; HL = the variable when found; Z for a string or an array (the\ncaller then reads its subscripts or slice, SUBSCRIPTS). Report C when there is no letter.\nAs stored (a run of LET A=1, LET AB=2, LET A$=\"X\"): $66 and 5 bytes; $A6, $A7 (B, bit 7\nmarking the last character) and 5 bytes; $46, a length of 1 and X; then the $80 end marker.","comment":"Assume a number (FLAGS bit 6)"},
        "4384": {"comment":"A = the name's first character"},
        "4385": {"comment":"A letter?"},
        "4388": {"comment":"No: report C"},
        "4391": {"comment":"The name's start"},
        "4392": {"comment":"C = the first letter"},
        "4394": {"comment":"Where the name goes on"},
        "4395": {"comment":"C bit 5 clear"},
        "4397": {"comment":"'(' at once: an array, bits 6 and 5 = 0, 0"},
        "4401": {"comment":"C bit 6"},
        "4403": {"comment":"'$': a string, bits 6 and 5 = 1, 0"},
        "4407": {"comment":"C bit 5: a number, bits 6 and 5 = 1, 1"},
        "4409": {"synopsis":"More letters or digits make a longer name (C bits 6, 5 = 0, 1).","comment":"More letters or digits?"},
        "4414": {"comment":"A longer name: bits 6 and 5 = 0, 1"},
        "4419": {"synopsis":"'$': past it, FLAGS bit 6 clear (a string).","comment":"Past the '$'"},
        "4420": {"comment":"FLAGS bit 6 clear: a string"},
        "4424": {"synopsis":"B = the kind. Checking: C = the kind with bit 7, no search. Running: the search.","comment":"B = C, the name's kind"},
        "4425": {"comment":"Checking?"},
        "4433": {"comment":"Checking: C = the kind with bit 7; no search"},
        "4438": {"synopsis":"Running: the search starts at VARS.","comment":"Running: HL = VARS"},
        "4441": {"synopsis":"One variable: the end marker means not found; the same kind and letter is found for a\nsingle letter, a string or an array; a longer name is compared character by character.","comment":"A = the variable's first byte, bit 7 ignored"},
        "4444": {"comment":"0: the end marker ($80), not found"},
        "4446": {"comment":"The same kind and letter?"},
        "4449": {"comment":"Bit 6 into carry, bit 5 into the sign"},
        "4451": {"comment":"Bit 5 clear (a string or an array): the letter is the whole name"},
        "4454": {"comment":"Bit 6 set too (a single letter): found"},
        "4456": {"comment":"A longer name: DE = its start in the line"},
        "4459": {"synopsis":"The next stored character of a longer name.","comment":"The next stored character"},
        "4460": {"synopsis":"The next character in the line, spaces skipped, compared with the stored one; the stored\nlast one has bit 7, and the line's name must end there too.","comment":"The next character in the line"},
        "4462": {"comment":"Spaces in the line do not count"},
        "4465": {"comment":"The same?"},
        "4468": {"comment":"The last stored character has bit 7 set"},
        "4473": {"comment":"And does the name in the line end there?"},
        "4477": {"comment":"Yes: found"},
        "4479": {"synopsis":"This long name differs: on to the next variable.","comment":"Not this one"},
        "4480": {"synopsis":"Steps to the next variable (NEXT_ITEM)."},
        "4481": {"comment":"Step to the next variable"},
        "4488": {"synopsis":"B bit 7: not found.","comment":"B bit 7: not found"},
        "4490": {"synopsis":"After the name: '(' means an array or a slice; otherwise B bit 5, a simple variable."},
        "4491": {"comment":"The character after the name"},
        "4492": {"comment":"'(': an array, or a slice"},
        "4496": {"comment":"B bit 5: a simple variable"},
        "4500": {"synopsis":"A longer name found: one more word off the stack."},
        "4501": {"synopsis":"Found: HL = the variable."},
        "4503": {"comment":"HL = the variable"},
        "4505": {"synopsis":"Moves CH_ADD past the rest of the name.","comment":"Pass the rest of the name"},
        "4513": {"synopsis":"HL = the variable; carry = not found; Z for a string or an array.","comment":"HL = the variable"},
        "4514": {"comment":"Carry = B bit 7: not found"},
        "4516": {"comment":"Z = B's old bit 5 clear: a string or an array"},
        "4519": {"synopsis":"After LOOK_VARS, for a name followed by '(' or a string or array it found (Z). C = the\nname's kind (bit 7 when checking), HL = the variable when running.\nRunning:\n- A simple string: its details go on the calculator stack (STK_STRING, A = 1); a '(' after\n  it is a slice (STR_SLICE, repeated while '(' follows).\n- A string array of one dimension is a string of that length (STK_STRING, A = 0), sliced\n  the same way: DIM C$(5): LET C$=\"HI\" holds \"HI   \" (observed).\n- Any other array: one subscript per dimension, except a string array's last (the strings'\n  length). Each must be 1 up to its dimension (GET_SUBSCRIPT); out of range, too few, too\n  many, or no ')' is report 3. The offset accumulates as offset x dimension + subscript - 1:\n  the last subscript varies fastest (observed: A(2,3) of DIM A(3,4) at elements + 30).\n  A number array: HL = the element - 1, the byte before its 5, as for a simple number\n  (whose name byte is there); the callers take HL + 1.\n  A string array: the element's string goes on the calculator stack (A = 0); ')' ends it,\n  ',' makes the rest a slice of it (B$(2,2) is string 2's second character, observed).\nChecking: the subscripts are numbers (class 6), any number of them, not compared with\nanything; for a string name (C = $C0) ')' at once, TO, or a TO after the first subscript\ngoes on as a slice (STR_SLICE).\nA string result clears FLAGS bit 6.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL.","comment":"B = 0"},
        "4523": {"comment":"Checking"},
        "4525": {"comment":"Bit 7 of the first byte clear: a simple string"},
        "4530": {"synopsis":"A string (or a one-dimension string array): its length and text onto the calculator stack\n(STK_STRING); then any slices."},
        "4531": {"comment":"BC = its length"},
        "4535": {"comment":"DE = its text"},
        "4536": {"comment":"Its details onto the calculator stack"},
        "4539": {"comment":"The character after the name"},
        "4540": {"comment":"Slices while '(' follows"},
        "4543": {"synopsis":"An array: B = its number of dimensions. A one-dimension string array is a string; another\nstring array needs '(' (report 3).","comment":"An array: past the first byte and the length"},
        "4546": {"comment":"B = its number of dimensions"},
        "4547": {"comment":"A string array?"},
        "4551": {"comment":"One dimension: a string of that length (A = 0)"},
        "4554": {"comment":"DE holds the pointer while the line is read"},
        "4556": {"comment":"'(' must follow, else report 3"},
        "4561": {"synopsis":"DE = the pointer at the dimension count; then the subscripts.","comment":"DE = the pointer, at the dimension count"},
        "4564": {"synopsis":"After a subscript: ',' for another; running, anything else here is too few (report 3);\nchecking, ')' ends a number array's, a string name's may end or go on as a slice.","comment":"After a subscript: the next character"},
        "4567": {"comment":"',': another subscript"},
        "4571": {"comment":"Running and no ',': too few subscripts, report 3"},
        "4575": {"comment":"Checking a string name?"},
        "4579": {"comment":"Checking a number array: ')' must close, else report C"},
        "4583": {"comment":"Past ')'"},
        "4585": {"synopsis":"Checking a string name: ')' ends it; TO makes what was read a slice's start; else report C.","comment":"A string name: ')' closes it"},
        "4589": {"comment":"TO: what was read is a slice's start; anything else is report C"},
        "4593": {"synopsis":"CH_ADD moves back one, so STR_SLICE reads a slice with no start.","comment":"CH_ADD one before TO: STR_SLICE reads a slice with no start"},
        "4600": {"synopsis":"HL = 0: the element's offset so far.","comment":"HL = 0: the offset so far"},
        "4603": {"synopsis":"Past '(' or ','. Checking a string name: ')' or TO at once are a whole string or a slice."},
        "4604": {"comment":"Past '(' or ','"},
        "4607": {"comment":"Checking a string name?"},
        "4612": {"comment":"')' at once: the whole string"},
        "4616": {"comment":"TO at once: a slice with no start"},
        "4620": {"synopsis":"One subscript: checked against its dimension (GET_SUBSCRIPT, report 3), and the offset =\noffset x dimension + subscript - 1."},
        "4622": {"comment":"DE = this dimension, HL = its high byte's address"},
        "4625": {"comment":"HL = the offset; the address waits"},
        "4626": {"comment":"HL = the dimension, DE = the offset"},
        "4627": {"comment":"BC = the subscript; carry when 0 or over the dimension"},
        "4630": {"comment":"Report 3"},
        "4632": {"comment":"Subscript - 1"},
        "4633": {"comment":"HL = the dimension x the offset"},
        "4636": {"comment":"+ subscript - 1: the new offset"},
        "4637": {"comment":"DE = the dimension's high byte's address"},
        "4639": {"comment":"While dimensions are left (checking started B at 0: 256 rounds)"},
        "4641": {"comment":"Checking here: report C"},
        "4643": {"synopsis":"NZ is report C. All subscripts read: a number element (SUBS_NUMBER) or a string element\n(SUBS_STR_ELEM).","comment":"NZ: report C"},
        "4645": {"comment":"The offset waits"},
        "4646": {"comment":"A string array?"},
        "4650": {"comment":"A number array: BC = the last dimension's high byte, the elements - 1"},
        "4653": {"comment":"')' must follow (not too many subscripts), else report 3"},
        "4657": {"synopsis":"Stops with report 3: a subscript that does not fit, or a missing closing bracket."},
        "4659": {"synopsis":"Past ')': HL = the elements' start - 1 + the offset x 5, the byte before the element's 5.","comment":"Past ')'"},
        "4664": {"comment":"HL = the offset x 5"},
        "4667": {"comment":"HL = the element - 1, the byte before its 5"},
        "4669": {"synopsis":"A string element: its start and the last dimension (its length) onto the calculator stack;\n')' ends it, ',' a slice of it.","comment":"DE = the last dimension, the strings' length"},
        "4672": {"comment":"HL = the offset; the address waits"},
        "4673": {"comment":"x the length"},
        "4677": {"comment":"+ the last dimension's high byte's address + 1: the string's start"},
        "4679": {"comment":"BC = its length, DE = its start"},
        "4682": {"comment":"Onto the calculator stack, A = 0"},
        "4686": {"comment":"')': done"},
        "4690": {"comment":"',': the rest is a slice of it; anything else is report 3"},
        "4694": {"synopsis":"A slice (STR_SLICE)."},
        "4697": {"synopsis":"Past ')'.","comment":"Past ')'"},
        "4698": {"synopsis":"Another slice while '(' follows; then FLAGS bit 6 clear, a string.","comment":"'(' again: another slice"},
        "4702": {"comment":"A string result: FLAGS bit 6 clear"},
        "4707": {"synopsis":"Slices a string by the brackets at CH_ADD: '(' (or the ',' after a string array's\nsubscripts), then ')' for the whole string, n ')' for the nth character, or [m] TO [n] ')'\nfor characters m to n (m is 1 and n the length when left out). No ')' is report C.\nRunning, the string is taken off the calculator stack (STK_FETCH) and the bounds counted\nwith GET_SUBSCRIPT_A: A counts down from 0 for each that is 0 or past the length. An empty\nslice (n before m) is taken whatever the bounds; otherwise a bad one is report 3.\nThe slice goes back on the calculator stack with A = 0 (STK_STRING_0): a slice is never a\nwhole string, so LET assigns into it in place, cut or padded (FLAGX bit 0, LET_ASSIGN).\nFLAGS bit 6 is cleared and CH_ADD left on ')'. Called by SUBSCRIPTS (a variable) and\nSCAN_OPERATOR (any string operand followed by '(').\nObserved with A$=\"HELLO\": A$(2 TO 4) ELL, A$(TO 2) HE, A$(4 TO) LO, A$() HELLO, A$(3) L,\nA$(2 TO 4)(2) L; A$(4 TO 2), A$(10 TO 2) and A$(6 TO 5) empty; A$(0 TO 0), A$(6) and\nA$(2 TO 6) report 3; \"ABC\"(2) B; LET A$(2 TO 3)=\"XYZ\" makes HXYLO, =\"Q\" makes HQ LO.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL.","comment":"Checking: nothing on the calculator stack"},
        "4710": {"comment":"Running: DE = the text, BC = its length"},
        "4713": {"comment":"Past '(' (or ',')"},
        "4714": {"comment":"')' at once: the whole string"},
        "4718": {"comment":"The text's start waits"},
        "4719": {"comment":"A = 0: no bad bound yet"},
        "4721": {"comment":"The length"},
        "4722": {"comment":"DE = 1: the start when none is given"},
        "4726": {"comment":"HL = the length"},
        "4727": {"comment":"TO at once: no start"},
        "4731": {"comment":"A = the bad-bound count"},
        "4732": {"comment":"BC = the start; counted when 0 or past the length"},
        "4736": {"comment":"DE = the start"},
        "4741": {"comment":"TO: an end may follow"},
        "4745": {"comment":"Else only ')': n ')' is the nth character"},
        "4747": {"synopsis":"Report C when NZ. Otherwise n ')': the end = the start.","comment":"Report C"},
        "4750": {"comment":"The end = the start"},
        "4754": {"synopsis":"Past TO: ')' at once makes the end the length; else the end is read (GET_SUBSCRIPT_A) and\n')' must follow.","comment":"Past TO"},
        "4757": {"comment":"')' at once: the end is the length"},
        "4762": {"comment":"BC = the end; counted when 0 or past the length"},
        "4767": {"comment":"HL = the end"},
        "4769": {"comment":"')' must follow, else report C"},
        "4773": {"synopsis":"From the bounds: the first character's address, and the length (0 when the end is before\nthe start, whatever the bounds; a bad bound is report 3 otherwise).","comment":"A = the bad-bound count"},
        "4774": {"comment":"HL = the text's start; the end waits"},
        "4775": {"comment":"+ start - 1: the slice's first character"},
        "4777": {"comment":"That waits; HL = the end"},
        "4779": {"comment":"End - start"},
        "4781": {"comment":"BC = 0 ..."},
        "4784": {"comment":"... when the end is before the start: empty, the bounds not looked at"},
        "4786": {"comment":"The length: end - start + 1"},
        "4788": {"comment":"A bad bound: report 3"},
        "4793": {"synopsis":"DE = the slice's first character; FLAGS bit 6 clear.","comment":"DE = the slice's first character"},
        "4794": {"comment":"A string result"},
        "4798": {"synopsis":"Checking: done. Running: on into STK_STRING_0, the slice onto the calculator stack.","comment":"Checking: done"},
        "4801": {"comment":"Running: back onto the calculator stack, A = 0"},
        "4802": {"synopsis":"STK_STRING with A = 0."},
        "4803": {"synopsis":"Puts a string's details on the calculator stack: 5 bytes, A, then DE (its start) and BC\n(its length), after checking there is room (TEST_5_ROOM); FLAGS bit 6 cleared (a string).\nThe first byte is what CLASS_1 ORs into FLAGX: SUBSCRIPTS stacks a whole simple string with\nA = 1, so LET replaces it at any length; STR_SLICE and string array elements stack A = 0,\nso LET assigns in place (LET_ASSIGN, FLAGX bit 0).\nExit: HL = the new STKEND."},
        "4808": {"comment":"HL = STKEND"},
        "4821": {"comment":"STKEND past the 5 bytes"},
        "4829": {"synopsis":"Evaluates a subscript (CLASS_6: a numeric expression, else report C) and, when running,\nchecks it against HL: BC = its value (FIND_INT, report B past 65535).\nExit: carry and A = $FF when it is 0 or more than HL; HL and DE kept.\nEntered at GET_SUBSCRIPT_A ($12DE) with A already set, A is decremented instead when the\nvalue is out of range, with carry only when A was 0 (STR_SLICE counts this way).\nWhen checking nothing is compared (from $12DD, carry clear).\nChanges: AF, BC.","comment":"A = 0: no error so far"},
        "4830": {"synopsis":"GET_SUBSCRIPT with A as it is: A is decremented for a bound out of range."},
        "4833": {"comment":"Evaluate it"},
        "4837": {"comment":"Checking: no value to check"},
        "4843": {"comment":"BC = the value"},
        "4846": {"comment":"D = A on entry"},
        "4847": {"comment":"Zero: out of range"},
        "4852": {"comment":"HL = the limit"},
        "4855": {"comment":"Carry when the value is over it"},
        "4857": {"synopsis":"A = A - carry: the bad-bound count; carry out only when A was 0.","comment":"A - carry: carry out only when A was 0"},
        "4860": {"synopsis":"HL and DE back."},
        "4863": {"synopsis":"Steps to the next dimension word after DE (the dimension count, or the previous word's\nhigh byte).\nExit: DE = the dimension, HL = its high byte's address."},
        "4869": {"synopsis":"HL = HL x DE when running (16-bit shift and add); report 4 (NO_ROOM) when the product\npasses 65535. When checking it returns at once.\nChanges: AF, HL.","comment":"Checking: return"},
        "4874": {"comment":"16 bits of the multiplier"},
        "4876": {"comment":"A, C = the multiplier; HL = 0"},
        "4881": {"synopsis":"One bit of the multiply: the product doubled, then DE added for a set multiplier bit.","comment":"Product x 2; past 65535: report 4"},
        "4884": {"comment":"The multiplier's next bit into carry"},
        "4889": {"comment":"Set: add DE"},
        "4890": {"synopsis":"Report 4 (NO_ROOM) on a carry: past 65535.","comment":"Past 65535: report 4"},
        "4893": {"synopsis":"16 rounds; then BC back."},
        "4897": {"synopsis":"Assigns the value the expression left on the calculator stack to the variable class 1\nfound (DEST); where class 2 sends LET, and INPUT's answer, when running.\n- A new variable (FLAGX bit 1) is built at the end of the variables area: the room is made\n  at the end of the workspace, and everything between E_LINE and it removed, so it lands\n  where the $80 end marker was; a new $80 and E_LINE follow it. A number takes its name\n  (first byte $60 + the letter's bits 4-0; or $A0 + them, then the other characters, the\n  last with bit 7)\n  and 5 bytes; a string ($40 + them) its length and text (NEW_STRING).\n- An existing number gets its 5 bytes overwritten in place.\n- An existing string, whole (FLAGX bit 0), is made again at the end of the area and the old\n  one removed (REPLACE_STRING). A part of one (a slice or an array element) keeps its length:\n  the new text is cut or padded with spaces to fit (OLD_STRING).\nA run of LET A$=\"HELLO\", LET B=3, LET A$=\"HI\", LET B=4 left A$ after B, and B changed in\nplace.","comment":"HL = DEST, the variable"},
        "4900": {"comment":"FLAGX bit 1: a new variable"},
        "4906": {"comment":"BC = 5 for the value, then one for each name character"},
        "4909": {"synopsis":"One more name character counted in BC (5 for the value, then one per character)."},
        "4910": {"synopsis":"The name's next character in the line, spaces skipped: letters and digits count; '$' makes\na new string (NEW_STRING); then room for a new number (RST $30)."},
        "4912": {"comment":"Spaces in the line do not count"},
        "4915": {"comment":"A letter or digit: one more"},
        "4920": {"comment":"'$': a new string"},
        "4925": {"comment":"Room for the new number at the end of the workspace"},
        "4931": {"comment":"B = the characters after the first"},
        "4935": {"comment":"$40: the first byte for a single letter, with the XOR below"},
        "4937": {"comment":"A single letter"},
        "4939": {"synopsis":"Copies a longer name's other characters, spaces left out, the last with bit 7; the first\nbyte is then $80 XOR the letter."},
        "4945": {"comment":"Copy the other characters, spaces left out"},
        "4948": {"comment":"The last one gets bit 7"},
        "4951": {"comment":"$80: the first byte for a longer name"},
        "4953": {"synopsis":"The first byte, the letter XOR $40 or $80; the new variable is moved to the end of the\nvariables area (PUT_AT_VARS_END)."},
        "4956": {"comment":"First byte = the letter XOR $40 or $80: $66 for A, $A6 for AB"},
        "4958": {"comment":"Move it to the end of the variables area, first byte and $80 after"},
        "4961": {"synopsis":"The value comes off the calculator stack; HL = where the variable's 5 bytes go, and they\nare copied (COPY_VALUE)."},
        "4962": {"comment":"Take the value off the calculator stack"},
        "4970": {"comment":"HL = where the variable's 5 bytes go"},
        "4974": {"synopsis":"An existing variable: a number has its 5 bytes overwritten (HL = DEST + 1); a string goes\nto OLD_STRING.","comment":"An existing variable: a number?"},
        "4980": {"comment":"Its 5 bytes follow its name: HL = DEST + 6, less 5 later"},
        "4986": {"synopsis":"An existing string: a whole one is replaced (REPLACE_STRING); a part keeps its length, the\nnew text cut or padded with spaces in a copy, then copied in.","comment":"An existing string: HL = DEST"},
        "4989": {"comment":"BC = STRLEN, the length it has"},
        "4993": {"comment":"FLAGX bit 0: a whole string, replaced"},
        "5001": {"comment":"A part of length 0: nothing to do"},
        "5003": {"comment":"Room for a copy of that length"},
        "5009": {"comment":"Fill it with spaces"},
        "5014": {"comment":"DE, BC = the new text"},
        "5020": {"comment":"Longer than the room?"},
        "5025": {"comment":"Then only what fits"},
        "5027": {"synopsis":"The new text (as much as fits) into the copy filled with spaces."},
        "5033": {"comment":"The new text into the padded copy"},
        "5035": {"synopsis":"The registers back for COPY_VALUE: the copy goes into the old place."},
        "5038": {"synopsis":"Copies BC bytes from HL to DE (after EX DE,HL): the value into the variable.","comment":"DE = the variable, HL = the value"},
        "5043": {"comment":"Copy the value in"},
        "5047": {"synopsis":"A whole string: the new one is made at the end with the old first byte (MAKE_STRING_VAR),\nthen the old one, its length + 3 bytes, is reclaimed.","comment":"HL = the old string's first byte"},
        "5053": {"comment":"Make the new one at the end, with the same first byte"},
        "5058": {"comment":"Its length + 3 ..."},
        "5061": {"comment":"... removed: the old string goes"},
        "5064": {"synopsis":"A new string: first byte = the letter XOR $60; on into MAKE_STRING_VAR.","comment":"A new string: first byte = the letter XOR $60, $46 for A$"},
        "5070": {"synopsis":"Makes a string variable with first byte A from the string on the calculator stack, at the\nend of the variables area: its text, its length below it, then A (PUT_AT_VARS_END)."},
        "5071": {"comment":"DE, BC = the string"},
        "5077": {"comment":"Room for the text, the length and the first byte"},
        "5086": {"comment":"Copy the text in, downwards"},
        "5091": {"comment":"The length below it"},
        "5095": {"synopsis":"Moves a variable just built at the end of the workspace (at HL) to the end of the variables\narea: removes everything from E_LINE to it (RECLAIM_ELINE), writes A as its first byte over\nthe old $80 end marker, and puts a new $80 and E_LINE after it.\nExit: HL = the new $80.","comment":"Remove what lies between E_LINE and it"},
        "5101": {"comment":"A over the old end marker: the first byte"},
        "5105": {"comment":"E_LINE = STKBOT"},
        "5109": {"comment":"The new end marker before it"},
        "5112": {"synopsis":"Takes the top 5 bytes off the calculator stack: A, then DE and BC (a string's details, as\nSTK_STRING left them: DE its start, BC its length).\nExit: HL = STKEND, the bytes' address."},
        "5129": {"synopsis":"DIM name(d1, d2, ...) (class 5, from the syntax table): makes a new array.\nA number array (A(...)) holds 5 bytes per element; a string array (A$(...)) one character,\nits last dimension being the strings' length. The name must be followed by '(' (Z from\nLOOK_VARS), else report C.\nChecking: SUBSCRIPTS checks the dimensions as numbers (C bit 6 cleared), then STATEMENT_END.\nRunning: an array of the same name is reclaimed first. Each dimension (GET_SUBSCRIPT) must\nbe 1 up to $40xx (H = $40, L the low byte of CH_ADD: about 16K), else report 3; the size of\nthe elements, the dimensions times the element size (MULTIPLY_HL_DE), past 65535 is report 4.\nThe room is made before the variables' end marker (E_LINE - 1): the array is the last variable.\nLayout: the first byte (the letter's code with bit 5 clear, bit 6 set for a string array,\nbit 7 set: an array); 2 bytes, the length of the rest; 1 byte, the number of dimensions;\n2 bytes per dimension, the first one first; then the elements, all 0 (zero numbers, spaces),\nthe last subscript varying fastest. Observed: DIM A(3,4) gives $86 $41 $00 $02 $03 $00 $04\n$00 and 60 zeros; DIM B$(2,3) gives $C7 $0B $00 $02 $02 $00 $03 $00 and 6 zeros; DIM A(0)\nand DIM A(17000) report 3, DIM A(16000) report 4.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL.","comment":"C = the name's kind; Z when '(' follows"},
        "5132": {"synopsis":"Report C without '(' after the name. Checking: the dimensions are checked as numbers\n(SUBSCRIPTS), then the statement must end.","comment":"No '(' (or, from $144F, no ')' after the dimensions): report C"},
        "5140": {"comment":"Checking: the dimensions are numbers whatever the name"},
        "5145": {"comment":"When checking this returns past DIM_CMD"},
        "5148": {"synopsis":"Running: an old array of the name is reclaimed first.","comment":"LOOK_VARS's carry (BIT leaves it): no such array yet"},
        "5151": {"comment":"Step over the old array: BC = its length"},
        "5154": {"comment":"Reclaim it"},
        "5158": {"synopsis":"The new array's first byte (bit 7) and dimension count 0 wait on the stack; the element size\nis 1 for a string array, 5 for a number one.","comment":"C = the first byte: bit 7, an array"},
        "5160": {"comment":"B = 0 dimensions so far"},
        "5163": {"comment":"Element size 1 for a string array ..."},
        "5170": {"comment":"... 5 for a number array"},
        "5172": {"synopsis":"DE = the element size, the product's start.","comment":"DE = the element size: it becomes the product"},
        "5173": {"synopsis":"One dimension: 1 up to $40xx (report 3), multiplied into DE (report 4), pushed; ',' for the\nnext, ')' to end (else report C).","comment":"Past '(' or ','; HL = CH_ADD"},
        "5174": {"comment":"The limit: $40, CH_ADD's low byte"},
        "5176": {"comment":"BC = the dimension; carry when it is 0 or over the limit"},
        "5179": {"comment":"Report 3"},
        "5182": {"comment":"H = the dimensions so far, L = the first byte"},
        "5183": {"comment":"The dimension waits on the stack"},
        "5184": {"comment":"One more dimension"},
        "5188": {"comment":"HL = the dimension x the product so far; report 4 past 65535"},
        "5191": {"comment":"DE = the new product"},
        "5193": {"comment":"',': another dimension"},
        "5197": {"comment":"')' must close them, else report C at $140C (NZ)"},
        "5201": {"comment":"Past ')'"},
        "5202": {"comment":"B = the number of dimensions, C = the first byte"},
        "5204": {"comment":"HL = 2 x (dimensions + 2): the header and the dimension words"},
        "5210": {"comment":"+ the elements"},
        "5211": {"comment":"Past 65535: report 4"},
        "5219": {"comment":"HL = E_LINE - 1, the variables' $80 end marker"},
        "5223": {"comment":"Room before it; DE = the room's last byte"},
        "5227": {"comment":"The first byte"},
        "5228": {"comment":"BC = the total size"},
        "5229": {"comment":"Minus 3: the length field"},
        "5236": {"comment":"A = the number of dimensions (pushed as B)"},
        "5238": {"comment":"The number of dimensions"},
        "5239": {"comment":"HL = the room's last byte, DE = the one below"},
        "5242": {"comment":"Zero it ..."},
        "5244": {"comment":"BC = the elements' size"},
        "5245": {"comment":"... and copy the zero down over the elements (one byte more, overwritten next)"},
        "5247": {"synopsis":"DIM's last loop: the dimensions off the stack into the array, the last first, downwards.","comment":"The dimensions, the last one first, downwards"},
        "5256": {"synopsis":"The rest of WORK_ROOM: opens the room before STKBOT - 1 (MAKE_ROOM), so that byte's copy\nbecomes the room's last byte, and puts E_LINE back.","comment":"HL = STKBOT - 1"},
        "5266": {"comment":"E_LINE as it was"},
        "5274": {"synopsis":"Empties the variables area: writes the end marker at VARS, starts the edit line\n(E_LINE) right after it, and makes the calculator stack empty there\n(STKBOT = STKEND = E_LINE).\nChanges: HL.","comment":"HL = VARS"},
        "5277": {"comment":"$80 marks the end of the variables"},
        "5280": {"comment":"E_LINE = VARS+1"},
        "5283": {"synopsis":"STKBOT = STKEND = E_LINE: an empty calculator stack at the edit line."},
        "5286": {"synopsis":"STKBOT = STKEND = HL: an empty calculator stack at HL."},
        "5289": {"synopsis":"STKEND = HL."},
        "5293": {"synopsis":"Puts an empty edit line at E_LINE: the cursor and a NEWLINE. Sets DF_SZ to 2 and\nmakes the calculator stack empty after the line (SET_STKBOT).\nChanges: HL.","comment":"HL = E_LINE"},
        "5296": {"comment":"$7F is the cursor"},
        "5299": {"comment":"$76 (NEWLINE) ends the line"},
        "5302": {"comment":"DF_SZ = 2: the lower part of the screen is two lines"},
        "5306": {"comment":"STKBOT = STKEND = the byte after the line"},
        "5308": {"synopsis":"Empties the calculator stack: MEM = MEMBOT ($405D), STKEND = STKBOT (SET_STKEND).\nCalled by SCAN_LINE, EDIT_LINE_NO and the error handler.\nChanges: HL."},
        "5311": {"comment":"MEM = MEMBOT"},
        "5314": {"comment":"STKEND = STKBOT"},
        "5319": {"synopsis":"Removes the bytes from E_LINE up to, not including, HL (RECLAIM_DE_HL)."},
        "5326": {"synopsis":"Carry set when A is a letter ($26-$3F)."},
        "5330": {"synopsis":"Carry set when A is a digit or a letter ($1C-$3F)."},
        "5332": {"synopsis":"ALPHA's and ALPHANUM's common test: carry when A is from the lower bound up to $3F."},
        "5337": {"synopsis":"Reads the decimal number at CH_ADD onto the calculator stack: the whole part\n(READ_INT), a fraction after '.', each digit worth a tenth of the one before (memory 0\nholds that place value), and an exponent after E (EXPONENT_PART).\nExit: A = the character after the number.\nRuns: 1.5E3 = 1500, 2E-3 = .002, 12345.678, 1E+2 = 100, .125; a program line 10 PRINT 1.5\nwas stored as 00 0A 0B 00 F5 1D 1B 21 7E 81 40 00 00 00 76: the digits, the $7E marker\nand 1.5's 5 bytes (81 40 00 00 00).","comment":"The whole part"},
        "5340": {"comment":"A '.'?"},
        "5344": {"comment":"Memory 0 = 1, the place value"},
        "5349": {"synopsis":"A digit after the point: its value x the place value (memory 0, divided by 10 each time)\nis added on.","comment":"The next character"},
        "5350": {"comment":"A digit onto the stack? No: on to the exponent"},
        "5355": {"comment":"Place value / 10, the digit times it, added on"},
        "5365": {"synopsis":"An 'E': the exponent's sign (memory 0's first byte $FF positive, 0 negative), its digits,\nand the number x 10^exponent (e-to-fp).","comment":"An 'E'?"},
        "5368": {"comment":"Memory 0's first byte = $FF: a positive exponent so far"},
        "5373": {"comment":"'+': nothing to do"},
        "5377": {"comment":"'-'?"},
        "5381": {"comment":"Then that byte = 0"},
        "5384": {"synopsis":"Past the sign."},
        "5385": {"synopsis":"The exponent's digits (READ_INT); negated for '-', then e-to-fp.","comment":"The exponent's digits"},
        "5388": {"comment":"Memory 0 reads as a number: $FF00... is not 0, $00... is; negated when it is 0;\nthen x 10^exponent (e-to-fp)"},
        "5396": {"synopsis":"When A is a digit ('0' to '9', $1C-$25), puts its value on the calculator stack, carry\nclear; otherwise carry set."},
        "5405": {"synopsis":"Puts A on the calculator stack as a number (STACK_BC with BC = A)."},
        "5408": {"synopsis":"Puts BC on the calculator stack as a number. The exponent starts at $91 (16 bits; $89 and\nthe low byte only, when B is 0) and BC shifts left until its leading 1 falls out, the\nexponent down one each time; the 1 is then implied and the sign bit 0. BC = 0 stays 0.\nIY is set to $4000 first: usr-no returns here from machine code that may have changed it.","comment":"IY = the system variables again"},
        "5413": {"comment":"A 0 on the stack, HL at it"},
        "5417": {"comment":"Exponent $91: BC as a 16-bit whole number"},
        "5421": {"comment":"B = 0: only 8 bits"},
        "5425": {"comment":"BC = 0: the number 0"},
        "5428": {"comment":"C up into B, exponent $89"},
        "5430": {"synopsis":"Shifts BC left with the exponent going down until the leading 1 is out; a 0 sign bit\nreplaces it, and BC is the mantissa's first two bytes.","comment":"Exponent down, BC left, until the leading 1 falls out"},
        "5437": {"comment":"A 0 in its place: the sign bit, positive"},
        "5442": {"comment":"The mantissa's first two bytes"},
        "5448": {"synopsis":"Reads the digits at CH_ADD onto the calculator stack as a whole number: total x 10 + digit\nfor each.\nExit: A = the character after the digits.","comment":"A 0 to start with"},
        "5453": {"synopsis":"One digit: total x 10 + the digit; until a character that is not a digit.","comment":"A digit? No: done"},
        "5457": {"comment":"Total x 10 + the digit"},
        "5463": {"comment":"The next character"},
        "5466": {"synopsis":"e-to-fp ($38): x x 10^e, from x and e (e on top). Memory 0 holds whether e is negative;\nthen, counting |e| down, x is multiplied (divided, for a negative e) by 10^7 while 7 or more\nare left, by 10 otherwise."},
        "5514": {"synopsis":"Takes the number off the calculator stack and gives its size in BC, rounded to the nearest\nwhole number.\nExit: carry set when it is 65536 or more; Z when it is positive; A = C.","comment":"A = the exponent; E, D, C, B the mantissa"},
        "5518": {"comment":"The number 0: BC = 0, Z"},
        "5525": {"synopsis":"BC = the top 16 mantissa bits; carry when the exponent is $91 or more (too big); Z for a\npositive number; then the shift right for the exponent.","comment":"BC = the top 16 mantissa bits, E the next 8"},
        "5528": {"comment":"Carry: exponent $91 or more, too big"},
        "5531": {"comment":"Z: positive"},
        "5534": {"comment":"The leading 1 back"},
        "5538": {"comment":"A = how many bits right: $90 - exponent"},
        "5541": {"comment":"8 or more: a whole byte first"},
        "5551": {"synopsis":"D = the bits still to shift; carry = the bit below, for rounding when there is no shift.","comment":"D = the bits left to shift"},
        "5554": {"comment":"Carry = the bit below, for no shift at all"},
        "5557": {"synopsis":"BC one bit right, D times; carry = the last bit out.","comment":"BC one bit right; carry = the bit out"},
        "5564": {"synopsis":"Rounds up on the last bit out; wrapping to 0 means too big (carry).","comment":"Round up on the last bit out"},
        "5569": {"comment":"It wrapped to 0: too big"},
        "5574": {"synopsis":"The number's address back (end-calc); A = C, carry and Z as found."},
        "5575": {"comment":"HL, DE at the stack's top again"},
        "5579": {"comment":"A = C; carry, Z as set above"},
        "5581": {"synopsis":"Takes the number off the calculator stack and gives its size in A, rounded (FP_TO_BC).\nExit: carry set when it is over 255; Z when it is positive.","comment":"Too big for BC"},
        "5586": {"comment":"B not 0: over 255"},
        "5593": {"synopsis":"Flags as FP_TO_BC left them."},
        "5595": {"synopsis":"Prints the number on the calculator stack, and takes it off.\n0 prints as 0; a negative number as '-' and its size (PRINT_MINUS). Otherwise:\n1. E = INT((exponent - 128.5) x 0.30103), an estimate of the decimal exponent (log10 2 =\n   0.30103), kept in calculator memory 1.\n2. The number x 10^(8 - E), rounded to a whole number, is split into 10 decimal digits at\n   $406C-$4075 in MEMBOT, the lowest first, each as $90 + digit (PFP_DIGIT_LOOP).\n3. 8 significant digits are kept, rounded on the 9th with BCD (PFP_ROUND); the ones below\n   are marked $80, which ends the digits.\n4. Plain form, or E form when the exponent is 13 or more, or small enough (PFP_E_FORM): one\n   digit, the rest after '.', then E, a sign and the exponent.\nRuns printed 0, -2.5, 0.33333333 (1/3), 123456790 (123456789), 1000000000000 (1E12),\n1E+13, .0001, .00001, 6.6666667E-8 (2/3E7), -1E-20 and 1E+38.","comment":"Negative, positive or 0?"},
        "5606": {"comment":"0: print '0'"},
        "5610": {"synopsis":"Calculator literals reached by PRINT_FP's first jump-true (a negative number): $27 abs and\n$34 end-calc, so the number is made positive before the '-' is printed (PRINT_MINUS)."},
        "5612": {"synopsis":"A negative number: '-' printed, then its size goes on as a positive one.","comment":"Print '-'; the number is its size now"},
        "5615": {"comment":"Rejoin the positive case"},
        "5617": {"synopsis":"A positive number: E = INT((exponent - 128.5) x log10 2), and the number scaled by\n10^(8 - E) and rounded, so its whole part holds the digits.","comment":"A = the number's exponent byte"},
        "5618": {"comment":"On the stack as a number"},
        "5621": {"comment":"E = INT((exponent - 128.5) x log10 2) into memory 1; the number x 10^(8 - E),\nrounded"},
        "5646": {"comment":"HL = $406B: a $90 below the digits"},
        "5651": {"comment":"10 digits"},
        "5653": {"synopsis":"The digits, the lowest first: n-mod-m by 10 gives each, stored as $90 + digit."},
        "5656": {"comment":"Divide by 10: the remainder is the next digit up"},
        "5661": {"comment":"A = the digit"},
        "5664": {"comment":"Stored as $90 + digit"},
        "5671": {"comment":"HL = past the top digit"},
        "5676": {"synopsis":"Down past the leading zeros; the 9th digit decides the rounding.","comment":"Down past the leading zeros ($90)"},
        "5682": {"comment":"HL = 8 digits below the first significant one: the 9th"},
        "5686": {"comment":"Carry when the 9th digit is 5 or more"},
        "5689": {"synopsis":"Adds the carry to the digit above as BCD; a 0 after it carries on up. Rounded digits are\nstored as $80 + digit."},
        "5691": {"comment":"Add the carry to the digit above ..."},
        "5694": {"comment":"... as BCD: $9A becomes $00 with carry"},
        "5699": {"comment":"Rounded digits are $80 + digit"},
        "5701": {"comment":"A 0 after rounding: the carry goes on up"},
        "5705": {"comment":"The 6 digits below the 8 kept ..."},
        "5707": {"synopsis":"The 6 digits below the 8 kept become $80, the end of the digits; E comes back from memory 1.","comment":"... become $80: the end of the digits"},
        "5712": {"comment":"The whole number off; E back from memory 1"},
        "5716": {"comment":"A = the size of E, Z when positive"},
        "5723": {"synopsis":"E = the decimal exponent + 2.","comment":"E = the decimal exponent + 2 ..."},
        "5727": {"synopsis":"Down past zero digits, E down with them; then the form: E form for a large or very small\nnumber, else digits, point and the rest.","comment":"... down by one for each leading zero"},
        "5729": {"comment":"A digit of 0?"},
        "5734": {"comment":"Which form?"},
        "5737": {"comment":"Exponent large: E form"},
        "5742": {"comment":"Very small: E form"},
        "5747": {"comment":"The digits before the point"},
        "5749": {"comment":"None: '0.' and the rest"},
        "5751": {"comment":"A small number: '.', zeros, the rest"},
        "5755": {"synopsis":"The digits before the point (B of them), then the fraction.","comment":"Print the digits before the point"},
        "5760": {"comment":"Then the rest after a point"},
        "5762": {"synopsis":"E form: one digit, the rest after a point, 'E', the sign and the exponent.","comment":"E form: one digit ..."},
        "5766": {"comment":"... the rest after a point ..."},
        "5769": {"comment":"... then 'E'"},
        "5772": {"comment":"The exponent's sign"},
        "5780": {"comment":"'-'"},
        "5784": {"synopsis":"'+' for a positive exponent.","comment":"'+'"},
        "5786": {"synopsis":"Prints the sign; B counts the exponent's tens."},
        "5788": {"comment":"B = the tens"},
        "5790": {"synopsis":"B = the tens, C = the units; the tens only when not 0."},
        "5797": {"comment":"C = the units"},
        "5800": {"comment":"No tens: one digit only"},
        "5805": {"synopsis":"The units digit.","comment":"The units"},
        "5810": {"synopsis":"A small number: '.', then B zeros, then the digits.","comment":"A small number: '.' ..."},
        "5816": {"comment":"... then the zeros after it"},
        "5818": {"synopsis":"One zero after the point; B of them."},
        "5823": {"synopsis":"'0' before the point when there are no digits before it.","comment":"'0' before the point"},
        "5826": {"synopsis":"The fraction: nothing when no digits are left ($80); else '.' and the digits.","comment":"No digits left ($80)? Then no point"},
        "5829": {"comment":"'.'"},
        "5832": {"synopsis":"The next digit, until the $80 that ends them.","comment":"No digits left ($80)?"},
        "5835": {"comment":"The next digit"},
        "5840": {"synopsis":"Prints the digit at HL (its low 4 bits) and moves HL down to the next.\nThe test DEC (HL) / INC (HL) / RET PE before each call stops at $80, the end marker: only\n$80 overflows on the way back up."},
        "5848": {"synopsis":"Prepares the number at HL for addition, as a 5-byte two's-complement value: the exponent\nbyte becomes the sign byte (0, or $FF for a negative number), the mantissa's leading 1 is\nput back in place of the sign bit, and a negative number is negated across all 5 bytes.\nExit: A = the exponent (0 for the number 0, which is left as 0).","comment":"A = the exponent; the byte becomes the sign byte"},
        "5852": {"comment":"The number 0"},
        "5854": {"comment":"Z: positive"},
        "5856": {"comment":"The leading 1 back"},
        "5859": {"comment":"Positive: done"},
        "5864": {"comment":"Negative: negate all 5 bytes, from the last"},
        "5868": {"synopsis":"Two's complement of the mantissa, byte by byte: CPL plus the carry."},
        "5870": {"comment":"CPL, plus the carry"},
        "5879": {"synopsis":"Loads two prepared numbers (PREP_ADD) into registers: the one at HL into B'C' (high) and\nC B (bytes 3, 4), the one at DE into D'E' (high) and D E (low); their sign bytes into H'\n(HL's) and L' (DE's). A is left in the second byte of the number at HL.\nExit: HL and A as on entry."},
        "5914": {"synopsis":"Shifts the number in L' D'E' D E (sign byte and mantissa) right by A bits, the sign\nextended, to line it up with a number A binary places larger. 33 or more: it becomes 0.\nA 1 shifted out last rounds the result up (INC_32).","comment":"No shift"},
        "5916": {"comment":"33 or more: nothing left"},
        "5922": {"synopsis":"The 32 bits and the sign byte one bit right, B times; a 1 shifted out last rounds up\n(INC_32), and a rounding that wraps the 32 bits to 0 makes the whole number 0\n(SHIFT_TO_ZERO)."},
        "5923": {"comment":"Sign byte, then the mantissa, one bit right"},
        "5937": {"comment":"The last bit out 0: no rounding"},
        "5938": {"comment":"Round up"},
        "5942": {"synopsis":"Every register 0: the number shifted out entirely.","comment":"0 in every register"},
        "5944": {"synopsis":"L' = 0, D' = A, E' = 0, D E = 0 (SHIFT_TO_ZERO enters with A = 0, NORM_CLEAR with A = $80\nor 0). Ends in the main register set."},
        "5953": {"synopsis":"Adds 1 to the 32-bit D'E' D E. Z when it wraps round to 0."},
        "5962": {"synopsis":"EXX back, return."},
        "5964": {"synopsis":"subtract ($03): flips the second number's sign, and adds (CALC_ADDITION). A second number\nof 0 leaves the first as it is.","comment":"The second number 0?"},
        "5969": {"comment":"Flip its sign"},
        "5973": {"synopsis":"addition ($0F): first (HL) + second (DE); the result replaces the first.\n1. Both are prepared as two's-complement values (PREP_ADD), and the one with the smaller\n   exponent is shifted right by the difference (SHIFT_RIGHT); the result takes the larger\n   exponent.\n2. The two 32-bit mantissas are added, then their sign bytes with the carry. A carry out of\n   the top shifts the sum right by one and the exponent goes up; past $FF is report 6.\n3. A negative sum is negated back to its size, its sign kept for the result (ADD_SIGN).\n4. NORMALISE shifts it left until its top bit is 1, rounds, and STORE_RESULT writes it.\nRuns: 1+2 = 3, 1-1 = 0, -1+.5 = -0.5, .1+.2 = 0.3, 5-8 = -3, 1-1E-10 = 1, 1E38+1E38 report 6.","comment":"Keep the code pointer"},
        "5978": {"comment":"Prepare the first; B = its exponent"},
        "5983": {"comment":"Prepare the second; C = its exponent"},
        "5987": {"comment":"HL = the one with the larger exponent"},
        "5993": {"synopsis":"The smaller number shifted right by the exponents' difference (SHIFT_RIGHT); the larger\nexponent is the result's; the 32 bits and the sign bytes are added. A sum out of the top is\nshifted right one bit with the exponent up (past $FF: report 6)."},
        "5994": {"comment":"A = how much smaller the other one is"},
        "5995": {"comment":"Both into registers"},
        "5998": {"comment":"Line the smaller one up"},
        "6003": {"comment":"The result's exponent: the larger one"},
        "6007": {"comment":"The low 16 bits"},
        "6010": {"comment":"The high 16 bits"},
        "6014": {"comment":"The sign bytes, with the carry"},
        "6017": {"comment":"Bit 7: the sum ran out of the top"},
        "6022": {"comment":"No: it fits"},
        "6026": {"comment":"Yes: one bit right ..."},
        "6029": {"comment":"... and the exponent up"},
        "6030": {"comment":"Past $FF: report 6"},
        "6032": {"synopsis":"The result's sign into its second byte; a negative sum is negated to its size."},
        "6033": {"comment":"The result's sign ..."},
        "6038": {"comment":"... kept in its second byte for STORE_RESULT"},
        "6040": {"comment":"Positive: done"},
        "6042": {"comment":"Negative: negate the 32 bits, its size"},
        "6062": {"comment":"No carry out of the top"},
        "6064": {"comment":"It was the top bit itself: one bit right, the exponent up"},
        "6067": {"synopsis":"Report 6 when the exponent overflows."},
        "6071": {"synopsis":"The negated sum's top byte."},
        "6073": {"synopsis":"No rounding bits; on to NORMALISE.","comment":"No rounding bits; normalise"},
        "6076": {"synopsis":"Prepares the number at HL for multiplying or dividing: carry set when it is 0. Otherwise\nthe sign bit is XORed into A (so A's bit 7 builds the result's sign) and the leading 1 put\nback in its place; carry clear.","comment":"Carry set for the number 0"},
        "6081": {"comment":"A bit 7 XOR the sign"},
        "6082": {"comment":"The leading 1 back"},
        "6086": {"synopsis":"multiply ($04): first (HL) x second (DE); the result replaces the first.\n1. Both prepared (PREP_MULT): a 0 makes the result 0; A's bit 7 becomes the product's sign,\n   which FETCH_TWO leaves in the first number's second byte for STORE_RESULT.\n2. 33 rounds of shift and add (MULT_LOOP): the first mantissa, in B'C' C A, is shifted\n   right one bit at a time; for each 1 bit out, the second (D'E' D E) is added into H'L' H L,\n   which shifts right too, its low bits into the first's place. The 33rd round gives a\n   rounding bit.\n3. The exponent: the two exponents added, less the bias, by the flag arithmetic at $1809\n   (the S flag there is DEC A's: RLA and RRA leave it alone). Too large: report 6. Too small:\n   NORMALISE is entered with carry set, which makes the result 0.\n4. NORMALISE and STORE_RESULT, with A = the next product bits for the rounding.\nRuns: 3*4 = 12, -2*3 = -6, -2*-3 = 6, .5*.5 = 0.25, 0*5 = 0, 5*0 = 0, 1.5*1.5 = 2.25,\n123456*1000 = 123456000, 1E19*1E19 = 1E+38, 1E-20*1E-20 = 0, 1E20*1E20 report 6.","comment":"A = 0: the sign to build"},
        "6087": {"comment":"The first 0: so is the result"},
        "6091": {"comment":"Keep the code pointer and the second's address"},
        "6096": {"comment":"Prepare the second"},
        "6100": {"comment":"The second 0: the result is 0"},
        "6103": {"comment":"Both into registers; the sign into the first's second byte"},
        "6106": {"comment":"A = the first's lowest byte"},
        "6108": {"comment":"H'L' H L = 0, the product so far"},
        "6111": {"comment":"Keep H', L': the two exponents"},
        "6115": {"comment":"33 rounds"},
        "6119": {"synopsis":"One bit of the multiply: when the first number's bit out was 1, the second is added to the\nproduct.","comment":"The bit out was 1: add the second"},
        "6126": {"synopsis":"The product one bit right.","comment":"The product one bit right ..."},
        "6136": {"synopsis":"The first number one bit right, its low bit into carry; 33 rounds. Then the product's top\n32 bits, and the exponents.","comment":"... and the first, its low bit into carry"},
        "6147": {"comment":"D'E' D E = the product's top 32 bits"},
        "6151": {"comment":"B, C = the two exponents"},
        "6153": {"comment":"Added, the carry kept"},
        "6158": {"synopsis":"The bias taken off the exponents' sum, through the carry; on into EXP_TAIL.","comment":"The bias taken off, through the carry"},
        "6160": {"synopsis":"The exponent tail of multiply and division: from A (the two exponents added, or the\nsecond's taken from the first's) and the carry, sets the result's exponent with the bias\nright, report 6 when too large, and normalises (NORMALISE, with carry set when too small)."},
        "6163": {"comment":"In range?"},
        "6166": {"comment":"Too large: report 6"},
        "6169": {"synopsis":"A + 1 is the result's exponent; when it wraps to 0 with no carry, a mantissa with its top bit\nset is too large (report 6)."},
        "6178": {"comment":"Too large: report 6"},
        "6180": {"synopsis":"Stores the result's exponent; A = the bits past the mantissa, for rounding; on into\nNORMALISE.","comment":"The result's exponent"},
        "6182": {"comment":"A = the next product bits, for rounding"},
        "6184": {"synopsis":"Normalises the 32-bit mantissa in D'E' D E for the number at HL (its exponent there) and\nstores it (STORE_RESULT). It is shifted left until its top bit is 1, the exponent going\ndown each time. If the exponent runs down to 0 first, the result is 0, or the smallest\nnumber (exponent 1) when the top bit has just become 1; no 1 bit at all also gives 0.\nThen it is rounded up on the top bit of A, the bits shifted past the mantissa; a carry out\nof the top makes it $80000000 with the exponent up one (report 6 past $FF).\nEntered with carry set, the exponent-0 test is made at once, on the exponent at HL.","comment":"Carry clear: normalise"},
        "6186": {"comment":"Carry set on entry: is the exponent 0?"},
        "6188": {"synopsis":"The exponent ran down to 0 (or is 0 on entry): A = $80 for the test at NORM_CLEAR."},
        "6192": {"synopsis":"A = 0: the result is 0."},
        "6193": {"synopsis":"The registers cleared but D' = A AND D' ($80 or 0): exponent 1, the smallest number, when\nthe top bit was 1; else 0."},
        "6195": {"comment":"The registers 0 but D' = A ($80 or 0)"},
        "6199": {"comment":"Exponent 0, or 1 (with D' = $80: the smallest number)"},
        "6207": {"synopsis":"Up to 32 shifts left.","comment":"Up to 32 bits left"},
        "6209": {"synopsis":"Shifts left until the top bit is 1, the exponent down each time; 0 goes to NORM_ZERO."},
        "6210": {"comment":"Top bit 1: normalised"},
        "6215": {"comment":"One bit left, through A, E, D, E', D'"},
        "6226": {"comment":"The exponent down; 0: the result is 0"},
        "6231": {"comment":"No 1 bit at all: 0"},
        "6233": {"synopsis":"Rounds up on the bit past the mantissa; a wrap gives $80000000 with the exponent up (report 6\npast $FF); then STORE_RESULT.","comment":"The bit past the mantissa: round up?"},
        "6236": {"comment":"Round up"},
        "6242": {"comment":"It wrapped: $80000000, the exponent up"},
        "6246": {"comment":"Past $FF: report 6"},
        "6248": {"synopsis":"Writes the mantissa D'E' D E into the number at HL, with the sign from bit 7 of its\nsecond byte in place of the leading 1. Then DE = the second number's address, the new\nSTKEND, and HL' = the code pointer saved on entry.","comment":"BC = D'E', the high 16 bits"},
        "6254": {"comment":"The leading 1 out, the sign in"},
        "6267": {"comment":"DE = the second number: the new STKEND"},
        "6269": {"comment":"HL' = the code pointer again"},
        "6272": {"synopsis":"Stops with report 6: a result too large for a number (exponent past $FF)."},
        "6274": {"synopsis":"division ($05): first (HL) / second (DE); the result replaces the first.\n1. Both prepared (PREP_MULT): dividing by 0 is report 6; 0 divided by anything is 0. The\n   sign of the quotient is built in A and left for STORE_RESULT, as multiply does.\n2. Restoring long division (DIV_LOOP): the dividend in H'L' H L, the divisor in D'E' D E.\n   For each quotient bit the divisor is subtracted: no borrow gives a 1; a borrow gives a 0\n   and the divisor is added back. A bit carried out of the dividend's top means it must go\n   (DIV_BIG). The bits shift into B'C' C A; B counts up from $DF, and the last two bits are\n   kept in B' for the rounding.\n3. The exponent: the first's minus the second's, then multiply's own tail (EXP_TAIL) puts\n   the bias back, checks the range and normalises.\nRuns: 12/4 = 3, 1/3 = 0.33333333, 2/3 = 0.66666667, -6/3 = -2, -6/-3 = 2, 7/7 = 1, 0/5 = 0,\n1E-30/1E30 = 0; 1/0, 1E30/1E-30 and 1E38/.5 report 6.","comment":"The divisor first"},
        "6276": {"comment":"Dividing by 0: report 6"},
        "6282": {"comment":"The dividend 0: so is the quotient"},
        "6286": {"comment":"Keep the code pointer and both addresses"},
        "6291": {"comment":"Both into registers; the sign into the first's second byte"},
        "6295": {"comment":"Keep H', L': the two exponents"},
        "6296": {"comment":"The dividend: H'L' = its high 16 bits ..."},
        "6299": {"comment":"... H L its low 16"},
        "6302": {"comment":"B counts up from $DF: 33 bits, and 2 for rounding"},
        "6306": {"synopsis":"One quotient bit: shifted in, and the dividend doubled; a bit out of its top means the divisor\nsurely goes (DIV_BIG).","comment":"The quotient bit in: through A, C, C', B'"},
        "6315": {"comment":"The dividend one bit left"},
        "6320": {"comment":"A bit out of its top: the divisor goes for sure"},
        "6322": {"synopsis":"Subtracts the divisor: it goes (a 1), or it is added back (a 0).","comment":"Subtract the divisor"},
        "6328": {"comment":"It went: a 1"},
        "6330": {"comment":"It did not: add it back, a 0"},
        "6338": {"synopsis":"The divisor subtracted with no test."},
        "6345": {"synopsis":"A 1 bit.","comment":"A 1"},
        "6346": {"synopsis":"The next bit while B is negative; then two more for the rounding, and D'E' D E = the quotient.","comment":"The next bit, while B is negative"},
        "6350": {"comment":"Past it: the two rounding bits, one more round for the second"},
        "6353": {"comment":"D'E' D E = the quotient"},
        "6358": {"comment":"The two extra bits into B', for the rounding"},
        "6365": {"comment":"B, C = the two exponents"},
        "6367": {"comment":"A = the first's minus the second's"},
        "6369": {"comment":"On as multiply does"},
        "6372": {"synopsis":"truncate ($36): clears the fraction bits of the number at HL, so it rounds towards 0.\nBelow 1 in size (exponent under $81) it becomes 0; from exponent $A0 up there are none;\notherwise $A0 - exponent bits are cleared from its end: whole bytes, then the low bits of\nthe next one.","comment":"A = the exponent"},
        "6373": {"comment":"Size 1 or more?"},
        "6377": {"comment":"Below 1: exponent 0 ..."},
        "6379": {"comment":"... and all 32 mantissa bits cleared"},
        "6383": {"synopsis":"A = $A0 - the exponent, the fraction bits to clear; none for $A0 and over.","comment":"Exponent $A0 or more: no fraction bits"},
        "6386": {"comment":"A = $A0 - exponent: the bits to clear"},
        "6388": {"synopsis":"Clears the fraction bits from the number's end: whole bytes, then the rest with a mask."},
        "6389": {"comment":"HL = the number's last byte"},
        "6392": {"comment":"B = the whole bytes"},
        "6400": {"synopsis":"Clears B whole bytes, downwards.","comment":"Cleared"},
        "6405": {"synopsis":"The bits left over (A AND 7): a mask with that many 0s at the bottom.","comment":"The bits left over"},
        "6410": {"comment":"A mask of that many 0s at the bottom"},
        "6412": {"synopsis":"Builds the mask (SLA, B times), then ANDs it into the next byte."},
        "6416": {"comment":"Cleared from the next byte"},
        "6418": {"synopsis":"HL and DE back."},
        "6421": {"synopsis":"The calculator's constants, in the compact form STK_DATA unpacks (stk-const reads them):\n00 B0 00 = 0, 31 00 = 1, 30 00 = 0.5, F1 49 0F DA A2 = PI/2, 34 20 = 10."},
        "6435": {"synopsis":"The calculator's operation table: one address for each code $00-$38 (read as $1923 + 2 x\ncode), then one for each group of codes with a parameter: $1995 series ($80-$9F), $1997\nstk-const ($A0-$BF), $1999 st-mem ($C0-$DF), $199B get-mem ($E0-$FF). The 12 comparisons\nshare one routine (CALC_COMPARE). The names are those Klive's calculator decoder shows."},
        "6557": {"synopsis":"The calculator. Runs the codes that follow the RST $28 one by one, on the calculator\nstack (STKBOT to STKEND, 5 bytes a number), until end-calc ($34) returns to the code after\nthem. B on entry is kept in BERG for fp-calc-2, series and dec-jr-nz.\nA number is 5 bytes: an exponent with bias $80 (0: the number is 0), then 4 bytes of\nmantissa whose top bit, always 1, is not stored: bit 7 of the first holds the sign. 1 is\n$81 00 00 00 00, 3 is $82 40 00 00 00.\nEach code's routine is found in the table at $1923 and called with HL = its operand\n(codes $00-$17, which take two: HL = the first, DE = the second, and STKEND drops by 5),\nDE = STKEND, B = BERG; HL' points at the next code. It returns with DE = the new STKEND.","comment":"HL = the top number, DE = STKEND"},
        "6560": {"synopsis":"Starts a calculator run whose codes follow the CALL to here, with BERG = B, keeping HL and\nDE as they are (CALCULATE without STK_TOP). series runs its own codes this way."},
        "6561": {"comment":"BERG = B"},
        "6564": {"synopsis":"Resumes a calculator run whose codes follow the CALL to here, BERG left as it is."},
        "6565": {"comment":"HL' = the codes after the RST"},
        "6567": {"synopsis":"The calculator's loop: STKEND = DE, then the next code from HL'.","comment":"STKEND = DE"},
        "6572": {"comment":"A = the next code"},
        "6574": {"synopsis":"Runs code A, keeping the code pointer on the stack; entered from $19E9 with A = BERG, a\ncode given in a register instead of read from the list.","comment":"Keep the code pointer"},
        "6576": {"comment":"$00-$7F: a code of its own"},
        "6580": {"comment":"$80-$FF: one of four groups (bits 6, 5) ..."},
        "6586": {"comment":"... at $1995 + 2 x group"},
        "6590": {"comment":"A = the parameter, the low 5 bits"},
        "6594": {"synopsis":"Codes $00-$17 take two numbers: HL = the first, DE = the second; then the routine from the\ntable.","comment":"$00-$17 take two numbers"},
        "6599": {"comment":"DE = the top one, HL = the one below"},
        "6606": {"synopsis":"L = 2 x the code: its place in the table.","comment":"2 x code"},
        "6608": {"synopsis":"DE = the code's routine from the table at $1923; it runs with CALC_LOOP as its return, B =\nBERG.","comment":"DE = the routine, from the table"},
        "6617": {"comment":"It returns to CALC_LOOP"},
        "6623": {"comment":"B = BERG"},
        "6627": {"synopsis":"delete ($02): nothing to do; as a two-number code, STKEND already drops to the top\nnumber's start."},
        "6628": {"synopsis":"fp-calc-2 ($37): runs the code in BERG (B on entry to RST $28) as if it were the next one.\nHow the expression evaluator does a waiting operation."},
        "6635": {"synopsis":"BC = 5, and checks that 5 more bytes fit (TEST_ROOM). Keeps DE and HL."},
        "6646": {"synopsis":"Copies a 5-byte value from HL to DE, after checking for room (TEST_5_ROOM).\nExit: HL and DE past it."},
        "6652": {"synopsis":"stk-data ($30): puts the number in compact form that follows the code on the stack.\nThe first byte's bits 7-6 + 1 give how many mantissa bytes follow (1 to 4), its bits 5-0\nthe exponent minus $50 (0: the exponent is the next byte). The mantissa bytes not given are 0."},
        "6654": {"synopsis":"Unpacks the compact number at HL' to DE (STK_DATA's work, also stk-const's)."},
        "6667": {"comment":"C = how many mantissa bytes (1-4)"},
        "6672": {"comment":"Exponent bits 0: the exponent is the next byte"},
        "6676": {"synopsis":"The exponent byte, + $50.","comment":"Exponent + $50"},
        "6679": {"comment":"A = how many zero bytes after the mantissa"},
        "6686": {"comment":"The mantissa bytes given"},
        "6695": {"synopsis":"Zero bytes after the mantissa, B - 1 of them."},
        "6697": {"comment":"The rest zero"},
        "6701": {"synopsis":"Steps HL' over A compact numbers, unpacking each to address 0, a ROM write that is lost."},
        "6702": {"synopsis":"SKIP_CONSTS's loop: returns when A reaches 0."},
        "6716": {"synopsis":"HL = HL + 5 x A: the address of memory A from MEM."},
        "6725": {"synopsis":"get-mem ($E0 + n): copies memory n (MEM + 5n) onto the stack."},
        "6737": {"synopsis":"stk-const ($A0 + n): puts constant n from $1915 on the stack: stk-zero, stk-one, stk-half,\nstk-pi/2, stk-ten."},
        "6755": {"synopsis":"st-mem ($C0 + n): copies the top number into memory n (MEM + 5n); it stays on the stack."},
        "6770": {"synopsis":"exchange ($01): swaps the top two numbers, byte by byte."},
        "6772": {"synopsis":"exchange's loop: swaps one byte of each number."},
        "6783": {"synopsis":"series ($80 + n): sums a series of n constants for the number x on top, the way a\nChebyshev series is summed: B(k) = 2x B(k+1) - B(k+2) + A(k), for the constants A in\nturn, and the result B - M1 (the B two steps back) at the end.\nIt is a loop that runs partly as calculator codes and partly as Z80. The codes after\nCALL $19A0 (at $1A83) set up M0 = 2x and M2 = 0 and do the first half of each step; their\nend-calc returns to SERIES_LOOP, which fetches the next constant from the caller's code\nstream (STK_DATA reads HL', which points after the series code there), and CALL $19A4\nruns the codes at $1A96 for the second half; their dec-jr-nz jumps back into the first block\nwhile BERG counts the constants down.\nUsed by exp (8 constants), ln (12), sin and cos (6) and atn (12). Runs: EXP 1 = 2.7182818,\nSIN 1 = 0.84147099, COS 0 = 1, LN 2 = 0.69314718, ATN 1 = 0.78539816, SQR 2 = 1.4142136.","comment":"B = n, the number of constants"},
        "6784": {"comment":"The codes after this CALL run, BERG = n"},
        "6787": {"synopsis":"series' first calculator codes, run by the CALL $19A0 before them: duplicate, addition,\nst-mem-0 (M0 = 2x), delete, stk-zero, st-mem-2 (M2 = 0); then, from $1A89, the loop's first\nhalf: duplicate, get-mem-0, multiply (2x B), get-mem-2, st-mem-1 (M1 = M2), subtract\n(2x B - M2), end-calc, so the Z80 goes on at SERIES_LOOP."},
        "6800": {"synopsis":"One round of the series: the next constant from the caller's codes (CALC_STK_DATA), then\nthe codes at $1A96.","comment":"The next constant, from the caller's codes"},
        "6803": {"comment":"The codes after this CALL run"},
        "6806": {"synopsis":"series' second calculator codes, run by the CALL $19A4 before them: addition (+ the\nconstant), exchange, st-mem-2 (M2 = the B before), delete, dec-jr-nz back to $1A89 while\nBERG counts down; then get-mem-1, subtract (B - M1), end-calc, and the RET at $1A9F."},
        "6815": {"comment":"Back to the caller's codes, after the constants"},
        "6816": {"synopsis":"neg ($18): flips the sign bit; 0 stays 0."},
        "6826": {"synopsis":"abs ($27): clears the sign bit."},
        "6831": {"synopsis":"sgn ($26): 0 stays 0; otherwise 1 with the number's sign."},
        "6846": {"synopsis":"peek ($28): the byte at the address on the stack (FIND_INT, STACK_A)."},
        "6853": {"synopsis":"usr-no ($29): calls the machine code at the address on the stack; BC on its return becomes\nthe result (STACK_BC is pushed as its return address)."},
        "6862": {"synopsis":"greater-0 ($33): 1 when the number is above 0, else 0 (FP_0_OR_1)."},
        "6869": {"synopsis":"not ($2C): 1 when the number is 0, else 0."},
        "6875": {"synopsis":"less-0 ($32): 1 when the number is below 0, else 0."},
        "6876": {"synopsis":"Carry = the sign bit XOR A, then FP_0_OR_1: less-0 (A = 0) gives 1 for a negative number,\ngreater-0 (A = $FF) for a positive one."},
        "6880": {"synopsis":"Replaces the number at HL with 1 when carry is set, 0 when not."},
        "6883": {"synopsis":"Clears the 5 bytes, one at a time."},
        "6893": {"synopsis":"or ($07): X OR Y is 1 when Y is not 0, otherwise X.","comment":"Y 0: the result is X"},
        "6896": {"comment":"Otherwise 1"},
        "6899": {"synopsis":"no-&-no ($08): X AND Y is X when Y is not 0, otherwise 0.","comment":"Y not 0: the result is X"},
        "6902": {"comment":"Otherwise 0"},
        "6904": {"synopsis":"str-&-no ($10): A$ AND Y is A$ when Y is not 0, otherwise the empty string (its length set\nto 0).","comment":"Y not 0: the result is A$"},
        "6908": {"comment":"Otherwise A$'s length = 0"},
        "6915": {"synopsis":"The 12 comparisons, one routine: no-l-eql, no-gr-eql, nos-neql, no-grtr, no-less, nos-eql\n($09-$0E, numbers: <=, >=, <>, >, <, =) and the same for strings ($11-$16). The code comes in B\n(from BERG); its bits, after the arithmetic at $1B04, say:\n- swap the two operands (>= and <): so >= is tested as <=, < as >;\n- numbers or strings;\n- the base test: 'equal' for = and <>, 'greater' for the others;\n- negate the result: for <=, >= (not greater) and <> (not equal).\nNumbers are subtracted and the difference tested. Strings are compared character by character\n(their codes); a string that ends first, where the other goes on, is the smaller.\nThe result is 1 or 0.\nRuns: 1<2 = 1, 2<1 = 0, 1=1 = 1, 1=2 = 0, 3>2 = 1, \"A\"<\"B\" = 1, \"AB\">\"A\" = 1, \"A\"=\"A\" = 1,\n\"\"<\"A\" = 1, \"B\"<\"AB\" = 0, \"AB\"=\"ABC\" = 0.","comment":"A = the code - 8: 1-6, or 9-14 for strings"},
        "6918": {"comment":"<=, >=, <> one lower, so the bits line up"},
        "6923": {"synopsis":"Bit 0 of the comparison's code: the operands are swapped first (exchange).","comment":"Bit 0 into carry: swap the operands?"},
        "6928": {"comment":"Swap them (exchange)"},
        "6934": {"synopsis":"Strings (bit 2) or numbers; numbers by their difference (CALC_SUBTRACT).","comment":"Strings?"},
        "6938": {"comment":"Numbers: carry = 'equal' is the base test"},
        "6940": {"comment":"The difference, first - second"},
        "6945": {"synopsis":"Strings: both fetched, then compared character by character.","comment":"Strings: carry = 'equal' is the base test"},
        "6947": {"comment":"The second string: DE, BC"},
        "6952": {"comment":"The first: DE, BC"},
        "6956": {"synopsis":"The second string ended? Then equal or greater by the first; else the characters are compared.","comment":"The second's length 0?"},
        "6960": {"comment":"No: compare characters"},
        "6962": {"comment":"The second ended; the first too?"},
        "6963": {"synopsis":"The first goes on (or its character is larger): greater."},
        "6966": {"comment":"The first goes on: greater, not equal"},
        "6970": {"synopsis":"Both ended: equal.","comment":"Both ended: equal, not greater"},
        "6973": {"synopsis":"The first ended: less; else the characters' difference decides, or the next pair.","comment":"The first ended: less"},
        "6976": {"comment":"First character - second"},
        "6978": {"comment":"Smaller: less"},
        "6980": {"comment":"Larger: greater"},
        "6982": {"comment":"The same: on to the next pair"},
        "6989": {"synopsis":"Less: neither equal nor greater.","comment":"Less: neither equal nor greater"},
        "6992": {"synopsis":"A 0 on the stack, the answer in carry.","comment":"A 0 on the stack, the answer in carry"},
        "6996": {"synopsis":"The answer as 1 or 0: NOT for an 'equal' test, greater-0, then NOT for <=, >= and <>."},
        "6998": {"comment":"'Equal' test: NOT the difference, 1 when it was 0"},
        "7001": {"comment":"Above 0? 1 or 0"},
        "7005": {"comment":"Negate for <=, >=, <>"},
        "7010": {"synopsis":"strs-add ($17): joins two strings, A$ + B$. Room for both is made at the end of the\nworkspace (RST $30), the new string's details go on the calculator stack, and both are\ncopied in, A$ first. Ends in STK_TOP.\nRun: \"AB\"+\"CD\" = \"ABCD\".","comment":"B$: DE, BC"},
        "7015": {"comment":"A$: DE, BC"},
        "7022": {"comment":"BC = the two lengths together"},
        "7025": {"comment":"Room for the joined string"},
        "7026": {"comment":"Its details onto the stack"},
        "7035": {"comment":"A$ copied in"},
        "7037": {"synopsis":"B$ copied in after A$; then STK_TOP."},
        "7043": {"comment":"B$ after it"},
        "7045": {"synopsis":"HL = STKEND - 5 (the top number), DE = STKEND. Also the end of strs-add and val."},
        "7055": {"synopsis":"chrs ($2B), CHR$: a one-character string of the code on the stack, in 1 byte of\nworkspace. Over 255 or negative is report B.\nRuns: CHR$ 38 = \"A\"; CHR$ 300 report B.","comment":"A = the code"},
        "7058": {"comment":"Over 255 or negative: report B"},
        "7066": {"comment":"1 byte of room"},
        "7068": {"comment":"The character"},
        "7069": {"comment":"The string's details onto the stack"},
        "7074": {"synopsis":"Report B: a code over 255 or negative."},
        "7076": {"synopsis":"val ($1A), VAL: evaluates the string as a numeric expression. It is copied to the\nworkspace with a NEWLINE after it, and CH_ADD pointed there. The copy is first checked\n(FLAGS bit 7 clear) as a number that must end the line, report C otherwise; the check also\ninserts the hidden form of each number in it. Then it is run (FLAGS bit 7 set) through\nSCAN_EXPR, and CH_ADD put back.\nRuns: VAL \"2*3+1\" = 7; VAL \"1+\" report C.","comment":"Keep CH_ADD"},
        "7080": {"comment":"The string: DE, BC"},
        "7084": {"comment":"Room for it and a NEWLINE"},
        "7087": {"comment":"CH_ADD = the copy"},
        "7092": {"comment":"Copy it"},
        "7096": {"comment":"A NEWLINE after it"},
        "7098": {"comment":"Check only ..."},
        "7102": {"comment":"... a number, and the line must end there"},
        "7109": {"comment":"CH_ADD = the copy again"},
        "7112": {"comment":"Run it this time"},
        "7120": {"comment":"CH_ADD as it was"},
        "7125": {"synopsis":"str$ ($2A), STR$: the number as PRINT shows it, as a string. 1 byte of workspace room\nholds a NEWLINE; DF_CC is pointed at it and S_POSN's column at $FF, and the number printed\n(PRINT_FP). PRINT_SCREEN, finding the NEWLINE there, inserts each character before it, as it\ngrows a collapsed display line, so the string grows in the workspace. DF_CC and S_POSN are\nput back.\nRun: STR$ 1.5 = \"1.5\".","comment":"1 byte of room"},
        "7129": {"comment":"A NEWLINE in it"},
        "7131": {"comment":"Keep S_POSN ..."},
        "7135": {"comment":"... and set its column count to $FF: no line ends"},
        "7140": {"comment":"Keep DF_CC ..."},
        "7144": {"comment":"... and print at the room"},
        "7149": {"comment":"Print the number into it"},
        "7153": {"comment":"BC = how far printing went: the length"},
        "7162": {"comment":"DF_CC as it was"},
        "7166": {"comment":"S_POSN as it was"},
        "7169": {"comment":"The string's details onto the stack"},
        "7174": {"synopsis":"code ($19), CODE: the code of the string's first character, 0 for the empty string.\nRuns: CODE \"A\" = 38; CODE \"\" = 0.","comment":"The string: DE, BC"},
        "7179": {"comment":"Empty: 0"},
        "7181": {"comment":"The first character"},
        "7182": {"synopsis":"Stacks A, the code or 0 (STACK_A)."},
        "7185": {"synopsis":"len ($1B), LEN: the string's length (STACK_BC).\nRun: LEN \"HELLO\" = 5."},
        "7191": {"synopsis":"dec-jr-nz ($31): BERG - 1; jumps (as jump) while it is not 0, else steps over the\ndisplacement."},
        "7203": {"synopsis":"jump ($2F): adds the signed displacement that follows to the code pointer (relative to\nthe displacement byte)."},
        "7204": {"synopsis":"Adds the displacement at HL', sign-extended (JUMP_ADD), to the code pointer."},
        "7211": {"synopsis":"D = the displacement's sign extension; HL + DE."},
        "7215": {"synopsis":"jump-true ($00): jumps (as jump) when the top number is not 0, and takes it off; otherwise\nsteps over the displacement."},
        "7223": {"synopsis":"n-mod-m ($2E): from n and m (m on top), leaves n - m x INT(n/m) and, on top, INT(n/m).\nA calculator program of its own: memory 0 holds m, then the quotient."},
        "7238": {"synopsis":"int ($24): the largest whole number not above the number. A positive one is truncated\n(truncate); a negative one goes on through the codes at $1C4E: one less than its truncated\nvalue unless it was whole.\nRuns: INT 2.7 = 2, INT -2.7 = -3, INT -3 = -3, INT .5 = 0, INT -.5 = -1, INT -1E-10 = -1."},
        "7246": {"synopsis":"int's codes for a negative number, reached by its jump-true: duplicate, truncate,\nst-mem-0, subtract (the fraction), get-mem-0, exchange, not, jump-true to the end-calc\n(a whole number stays as it is), stk-one, subtract (one less than the truncated value),\nend-calc. Then the RET at $1C5A."},
        "7258": {"synopsis":"The RET after int's codes for a negative number.","comment":"Back from int's codes for a negative number"},
        "7259": {"synopsis":"exp ($23): e to the power x, as 2 to the power x log2 e.\ny = x x 1.442695 (log2 e) is split into n = INT y (memory 3) and f = y - n, from 0 to 1.\n2^f comes from a series of 8 constants on 2f - 1, in -1 to 1. Then n is added to that\nresult's exponent byte: 2^f x 2^n. n over 255, or an exponent past $FF, is report 6; a\nnegative n that takes the exponent to 0 or below gives 0.\nRuns: EXP 0 = 1, EXP 1 = 2.7182818, EXP 10 = 22026.466, EXP -1 = 0.36787944, EXP .5 =\n1.6487213, EXP 88 = 1.6516362E+38, EXP -88 = 6.054602E-39, EXP -100 = 0; EXP 89 report 6.","comment":"y = x log2 e; n = INT y into memory 3, f = y - n; the series on 2f - 1\ngives 2^f; n on top"},
        "7311": {"comment":"A = the size of n; carry over 255, Z positive"},
        "7314": {"comment":"n negative"},
        "7316": {"comment":"Over 255: report 6"},
        "7318": {"comment":"2^f's exponent + n"},
        "7319": {"comment":"Not past $FF: that is the result"},
        "7321": {"synopsis":"Report 6: the result's exponent past $FF."},
        "7323": {"synopsis":"n negative: below -255, or an exponent reaching 0 or less, gives 0; else the exponent - |n|.","comment":"Below -255: the result is 0"},
        "7325": {"comment":"|n| - the exponent"},
        "7326": {"comment":"The exponent would reach 0 or below: 0"},
        "7328": {"comment":"The exponent - |n|"},
        "7330": {"synopsis":"The new exponent stored.","comment":"The new exponent"},
        "7332": {"synopsis":"Too small: the result is 0.","comment":"Too small: 0 instead"},
        "7337": {"synopsis":"ln ($22): the natural logarithm. 0 or less is report A.\nx is split into m x 2^n: m keeps x's mantissa under exponent $80, so it is 0.5 to 1, and n is\nx's exponent - 128 (LN_SPLIT). When m is 0.8 or less it is doubled and n lowered by one\n(LN_DOUBLE), so m is 0.8 to 1.6. Then ln x = n ln 2 + (m - 1) S, S a series of 12\nconstants on 2.5 (m - 1) - 0.5, which keeps it in -1 to 1.\nRuns: LN 1 = 0, LN 2 = 0.69314718, LN 10 = 2.3025851, LN .5 = -0.69314718, LN .9 =\n-0.10536052, LN 1.5 = 0.40546511, LN 1E38 = 87.498234, LN 1E-38 = -87.498234; LN 0 and\nLN -1 report A.","comment":"Above 0?"},
        "7343": {"comment":"No: report A"},
        "7345": {"synopsis":"ln's codes where its jump-true lands for x above 0: stk-zero, delete (which leave HL at x),\nend-calc; the Z80 goes on at LN_SPLIT."},
        "7348": {"synopsis":"Splits x into m (its mantissa under exponent $80, 0.5 to 1) and n = the exponent - 128;\nwhen m is 0.8 or less, n goes down one and m is doubled (LN_DOUBLE).","comment":"A = x's exponent"},
        "7349": {"comment":"m: the mantissa under exponent $80, 0.5 to 1"},
        "7351": {"comment":"The exponent on the stack; n = it - 128; m 0.8 or less?"},
        "7376": {"synopsis":"m doubled (its exponent + 1); then n ln 2 plus the series in m - 1.","comment":"m 0.8 or less: doubled (n lowered by one above)"},
        "7377": {"comment":"n ln 2; m - 1; the series on 2.5 (m - 1) - 0.5, times (m - 1), plus n ln 2"},
        "7448": {"synopsis":"get-argt ($35): reduces an angle x (radians) for sin and cos to w, from -1 to 1, with\nsin x = sin (w PI/2).\nv = x / 2PI (x 0.159155), less the nearest whole number (INT (v + 1/2)), times 4: quarter\nturns, -2 to 2. When |v| is 1 or less, w = v; otherwise w = 2 - v (or -2 - v for a negative\nv), the reflection that keeps the sine (codes at $1D35). Memory 0 = 1 when it was reflected,\n0 when not: the cosine's sign then turns.","comment":"v = x / 2PI, less the nearest whole number, times 4"},
        "7476": {"comment":"|v| 1 or less: w = v"},
        "7477": {"synopsis":"get-argt's codes for |v| above 1, where its jump-true lands: stk-one, subtract (|v| - 2),\nexchange, less-0, jump-true to the end-calc when v is negative; otherwise neg (2 - |v|);\nend-calc. Then the RET at $1D3D."},
        "7485": {"comment":"Reflected: w = 2 - v, or -2 - v"},
        "7486": {"synopsis":"cos ($1D): the cosine, as the sine a quarter turn on: from get-argt's w, 1 - |w|, negated\nwhen get-argt reflected (memory 0), then the sine's code from $1D4B.\nsin ($1C) begins inside these codes, at $1D49: the bytes $EF $35 there are, entered as sin,\nan RST $28 and get-argt; cos's own codes jump over them. The listing can show only cos's\nreading, so sin has no label of its own. sin x = w S(2w^2 - 1), S a series of 6 constants.\nRuns: SIN 0 = 0, SIN 1 = 0.84147099, SIN -1 = -0.84147099, SIN 2 = 0.90929743, SIN 100 =\n-0.50636565, SIN PI = 0, SIN (PI/2) = 1; COS 0 = 1, COS 1 = 0.54030231, COS 2 =\n-0.41614684, COS PI = -1."},
        "7534": {"synopsis":"tan ($1E): SIN x / COS x.\nRuns: TAN 1 = 1.5574077, TAN (PI/4) = 1."},
        "7542": {"synopsis":"atn ($21): the arctangent. With y and a constant c, atn x = c + y S(2y^2 - 1), S a series\nof 12 constants.\nFor |x| 1 or more (exponent $81 up) the codes after the RST at $1D7B make y = -1/x and\nc = PI/2 with x's sign, and jump to $1D8B. For |x| below 1 the Z80 jumps to $1D89, inside\nthose codes: the bytes $EF $A0 there are, entered that way, an RST $28 and stk-zero (y = x,\nc = 0); the other path's jumps pass over them. The listing shows the codes' reading; the\nlabel ATN_SMALL marks the entry.\nRuns: ATN 0 = 0, ATN 1 = 0.78539816, ATN -1 = -0.78539816, ATN .5 = 0.46364761,\nATN 10 = 1.4711277, ATN -10 = -1.4711277.","comment":"A = the exponent"},
        "7543": {"comment":"Below 1 in size: on at $1D89, y = x, c = 0"},
        "7547": {"comment":"y = -1/x, c = +-PI/2; then the series on y"},
        "7561": {"synopsis":"atn's entry for |x| below 1, from the JR C at $1D79: entered by the Z80, $EF is an RST $28\nand $A0 stk-zero (y = x, c = 0); the calculator's own path from $1D7B jumps over these two\nbytes. The listing shows the calculator's reading (see CALC_ATN)."},
        "7620": {"synopsis":"asn ($1F): the arcsine, as 2 ATN (x / (1 + SQR (1 - x^2))). Over 1 in size is report A\n(sqr of a negative number).\nRuns: ASN 1 = 1.5707963, ASN .5 = 0.52359878, ASN -1 = -1.5707963; ASN 2 report A."},
        "7636": {"synopsis":"acs ($20): the arccosine, PI/2 - ASN x.\nRuns: ACS 0 = 1.5707963, ACS 1 = 0, ACS -1 = 3.1415927, ACS .5 = 1.0471976."},
        "7643": {"synopsis":"sqr ($25): the square root, as x ** 0.5: 0 gives 0 at once; otherwise 1/2 is put on the\nstack and to-power follows.\nRuns: SQR 4 = 2, SQR 2 = 1.4142136, SQR 0 = 0, SQR 1E38 = 9.9999999E+18 (through ln and\nexp); SQR -1 report A.","comment":"0: the result is 0; else x, 1/2"},
        "7650": {"synopsis":"to-power ($06): x ** y, as EXP (y LN x): ln, multiply, then exp. A negative x is report A\n(from ln). x = 0 is handled by the codes at $1DEE: 0 ** 0 = 1, 0 ** y = 0 for y above 0,\nreport 6 for y below 0.\nRuns: 2**10 = 1024, 9**.5 = 3, 2**-1 = 0.5, 0**0 = 1, 0**2 = 0; 0**-1 report 6;\n-2**2 = -4 (unary minus binds less tightly than **).","comment":"x 0? Then the codes at $1DEE; else y LN x"},
        "7659": {"comment":"EXP of it"},
        "7662": {"synopsis":"to-power's codes for x = 0, where its jump-true lands: delete, duplicate, not, jump-true\nto $1DFB when y is 0 (delete, stk-one: 0**0 = 1); otherwise stk-zero, exchange, greater-0,\njump-true to the end-calc when y is above 0 (0**y = 0); otherwise stk-one, exchange,\ndivision: 1/0, report 6. $1DFD is the end-calc that sqr's jump-true (x = 0) lands on too.\nThen the RET at $1DFE."},
        "7680": {"synopsis":"$00 - Character: ' '"},
        "7688": {"synopsis":"$01 - Character: mosaic"},
